window.LESSONS = [{"id":"ch01","short":"Ch 1","title":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","label":"Chapter 1","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Insurance moves risk from one person to a large group. Know the company types, how insurers are classified, who sells insurance (agent vs. broker), and the federal regulation timeline.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch01-s2","title":"1. What insurance is","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurance</strong> (Louisiana law) = a contract where one party agrees to indemnify another, or pay a set amount, when a defined event happens.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurer</strong> = anyone in the business of making insurance contracts, except a fraternal benefit society.</li><li class=\"l0\">A reciprocal, an inter-insurance exchange, and a Lloyds organization all count as insurers.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=506206\">La. R.S. 22:46</a></p>","text":"Insurance (Louisiana law) = a contract where one party agrees to indemnify another, or pay a set amount, when a defined event happens. Insurer = anyone in the business of making insurance contracts, except a fraternal benefit society. A reciprocal, an inter-insurance exchange, and a Lloyds organization all count as insurers."},{"id":"ch01-s3","title":"2. Stock vs. mutual companies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Owned by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Stock company</strong></td><td>Stockholders, who are not necessarily insureds. Stockholders pay in a capital fund.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mutual company</strong></td><td>Its insureds (policyholders). No stockholders.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>M</strong>utual = <strong>M</strong>embers own it. <strong>S</strong>tock = <strong>S</strong>hareholders own it.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/stock-company\">IRMI Glossary: stock company</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/mutual-company\">IRMI Glossary: mutual company</a></p>","text":"Type Owned by Stock company Stockholders, who are not necessarily insureds. Stockholders pay in a capital fund. Mutual company Its insureds (policyholders). No stockholders. Memory trick M utual = M embers own it. S tock = S hareholders own it."},{"id":"ch01-s4","title":"3. Insurer classification (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Formed under the laws of</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Domestic</strong></td><td>Louisiana</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Foreign</strong></td><td>Another U.S. state, a U.S. territory, or the District of Columbia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alien</strong></td><td>A country other than the United States</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Authorized insurer</strong> = holds a certificate of authority or license under the Louisiana Insurance Code.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Unauthorized insurer</strong> = has no certificate of authority to do business in Louisiana.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Where was it <strong>born</strong>? Here = domestic. Another state = foreign. Another country = alien.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=506206\">La. R.S. 22:46</a></p>","text":"Term Formed under the laws of Domestic Louisiana Foreign Another U.S. state, a U.S. territory, or the District of Columbia Alien A country other than the United States Authorized insurer = holds a certificate of authority or license under the Louisiana Insurance Code. Unauthorized insurer = has no certificate of authority to do business in Louisiana. Memory trick Where was it born ? Here = domestic. Another state = foreign. Another country = alien."},{"id":"ch01-s3","title":"4. Other key entities","html":"<h4>Other key entities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lloyd's of London:</strong> A marketplace, not an insurer. Specialist syndicates price and underwrite the risks. The Lloyd's Corporation is not itself an insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Risk retention group (RRG):</strong> Owned by its members, who have similar liability exposures. Chartered or licensed as a liability insurer in one state. Writes liability coverage only.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purchasing group:</strong> Buys liability coverage together for its members, who have similar or related liability exposures. Must be domiciled in a state.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.lloyds.com/about-lloyds/what-is-lloyds\">Lloyd's</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/3901\">15 U.S.C. 3901 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Other key entities Lloyd's of London: A marketplace, not an insurer. Specialist syndicates price and underwrite the risks. The Lloyd's Corporation is not itself an insurer. Risk retention group (RRG): Owned by its members, who have similar liability exposures. Chartered or licensed as a liability insurer in one state. Writes liability coverage only. Purchasing group: Buys liability coverage together for its members, who have similar or related liability exposures. Must be domiciled in a state."},{"id":"ch01-s7","title":"5. Federal regulation timeline (memorize dates)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1868</td><td>Paul v. Virginia</td><td>The Court held that issuing a policy of insurance is not a transaction of commerce; states regulate it</td></tr><tr><td>1944</td><td>U.S. v. South-Eastern Underwriters Association</td><td>The Court held insurance can be interstate commerce, so the Sherman Act reaches it</td></tr><tr><td>1945</td><td>McCarran-Ferguson Act (Public Law 15)</td><td>States keep regulating insurance; the Sherman, Clayton, and FTC Acts apply only to the extent the business is not regulated by state law</td></tr><tr><td>1958</td><td>FTC v. National Casualty Co.</td><td>FTC cannot regulate insurer advertising in states that regulate it</td></tr><tr><td>1959</td><td>SEC v. Variable Annuity Life Insurance Co.</td><td>Variable annuities were not exempt as insurance; federal securities law applies</td></tr><tr><td>1994</td><td>18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034</td><td>Federal crimes and civil penalties for dishonest conduct in the insurance business</td></tr><tr><td>1999</td><td>Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA)</td><td>Financial institutions, including insurers, must tell customers their information-sharing practices and opt-out rights</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>CAN-SPAM Act</td><td>Honor opt-out requests within 10 business days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1994 (18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034):</strong></p><p class=\"cbullet\">&bull; <strong>Prison under 1033:</strong> Generally up to 10 years. Up to 15 years if the conduct jeopardized an insurer's safety and soundness and was a significant cause of its conservation, rehabilitation, or liquidation.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">&bull; <strong>Barred felons (1033(e)):</strong> Up to 5 years for willfully working in insurance, or willfully permitting it.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">&bull; <strong>Written consent exception:</strong> A barred person may participate only with the written consent of an insurance regulatory official that specifically refers to subsection (e).</p><p class=\"cbullet\">&bull; <strong>The &quot;$50,000&quot; figure is a CIVIL penalty under 1034:</strong> up to $50,000 per violation OR the compensation received or offered, whichever is greater.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">&bull; <strong>Threats:</strong> Threats or force to corruptly influence proceedings are covered by 1033(d).</p></div><h4>Industry oversight</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NAIC (National Association of Insurance Commissioners):</strong> Governed by state insurance regulators from the 50 states, D.C., and five U.S. territories.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> R.S. 22:1964 lists the unfair and deceptive acts, including unfair claims settlement practices in paragraph (14).</p></div><h4>State guaranty association</h4><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Under R.S. 22:2083(C), per life, the association pays up to: life death benefits <strong>$300,000</strong>; life cash surrender and withdrawal values <strong>$100,000</strong>; health benefits <strong>$500,000</strong>; annuity present value <strong>$250,000</strong>. Under subsection D, an aggregate cap of <strong>$500,000</strong> per individual applies.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/75/168\">Paul v. Virginia (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/322/533\">U.S. v. SEUA (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1012\">15 U.S.C. 1012 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/357/560\">FTC v. National Casualty (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/359/65\">SEC v. VALIC (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1033\">18 U.S.C. 1033 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1034\">18 U.S.C. 1034 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/gramm-leach-bliley-act\">FTC GLBA</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business\">FTC CAN-SPAM guide</a>; <a href=\"https://content.naic.org/about\">NAIC About</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509078\">La. R.S. 22:1964</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=509204\">La. R.S. 22:2083</a></p>","text":"Year Event What to know 1868 Paul v. Virginia The Court held that issuing a policy of insurance is not a transaction of commerce; states regulate it 1944 U.S. v. South-Eastern Underwriters Association The Court held insurance can be interstate commerce, so the Sherman Act reaches it 1945 McCarran-Ferguson Act (Public Law 15) States keep regulating insurance; the Sherman, Clayton, and FTC Acts apply only to the extent the business is not regulated by state law 1958 FTC v. National Casualty Co. FTC cannot regulate insurer advertising in states that regulate it 1959 SEC v. Variable Annuity Life Insurance Co. Variable annuities were not exempt as insurance; federal securities law applies 1994 18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034 Federal crimes and civil penalties for dishonest conduct in the insurance business 1999 Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) Financial institutions, including insurers, must tell customers their information-sharing practices and opt-out rights - CAN-SPAM Act Honor opt-out requests within 10 business days 1994 (18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034): &bull; Prison under 1033: Generally up to 10 years. Up to 15 years if the conduct jeopardized an insurer's safety and soundness and was a significant cause of its conservation, rehabilitation, or liquidation. &bull; Barred felons (1033(e)): Up to 5 years for willfully working in insurance, or willfully permitting it. &bull; Written consent exception: A barred person may participate only with the written consent of an insurance regulatory official that specifically refers to subsection (e). &bull; The \"$50,000\" figure is a CIVIL penalty under 1034: up to $50,000 per violation OR the compensation received or offered, whichever is greater. &bull; Threats: Threats or force to corruptly influence proceedings are covered by 1033(d). Industry oversight NAIC (National Association of Insurance Commissioners): Governed by state insurance regulators from the 50 states, D.C., and five U.S. territories. Louisiana: R.S. 22:1964 lists the unfair and deceptive acts, including unfair claims settlement practices in paragraph (14). State guaranty association Louisiana: Under R.S. 22:2083(C), per life, the association pays up to: life death benefits $300,000 ; life cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000 ; health benefits $500,000 ; annuity present value $250,000 . Under subsection D, an aggregate cap of $500,000 per individual applies."}]},{"id":"ch02","short":"Ch 2","title":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","label":"Chapter 2","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Four words that get mixed up on every exam: <strong>RISK</strong> (uncertainty), <strong>PERIL</strong> (cause of loss), <strong>HAZARD</strong> (condition that makes a peril more likely), <strong>LOSS</strong> (the damage). Learn these four, then the three types of hazard.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch02-s1","title":"1. Risk","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Risk</strong> = uncertainty about whether a given event will happen.</li><li class=\"l0\">Risk can also mean the insured person or the property a policy covers.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/risk\">IRMI Glossary: risk</a></p>","text":"Risk = uncertainty about whether a given event will happen. Risk can also mean the insured person or the property a policy covers."},{"id":"ch02-s2","title":"2. Peril, hazard, loss","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Peril</strong> = a cause of loss (for example, fire, windstorm, collision).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Hazard</strong> = a condition that increases the probability of loss (for example, poor housekeeping in a factory).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Loss</strong> = the basis of a claim for damages under the policy terms, or a loss of assets from a pure risk.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/peril\">IRMI Glossary: peril</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/hazard\">IRMI Glossary: hazard</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/loss\">IRMI Glossary: loss</a></p>","text":"Peril = a cause of loss (for example, fire, windstorm, collision). Hazard = a condition that increases the probability of loss (for example, poor housekeeping in a factory). Loss = the basis of a claim for damages under the policy terms, or a loss of assets from a pure risk."},{"id":"ch02-s3","title":"3. Pure risk vs. speculative risk","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Pure risk</strong> = opportunity for loss only, no chance of gain.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Speculative risk</strong> = could produce either a profit or a loss (for example, a business venture or a gamble).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/pure-risk\">IRMI Glossary: pure risk</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/speculative-risk\">IRMI Glossary: speculative risk</a></p>","text":"Pure risk = opportunity for loss only, no chance of gain. Speculative risk = could produce either a profit or a loss (for example, a business venture or a gamble)."},{"id":"ch02-s4","title":"4. Risk retention","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Risk retention</strong> = the planned acceptance of losses through deductibles, deliberate noninsurance, and loss-sensitive plans.</li><li class=\"l0\">Some risk is kept, not transferred to an insurer.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/risk-retention\">IRMI Glossary: risk retention</a></p>","text":"Risk retention = the planned acceptance of losses through deductibles, deliberate noninsurance, and loss-sensitive plans. Some risk is kept, not transferred to an insurer."}]},{"id":"ch03","short":"Ch 3","title":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","label":"Chapter 3","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> An insurance policy is a contract. It needs 4 essential elements, it has special features, it requires insurable interest, and it runs on good faith, agency law, and a few legal doctrines (waiver, estoppel, subrogation). Most of the traps are in the agent-vs-broker rules and the insurable-interest timing rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch03-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>A policy on another person's life needs insurable interest</strong> (or benefits payable to the insured or the insured's estate).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life = valued contract</strong> (pays a fixed amount regardless of the loss). <strong>Indemnity</strong> = payment that makes the party whole.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/valued-policy\">IRMI: Valued policy</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/indemnity\">IRMI: Indemnity</a></p>","text":"A policy on another person's life needs insurable interest (or benefits payable to the insured or the insured's estate). Life = valued contract (pays a fixed amount regardless of the loss). Indemnity = payment that makes the party whole."},{"id":"ch03-s2","title":"1. Insurance contract basics","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>An insurance policy is a contract.</strong> A contract needs mutual assent (offer and acceptance), consideration, capacity, and legality.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurance policies are <strong>contracts of adhesion</strong> (see below).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/contract\">Cornell LII: Contract</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/contract-of-adhesion\">IRMI: Contract of adhesion</a></p>","text":"An insurance policy is a contract. A contract needs mutual assent (offer and acceptance), consideration, capacity, and legality. Insurance policies are contracts of adhesion (see below)."},{"id":"ch03-s3","title":"2. The 4 essential elements (CRITICAL)","html":"<p>The basic elements of an enforceable contract:</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Offer and acceptance</strong></td><td>Mutual assent: one party offers, the other accepts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consideration</strong></td><td>Something of value is exchanged</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Legal purpose</strong></td><td>The agreement serves a lawful purpose</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Competent parties</strong></td><td>Parties are legally able to contract (for example, minimum age and mental competence)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (OCLC):</strong> <strong>O</strong>ffer, <strong>C</strong>onsideration, <strong>L</strong>egal purpose, <strong>C</strong>ompetent parties. &quot;Only Contracts Legally Count&quot; if all four are present.</p></div><h4>Counter-offers</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A counter-offer is a rejection of the original offer. It <strong>voids</strong> the original, so it can no longer be accepted.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/contract\">Cornell LII: Contract</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/counteroffer\">Cornell LII: Counteroffer</a></p>","text":"The basic elements of an enforceable contract: Element What it means Offer and acceptance Mutual assent: one party offers, the other accepts Consideration Something of value is exchanged Legal purpose The agreement serves a lawful purpose Competent parties Parties are legally able to contract (for example, minimum age and mental competence) Memory trick Memory trick (OCLC): O ffer, C onsideration, L egal purpose, C ompetent parties. \"Only Contracts Legally Count\" if all four are present. Counter-offers A counter-offer is a rejection of the original offer. It voids the original, so it can no longer be accepted."},{"id":"ch03-s4","title":"3. Special features of insurance contracts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Definition</th><th>Quick example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aleatory</strong></td><td>Outcome depends on an unpredictable event, so the parties may give and receive very different amounts</td><td>Pay premiums for years with no claim, or pay a small premium and collect a large payout after a loss</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Adhesion</strong></td><td>Drafted entirely by ONE party; the other can only accept or decline. Unclear wording is read AGAINST the writer (the insurer)</td><td>Policy wording is unclear, so the court reads it against the insurer</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>ADHESION</strong> = the insurer wrote it, so unclear wording goes AGAINST the insurer. <strong>ALEATORY</strong> = outcome depends on an uncertain event.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/aleatory-contract\">IRMI: Aleatory contract</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/contract-of-adhesion\">IRMI: Contract of adhesion</a></p>","text":"Feature Definition Quick example Aleatory Outcome depends on an unpredictable event, so the parties may give and receive very different amounts Pay premiums for years with no claim, or pay a small premium and collect a large payout after a loss Adhesion Drafted entirely by ONE party; the other can only accept or decline. Unclear wording is read AGAINST the writer (the insurer) Policy wording is unclear, so the court reads it against the insurer Memory trick ADHESION = the insurer wrote it, so unclear wording goes AGAINST the insurer. ALEATORY = outcome depends on an uncertain event."},{"id":"ch03-s5","title":"4. Valued vs. indemnity contracts (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>VALUED contract</th><th>INDEMNITY contract</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Payment</td><td>Fixed amount agreed in advance, regardless of the actual loss</td><td>Makes the party whole for harm that has occurred</td></tr><tr><td>Used for</td><td>Mainly <strong>life insurance</strong></td><td>Loss-reimbursement coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>A $300,000 death benefit pays exactly $300,000</td><td>A loss is paid to restore the insured, not more</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/valued-policy\">IRMI: Valued policy</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/indemnity\">IRMI: Indemnity</a></p>","text":"VALUED contract INDEMNITY contract Payment Fixed amount agreed in advance, regardless of the actual loss Makes the party whole for harm that has occurred Used for Mainly life insurance Loss-reimbursement coverage Example A $300,000 death benefit pays exactly $300,000 A loss is paid to restore the insured, not more"},{"id":"ch03-s6","title":"5. Insurable interest (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Definition</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>financial or legal stake</strong> in the thing being insured.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> No person may procure insurance on another individual's life unless the benefits are payable to the insured or the insured's representatives, or to a person with an insurable interest in the insured <strong>at the time the contract was made</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">A person of legal capacity may insure his own life for any beneficiary.</li></ul><h4>Who has it</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Self:</strong> Your own life.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Family related by blood or law:</strong> A substantial interest based on love and affection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Others:</strong> A lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Buy-sell parties:</strong> Parties to an agreement to buy or sell a business or closely held shares have an interest in each other, only for that agreement.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Does NOT count:</strong> An interest that arises only from, or grows with, the insured's death or injury.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>For life insurance, check insurable interest <strong>when the contract is made</strong>.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/insurable-interest\">IRMI: Insurable interest</a></p>","text":"Definition A financial or legal stake in the thing being insured. Louisiana: No person may procure insurance on another individual's life unless the benefits are payable to the insured or the insured's representatives, or to a person with an insurable interest in the insured at the time the contract was made . A person of legal capacity may insure his own life for any beneficiary. Who has it Self: Your own life. Family related by blood or law: A substantial interest based on love and affection. Others: A lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety. Buy-sell parties: Parties to an agreement to buy or sell a business or closely held shares have an interest in each other, only for that agreement. Does NOT count: An interest that arises only from, or grows with, the insured's death or injury. Memory trick For life insurance, check insurable interest when the contract is made ."},{"id":"ch03-s7","title":"6. Utmost good faith","html":"<h4>Warranty vs. representation vs. concealment</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>WARRANTY</th><th>REPRESENTATION</th><th>CONCEALMENT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>A factual assertion the insured makes to the insurer about the risk</td><td>A statement the applicant believes true, to the best of their knowledge</td><td>Intentionally keeping back facts that could matter to the insurer, even if not asked</td></tr><tr><td>If false</td><td>The policy can be voided</td><td>If the insurer relied on it and it was false when made, the insurer may void the contract</td><td>The policy can be voided</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>WARRANTY = &quot;I assert it.&quot; REPRESENTATION = &quot;I believe it.&quot; CONCEALMENT = &quot;I held it back.&quot;</p></div><h4>Rescission</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Rescission:</strong> The insurer treats the policy as if it never took effect, for example when the applicant hid a known claim or gave false information that would have led the insurer to decline the policy.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Life policies become incontestable after <strong>2 years</strong> in force during the insured's lifetime from the date of issue; exceptions include nonpayment of premiums and certain military or naval service conditions (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). For individual health and accident policies, after <strong>3 years</strong> from issue, application misstatements (other than fraudulent ones) cannot be used to void the policy or deny a claim for a loss that starts after that period (R.S. 22:975(A)(13)).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/warranty\">IRMI: Warranty</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/representation\">IRMI: Representation</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/concealment\">IRMI: Concealment</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/rescission\">IRMI: Rescission</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"Warranty vs. representation vs. concealment WARRANTY REPRESENTATION CONCEALMENT Definition A factual assertion the insured makes to the insurer about the risk A statement the applicant believes true, to the best of their knowledge Intentionally keeping back facts that could matter to the insurer, even if not asked If false The policy can be voided If the insurer relied on it and it was false when made, the insurer may void the contract The policy can be voided Memory trick WARRANTY = \"I assert it.\" REPRESENTATION = \"I believe it.\" CONCEALMENT = \"I held it back.\" Rescission Rescission: The insurer treats the policy as if it never took effect, for example when the applicant hid a known claim or gave false information that would have led the insurer to decline the policy. Louisiana: Life policies become incontestable after 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime from the date of issue; exceptions include nonpayment of premiums and certain military or naval service conditions (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). For individual health and accident policies, after 3 years from issue, application misstatements (other than fraudulent ones) cannot be used to void the policy or deny a claim for a loss that starts after that period (R.S. 22:975(A)(13))."},{"id":"ch03-s8","title":"7. Void vs. voidable contracts","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/contract\">Cornell LII: Contract</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch03-s9","title":"8. Waiver and estoppel (CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Waiver</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> The intentional or voluntary surrender of a right, claim, or privilege.</li></ul><h4>Estoppel</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> A principle that bars a person from reversing earlier conduct when another party reasonably relied on it.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Example:</strong> An insurer repeatedly accepts late premiums. It may lose the right to cancel for nonpayment later, because the insured reasonably assumed late payments were acceptable.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>WAIVER = giving up a right. ESTOPPEL = barred from reversing conduct someone relied on.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver\">IRMI: Waiver</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/estoppel\">IRMI: Estoppel</a></p>","text":"Waiver Definition: The intentional or voluntary surrender of a right, claim, or privilege. Estoppel Definition: A principle that bars a person from reversing earlier conduct when another party reasonably relied on it. Example: An insurer repeatedly accepts late premiums. It may lose the right to cancel for nonpayment later, because the insured reasonably assumed late payments were acceptable. Memory trick WAIVER = giving up a right. ESTOPPEL = barred from reversing conduct someone relied on."},{"id":"ch03-s10","title":"9. Law of agency","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/fiduciary_duty\">Cornell LII: Fiduciary duty</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch03-s11","title":"10. Agent vs. broker vs. solicitor","html":"<h4>Fiduciary responsibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A fiduciary has a legal obligation to act on behalf of another who trusts them.</li><li class=\"l0\">The fiduciary must put that person's interests first and must not profit personally from the role. Agents and principals are one example.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/fiduciary_duty\">Cornell LII: Fiduciary duty</a></p>","text":"Fiduciary responsibility A fiduciary has a legal obligation to act on behalf of another who trusts them. The fiduciary must put that person's interests first and must not profit personally from the role. Agents and principals are one example."},{"id":"ch03-s12","title":"11. Other legal concepts","html":"<h4>Parol evidence rule</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Earlier or simultaneous oral or written statements generally cannot be used to contradict a written contract the parties meant as their complete agreement.</li><li class=\"l0\">Exceptions include fraud, duress, and mutual mistake.</li></ul><h4>Subrogation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">After paying a claim, the insurer steps into the policyholder's shoes and may collect from the party legally responsible for the loss.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>SUBROGATION = SUBSTITUTE. The insurer steps into the insured's shoes.</p></div><h4>Negligence</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Failing to act with the care a reasonable person would have used in the same situation.</li></ul><h4>Errors and omissions (E&amp;O) insurance</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Protects professionals from liability when their work contains a mistake or a failure to act. It typically pays financial losses.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/parol_evidence_rule\">Cornell LII: Parol evidence rule</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/subrogation\">IRMI: Subrogation</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/negligence\">Cornell LII: Negligence</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/errors-and-omissions-insurance\">IRMI: Errors and omissions insurance</a></p>","text":"Parol evidence rule Earlier or simultaneous oral or written statements generally cannot be used to contradict a written contract the parties meant as their complete agreement. Exceptions include fraud, duress, and mutual mistake. Subrogation After paying a claim, the insurer steps into the policyholder's shoes and may collect from the party legally responsible for the loss. Memory trick SUBROGATION = SUBSTITUTE. The insurer steps into the insured's shoes. Negligence Failing to act with the care a reasonable person would have used in the same situation. Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance Protects professionals from liability when their work contains a mistake or a failure to act. It typically pays financial losses."},{"id":"ch03-s13","title":"12. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Name the four essential elements of a valid contract.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> After how many years is a Louisiana life policy incontestable, and from what date?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What happens when an applicant makes a counter-offer?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Which kind of insurance mainly uses valued contracts?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> A homeowner's neighbor buys a property policy on the homeowner's house. What is missing?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When must insurable interest exist for a Louisiana life policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the difference between a warranty and a representation?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What does estoppel prevent?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What must a fiduciary put first?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the definition of subrogation?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Donna's application says she is a non-smoker. She smokes, but she believed in good faith that her statement was accurate. Is this a warranty or a representation? If it is material and false, what can the insurer do?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> An insurer repeatedly accepts Tom's late premiums, then tries to cancel for one late payment. What doctrine helps Tom?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A person wants a policy on a stranger's life and names himself as beneficiary, with no connection to the stranger. What is missing?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a></p>","text":"1. Name the four essential elements of a valid contract. 2. After how many years is a Louisiana life policy incontestable, and from what date? 3. What happens when an applicant makes a counter-offer? 4. Which kind of insurance mainly uses valued contracts? 5. A homeowner's neighbor buys a property policy on the homeowner's house. What is missing? 6. When must insurable interest exist for a Louisiana life policy? 7. What is the difference between a warranty and a representation? 8. What does estoppel prevent? 9. What must a fiduciary put first? 10. What is the definition of subrogation? Scenario questions Scenario A: Donna's application says she is a non-smoker. She smokes, but she believed in good faith that her statement was accurate. Is this a warranty or a representation? If it is material and false, what can the insurer do? Scenario B: An insurer repeatedly accepts Tom's late premiums, then tries to cancel for one late payment. What doctrine helps Tom? Scenario C: A person wants a policy on a stranger's life and names himself as beneficiary, with no connection to the stranger. What is missing?"},{"id":"ch03-s14","title":"13. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> After 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime, counted from the date of issue (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> The original offer is voided. There is no contract unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Life insurance.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Insurable interest. The neighbor has no stake in the house.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> At the time the contract was made (R.S. 22:901(A)).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> A warranty is a factual assertion; if false, the policy can be voided. A representation is a statement believed true to the best of the applicant's knowledge.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> It bars a person from reversing earlier conduct that another party reasonably relied on.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> The other person's interests.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> After paying a claim, the insurer steps into the policyholder's shoes and collects from the party legally responsible.</p><p>Scenario A: A representation (believed true, to the best of knowledge). If it is material and false, the insurer may be able to rescind, subject to Louisiana's incontestability rules.</p><p>Scenario B: Estoppel. The insurer may be barred from reversing conduct Tom reasonably relied on.</p><p>Scenario C: Insurable interest. Louisiana does not allow insurance on another person's life unless the benefits go to the insured or to someone with an insurable interest.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 3:</strong> Build a one-page checklist: 4 elements, aleatory and adhesion, insurable interest timing, and warranty vs. representation vs. concealment. Quiz yourself for 5 minutes a day.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/contract\">Cornell LII: Contract</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/counteroffer\">Cornell LII: Counteroffer</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/estoppel\">IRMI: Estoppel</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/subrogation\">IRMI: Subrogation</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/fiduciary_duty\">Cornell LII: Fiduciary duty</a></p>","text":"1. Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties. 2. After 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime, counted from the date of issue (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). 3. The original offer is voided. There is no contract unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer. 4. Life insurance. 5. Insurable interest. The neighbor has no stake in the house. 6. At the time the contract was made (R.S. 22:901(A)). 7. A warranty is a factual assertion; if false, the policy can be voided. A representation is a statement believed true to the best of the applicant's knowledge. 8. It bars a person from reversing earlier conduct that another party reasonably relied on. 9. The other person's interests. 10. After paying a claim, the insurer steps into the policyholder's shoes and collects from the party legally responsible. Scenario A: A representation (believed true, to the best of knowledge). If it is material and false, the insurer may be able to rescind, subject to Louisiana's incontestability rules. Scenario B: Estoppel. The insurer may be barred from reversing conduct Tom reasonably relied on. Scenario C: Insurable interest. Louisiana does not allow insurance on another person's life unless the benefits go to the insured or to someone with an insurable interest. Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 3: Build a one-page checklist: 4 elements, aleatory and adhesion, insurable interest timing, and warranty vs. representation vs. concealment. Quiz yourself for 5 minutes a day."}]},{"id":"ch04","short":"Ch 4","title":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","label":"Chapter 4","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Underwriting = deciding WHO gets coverage, at WHAT rate. Learn the 4 risk classes, the 4 premium receipt types, the application's 3 parts, the information sources, and the free-look and replacement rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch04-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Underwriting guards against <strong>adverse selection</strong>: people who expect a high chance of loss buy insurance far more often than low-risk people.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a></p>","text":"Underwriting guards against adverse selection : people who expect a high chance of loss buy insurance far more often than low-risk people."},{"id":"ch04-s2","title":"1. Purpose of underwriting","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Goal:</strong> Protect against adverse selection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurable interest</strong> must exist (see next section).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a></p>","text":"Goal: Protect against adverse selection. Insurable interest must exist (see next section)."},{"id":"ch04-s3","title":"2. Insurable interest (Chapter 4 view)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Why:</strong> Louisiana bars insuring another person's life unless benefits are payable to the insured or the insured's representatives, or to someone with an insurable interest.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Timing:</strong> Judged at the time the contract was made.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Insurable interest?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self</td><td>Yes. May insure own life for any beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td>Family related by blood or law</td><td>Yes, a substantial interest based on love and affection</td></tr><tr><td>Business partners with a buy-sell agreement</td><td>Yes, but only for purposes of that agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Other persons (employer, creditor)</td><td>Only with a lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety</td></tr><tr><td>Interest that arises only from, or grows with, the insured's death or injury</td><td>NO</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a></p>","text":"Why: Louisiana bars insuring another person's life unless benefits are payable to the insured or the insured's representatives, or to someone with an insurable interest. Timing: Judged at the time the contract was made. Relationship Insurable interest? Self Yes. May insure own life for any beneficiary Family related by blood or law Yes, a substantial interest based on love and affection Business partners with a buy-sell agreement Yes, but only for purposes of that agreement Other persons (employer, creditor) Only with a lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety Interest that arises only from, or grows with, the insured's death or injury NO"},{"id":"ch04-s4","title":"3. Elements of insurable risk (life version)","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Law of large numbers:</strong> As more separate units face the possibility of loss, actual losses are more likely to match expected losses.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/law-of-large-numbers\">IRMI: Law of large numbers</a></p>","text":"Law of large numbers: As more separate units face the possibility of loss, actual losses are more likely to match expected losses."},{"id":"ch04-s5","title":"4. Who is involved","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/insurable-interest\">IRMI: Insurable interest</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch04-s6","title":"5. Agent responsibilities","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>E&amp;O:</strong> Protects professionals from liability when their work contains a mistake or a failure to act.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/errors-and-omissions-insurance\">IRMI: Errors and omissions insurance</a></p>","text":"E&O: Protects professionals from liability when their work contains a mistake or a failure to act."},{"id":"ch04-s7","title":"6. The application","html":"<h4>Representations vs. warranties</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Representation</th><th>Warranty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Meaning</td><td>A statement the applicant believes true, to the best of their knowledge</td><td>A factual assertion the insured makes about the risk</td></tr><tr><td>If false</td><td>If the insurer relied on it and it was false when made, the insurer may void the contract</td><td>The policy can be voided</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Minors on the application</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Majority is attained at 18 (Civil Code art. 29).</li><li class=\"l0\">A minor at least 15 years old (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance, and cannot void the contract because of minority (R.S. 22:852(B)).</li><li class=\"l0\">A minor who is not emancipated is not bound by an unperformed promise to pay premiums.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/representation\">IRMI: Representation</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/warranty\">IRMI: Warranty</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=109928\">La. Civil Code art. 29</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507733\">La. R.S. 22:852</a></p>","text":"Representations vs. warranties Representation Warranty Meaning A statement the applicant believes true, to the best of their knowledge A factual assertion the insured makes about the risk If false If the insurer relied on it and it was false when made, the insurer may void the contract The policy can be voided Minors on the application Louisiana: Majority is attained at 18 (Civil Code art. 29). A minor at least 15 years old (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance, and cannot void the contract because of minority (R.S. 22:852(B)). A minor who is not emancipated is not bound by an unperformed promise to pay premiums."},{"id":"ch04-s8","title":"7. Premium receipts (and when coverage starts)","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch04-s9","title":"8. Underwriting information sources","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch04-s10","title":"9. Federal laws in underwriting","html":"<h4>Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Investigative consumer report:</strong> Before procuring one, the requester must clearly disclose to the consumer in writing that a report may be made (character, reputation, personal characteristics, mode of living), within three days after first requesting it.</li><li class=\"l0\">On the consumer's written request, the requester must give a complete and accurate disclosure of the nature and scope of the investigation within five days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Adverse action:</strong> If action is taken based on a consumer report, the consumer must be told, given the reporting agency's name, address, and phone number, and told the agency did not make the decision.</li><li class=\"l0\">The consumer may get a free copy of the report within 60 days and may dispute its accuracy.</li></ul><h4>Anti-money-laundering programs (31 U.S.C. 5318(h))</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Each financial institution must establish anti-money-laundering and countering-the-financing-of-terrorism programs.</li><li class=\"l0\">Minimum parts: internal policies, procedures, and controls; a designated compliance officer; ongoing employee training; an independent audit function.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681d\">15 U.S.C. 1681d</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681m\">15 U.S.C. 1681m</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/5318\">31 U.S.C. 5318</a></p>","text":"Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) Investigative consumer report: Before procuring one, the requester must clearly disclose to the consumer in writing that a report may be made (character, reputation, personal characteristics, mode of living), within three days after first requesting it. On the consumer's written request, the requester must give a complete and accurate disclosure of the nature and scope of the investigation within five days. Adverse action: If action is taken based on a consumer report, the consumer must be told, given the reporting agency's name, address, and phone number, and told the agency did not make the decision. The consumer may get a free copy of the report within 60 days and may dispute its accuracy. Anti-money-laundering programs (31 U.S.C. 5318(h)) Each financial institution must establish anti-money-laundering and countering-the-financing-of-terrorism programs. Minimum parts: internal policies, procedures, and controls; a designated compliance officer; ongoing employee training; an independent audit function."},{"id":"ch04-s11","title":"10. Required disclosures","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch04-s12","title":"11. Risk classification (the 4 classes)","html":"<p>No source-verified content for this section.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a></p>","text":"No source-verified content for this section."},{"id":"ch04-s13","title":"12. Policy issue and delivery","html":"<h4>Free-look period</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sale type</th><th>Free-look period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual life (Louisiana)</td><td>10 days from receipt of the policy (R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a))</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> If the policy differs from what was explained, the insured may surrender it within the 10 days and get back any premium paid.</p></div><h4>Delivery</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">If a producer delivers the policy, the policyholder signs a receipt showing the policy number and delivery date. The insurer keeps it for two consecutive years.</li><li class=\"l0\">If mailed, the policy goes by certified mail, return receipt requested (or the insurer obtains a certificate of mailing). It is presumed received 10 days after mailing.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Free-look period Sale type Free-look period Individual life (Louisiana) 10 days from receipt of the policy (R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a)) Memory trick Study tip: If the policy differs from what was explained, the insured may surrender it within the 10 days and get back any premium paid. Delivery If a producer delivers the policy, the policyholder signs a receipt showing the policy number and delivery date. The insurer keeps it for two consecutive years. If mailed, the policy goes by certified mail, return receipt requested (or the insurer obtains a certificate of mailing). It is presumed received 10 days after mailing."},{"id":"ch04-s14","title":"13. Exam quick reference","html":"<h4>Critical points</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Adverse selection:</strong> higher-risk people buy insurance more often.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurable interest:</strong> measured when the contract is made.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana life incontestability:</strong> 2 years from issue, in force during the insured's lifetime.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana free look:</strong> 10 days from receipt of the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>FCRA:</strong> disclosure of an investigative consumer report within 3 days of the request.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minors:</strong> age 15 or older (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681d\">15 U.S.C. 1681d</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507733\">La. R.S. 22:852</a></p>","text":"Critical points Adverse selection: higher-risk people buy insurance more often. Insurable interest: measured when the contract is made. Louisiana life incontestability: 2 years from issue, in force during the insured's lifetime. Louisiana free look: 10 days from receipt of the policy. FCRA: disclosure of an investigative consumer report within 3 days of the request. Minors: age 15 or older (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance."},{"id":"ch04-s15","title":"14. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is adverse selection?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> After how many years can application misstatements (other than fraud) no longer void an individual Louisiana health and accident policy or defeat a later claim?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> For a non-family person, what kind of interest does Louisiana require to insure another's life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A statement the applicant believes true to the best of their knowledge: representation or warranty?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> At what age (nearest birthday) may a Louisiana minor contract for life insurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Which federal law requires disclosure when an investigative consumer report may be made?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Within how many days of the request must that disclosure be made?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> When is a mailed Louisiana life policy presumed received?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the age of majority in Louisiana?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> A Louisiana life policy was issued 18 months ago and the insured has just died. Has the 2-year incontestability point passed?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Sam, a business owner, wants to insure his key employee. What test decides whether his company has insurable interest?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Max is 16 and wants to buy a life policy on himself in Louisiana. May he?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507733\">La. R.S. 22:852</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=109928\">La. Civil Code art. 29</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681d\">15 U.S.C. 1681d</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/representation\">IRMI: Representation</a></p>","text":"1. What is adverse selection? 2. After how many years can application misstatements (other than fraud) no longer void an individual Louisiana health and accident policy or defeat a later claim? 3. For a non-family person, what kind of interest does Louisiana require to insure another's life? 4. A statement the applicant believes true to the best of their knowledge: representation or warranty? 5. At what age (nearest birthday) may a Louisiana minor contract for life insurance? 6. What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start? 7. Which federal law requires disclosure when an investigative consumer report may be made? 8. Within how many days of the request must that disclosure be made? 9. When is a mailed Louisiana life policy presumed received? 10. What is the age of majority in Louisiana? Scenario questions Scenario A: A Louisiana life policy was issued 18 months ago and the insured has just died. Has the 2-year incontestability point passed? Scenario B: Sam, a business owner, wants to insure his key employee. What test decides whether his company has insurable interest? Scenario C: Max is 16 and wants to buy a life policy on himself in Louisiana. May he?"},{"id":"ch04-s16","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> People who expect a high chance of loss buy insurance far more often than low-risk people.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> 3 years (R.S. 22:975(A)(13)).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> A lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety (R.S. 22:901(C)).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A representation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> 15 (R.S. 22:852(B)).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 10 days, starting from receipt of the policy.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> The Fair Credit Reporting Act.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Within three days after the report is first requested.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> 10 days after mailing.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> 18 (Civil Code art. 29).</p><p>Scenario A: No. Two years have not passed, so the incontestability point has not been reached.</p><p>Scenario B: A lawful and substantial economic interest in the key employee's continued life.</p><p>Scenario C: Yes. A minor 15 or older (nearest birthday) may contract for life insurance and cannot void it because of minority.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 4:</strong> Make a one-page sheet: insurable interest (22:901), minors at 15 (22:852), incontestability (2 and 3 years), free look (10 days), FCRA disclosure (3 days).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507771\">La. R.S. 22:901</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507733\">La. R.S. 22:852</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=109928\">La. Civil Code art. 29</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681d\">15 U.S.C. 1681d</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a></p>","text":"1. People who expect a high chance of loss buy insurance far more often than low-risk people. 2. 3 years (R.S. 22:975(A)(13)). 3. A lawful and substantial economic interest in the insured's continued life, health, or bodily safety (R.S. 22:901(C)). 4. A representation. 5. 15 (R.S. 22:852(B)). 6. 10 days, starting from receipt of the policy. 7. The Fair Credit Reporting Act. 8. Within three days after the report is first requested. 9. 10 days after mailing. 10. 18 (Civil Code art. 29). Scenario A: No. Two years have not passed, so the incontestability point has not been reached. Scenario B: A lawful and substantial economic interest in the key employee's continued life. Scenario C: Yes. A minor 15 or older (nearest birthday) may contract for life insurance and cannot void it because of minority. Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 4: Make a one-page sheet: insurable interest (22:901), minors at 15 (22:852), incontestability (2 and 3 years), free look (10 days), FCRA disclosure (3 days)."}]},{"id":"ch05_1","short":"Ch 5.1","title":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","label":"Chapter 5.1","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> This is the biggest &quot;which policy is this?&quot; chapter. Sort every product into TERM (temporary, no cash value) or PERMANENT (cash value). Then learn the flexible and variable products, and the tax traps (MEC, endowment).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch05_1-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Universal life</strong> = premium is usually flexible within limits; the policy continues while enough money remains to cover costs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Variable life</strong> = its own line of authority in Louisiana.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/universal-life-insurance\">IRMI: Universal life insurance</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=508521\">La. R.S. 22:1547</a></p>","text":"Universal life = premium is usually flexible within limits; the policy continues while enough money remains to cover costs. Variable life = its own line of authority in Louisiana."},{"id":"ch05_1-s2","title":"1. Three primary categories of life insurance","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s3","title":"2. Term life insurance","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Legal note:</strong> A borrower may buy required or requested insurance from an agent or broker of his own selection.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=106298\">La. R.S. 6:969.29</a></p>","text":"Legal note: A borrower may buy required or requested insurance from an agent or broker of his own selection."},{"id":"ch05_1-s4","title":"3. Key features of term life","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s5","title":"4. Whole life insurance","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s6","title":"5. Whole life payment options","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s7","title":"6. Endowment policies","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s8","title":"7. Family policies","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s9","title":"8. Joint life policies","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s10","title":"9. Universal and flexible products","html":"<h4>Universal life (UL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Flexible premium:</strong> usually flexible within limits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Unbundled:</strong> an accumulated fund earns interest; mortality and rider costs are deducted from it.</li><li class=\"l0\">The policy continues as long as enough money remains to cover those costs.</li></ul><h4>Corridor</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A federal tax test: the death benefit must always be at least a set percentage of the cash surrender value. The percentage falls as the insured ages (250% up to age 40, down to 100% near age 95).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/universal-life-insurance\">IRMI: Universal life insurance</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702\">26 U.S.C. 7702 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Universal life (UL) Flexible premium: usually flexible within limits. Unbundled: an accumulated fund earns interest; mortality and rider costs are deducted from it. The policy continues as long as enough money remains to cover those costs. Corridor A federal tax test: the death benefit must always be at least a set percentage of the cash surrender value. The percentage falls as the insured ages (250% up to age 40, down to 100% near age 95)."},{"id":"ch05_1-s11","title":"10. Variable and equity-indexed products","html":"<h4>Regulation of variable products</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana variable products:</strong> R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists &quot;variable life and variable annuity&quot; as a separate line of authority.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=508521\">La. R.S. 22:1547</a></p>","text":"Regulation of variable products Louisiana variable products: R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists \"variable life and variable annuity\" as a separate line of authority."},{"id":"ch05_1-s12","title":"11. Modified endowment contracts (MECs) (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>7-pay test:</strong> A contract fails if premiums paid during its first 7 contract years exceed the net level premiums that would have paid up the benefits with 7 level annual premiums. Failing the test = MEC.</li><li class=\"l0\">Applies to contracts entered into on or after June 21, 1988.</li><li class=\"l0\">A material change (including any increase in the death benefit) is treated as a new contract, so the test starts again.</li><li class=\"l0\">A contract received in exchange for a MEC is also a MEC.</li></ul><h4>MEC tax treatment</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Loans and assignments</strong> are treated as amounts received under the contract; withdrawals come out as income first.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>10% additional tax</strong> on the part included in gross income, unless the distribution is made on or after age 59½, is due to disability, or is a series of substantially equal periodic payments.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A\">26 U.S.C. 7702A (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">26 U.S.C. 72 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"7-pay test: A contract fails if premiums paid during its first 7 contract years exceed the net level premiums that would have paid up the benefits with 7 level annual premiums. Failing the test = MEC. Applies to contracts entered into on or after June 21, 1988. A material change (including any increase in the death benefit) is treated as a new contract, so the test starts again. A contract received in exchange for a MEC is also a MEC. MEC tax treatment Loans and assignments are treated as amounts received under the contract; withdrawals come out as income first. 10% additional tax on the part included in gross income, unless the distribution is made on or after age 59½, is due to disability, or is a series of substantially equal periodic payments."},{"id":"ch05_1-s13","title":"12. Participating vs. nonparticipating","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s14","title":"13. Specialized policies and features","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none retained</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch05_1-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the 7-pay test?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What extra tax applies to MEC distributions before age 59½?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Which R.S. section lists variable life and variable annuity as a line of authority?</p><h4>Scenario</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario:</strong> A lender tells a borrower to buy required insurance only from the lender&#39;s own agency. What does Louisiana law say?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A\">26 U.S.C. 7702A (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">26 U.S.C. 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=508521\">La. R.S. 22:1547</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=106298\">La. R.S. 6:969.29</a></p>","text":"1. What is the 7-pay test? 2. What extra tax applies to MEC distributions before age 59½? 3. Which R.S. section lists variable life and variable annuity as a line of authority? Scenario Scenario: A lender tells a borrower to buy required insurance only from the lender's own agency. What does Louisiana law say?"},{"id":"ch05_1-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Premiums in the first 7 contract years must not exceed the net level premiums that would pay up the benefits with 7 level annual premiums. Failing it makes the policy a MEC.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> A 10% additional tax on the taxable part (with exceptions for age 59½, disability, and periodic payments).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> R.S. 22:1547(A)(3).</p><p>Scenario: The borrower may buy required or requested insurance from an agent or broker of his own selection (R.S. 6:969.29).</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A\">26 U.S.C. 7702A (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">26 U.S.C. 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=508521\">La. R.S. 22:1547</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=106298\">La. R.S. 6:969.29</a></p>","text":"1. Premiums in the first 7 contract years must not exceed the net level premiums that would pay up the benefits with 7 level annual premiums. Failing it makes the policy a MEC. 2. A 10% additional tax on the taxable part (with exceptions for age 59½, disability, and periodic payments). 3. R.S. 22:1547(A)(3). Scenario: The borrower may buy required or requested insurance from an agent or broker of his own selection (R.S. 6:969.29)."}]},{"id":"ch05_2","short":"Ch 5.2","title":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","label":"Chapter 5.2","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three groups to master. (1) <strong>Standard provisions</strong> (the rules every policy has, such as the grace period, reinstatement, incontestable clause). (2) <strong>Options and riders</strong> (cash value options, dividend options, add-ons). (3) <strong>Exclusions</strong> (when the insurer does NOT pay). Know the key numbers in the Quick Numbers table.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch05_2-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Two years</strong> matters for two rules: the incontestable clause (life policies) and the suicide exclusion.</li><li class=\"l0\">Nonforfeiture options apply when premiums stop and the policy has cash value.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507791\">La. R.S. 22:935</a></p>","text":"Two years matters for two rules: the incontestable clause (life policies) and the suicide exclusion. Nonforfeiture options apply when premiums stop and the policy has cash value."},{"id":"ch05_2-s2","title":"1. Standard policy provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Incontestable clause</strong></td><td>After 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime, the insurer cannot contest the policy. Exceptions: nonpayment of premiums; military or naval service conditions; and, at the insurer's option, disability benefits and accidental death riders</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Misstatement of age</strong></td><td>If age was misstated, the benefit is adjusted to what the premium would have bought at the correct age</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Suicide</strong></td><td>Excluded if death occurs within 2 years from the issue date. The insurer then pays at least the policy reserve, plus paid-up additions and dividends, less any debt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grace period</strong></td><td>For each premium after the first: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option (life insurance). Coverage continues during the grace period</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reinstatement</strong></td><td>A lapsed life policy may be reinstated at any time within 3 years from the date of default</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Free look</strong></td><td>10 days from the date the owner receives the policy, to examine its provisions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Provision What it does Incontestable clause After 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime, the insurer cannot contest the policy. Exceptions: nonpayment of premiums; military or naval service conditions; and, at the insurer's option, disability benefits and accidental death riders Misstatement of age If age was misstated, the benefit is adjusted to what the premium would have bought at the correct age Suicide Excluded if death occurs within 2 years from the issue date. The insurer then pays at least the policy reserve, plus paid-up additions and dividends, less any debt Grace period For each premium after the first: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option (life insurance). Coverage continues during the grace period Reinstatement A lapsed life policy may be reinstated at any time within 3 years from the date of default Free look 10 days from the date the owner receives the policy, to examine its provisions"},{"id":"ch05_2-s3","title":"2. Cash value provisions","html":"<h4>Nonforfeiture (when premiums stop)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">If no other option is elected, the automatic nonforfeiture option applies. It is paid-up insurance on the same life, or continued insurance at the full face amount including accumulated dividends, less policy loans, as the policy provides.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may defer payment of a cash surrender value up to 6 months after demand. If it is not paid within 30 days of demand, interest is due.</li></ul><h4>Policy loans</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">An owner may take a loan on a policy that has loan value.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may defer a loan up to 6 months. This does not apply to a loan used to pay premiums.</li><li class=\"l0\">If loan plus accrued interest equals or exceeds the loan value, the policy terminates after the notice required by R.S. 22:905.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507791\">La. R.S. 22:935</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507792\">La. R.S. 22:936</a></p>","text":"Nonforfeiture (when premiums stop) If no other option is elected, the automatic nonforfeiture option applies. It is paid-up insurance on the same life, or continued insurance at the full face amount including accumulated dividends, less policy loans, as the policy provides. The insurer may defer payment of a cash surrender value up to 6 months after demand. If it is not paid within 30 days of demand, interest is due. Policy loans An owner may take a loan on a policy that has loan value. The insurer may defer a loan up to 6 months. This does not apply to a loan used to pay premiums. If loan plus accrued interest equals or exceeds the loan value, the policy terminates after the notice required by R.S. 22:905."},{"id":"ch05_2-s4","title":"3. Policy dividends","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Dividends the insurer keeps to pay premiums are <strong>not taxable</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Dividends paid to the owner are a partial return of premiums. They are not included in income until they exceed the total net premiums paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Interest</strong> on dividends left on deposit with the insurer <strong>is taxable</strong>.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550\">IRS Publication 550</a></p>","text":"Dividends the insurer keeps to pay premiums are not taxable . Dividends paid to the owner are a partial return of premiums. They are not included in income until they exceed the total net premiums paid. Interest on dividends left on deposit with the insurer is taxable ."},{"id":"ch05_2-s5","title":"4. Riders (add-ons; usually extra premium)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Accidental death and disability provisions are exempt from the incontestable clause only at the insurer's option.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Accidental death and disability provisions are exempt from the incontestable clause only at the insurer's option."},{"id":"ch05_2-s6","title":"5. Living benefits and settlement options","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accelerated benefits (terminal illness)</strong></td><td>A physician certifies that death can reasonably be expected within 24 months or less. Amounts received are excluded from gross income as death proceeds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long-term care (chronically ill)</strong></td><td>The insured must be unable to perform at least 2 of the 6 activities of daily living (eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence) without substantial help, for at least 90 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B</a></p>","text":"Benefit Key facts Accelerated benefits (terminal illness) A physician certifies that death can reasonably be expected within 24 months or less. Amounts received are excluded from gross income as death proceeds Long-term care (chronically ill) The insured must be unable to perform at least 2 of the 6 activities of daily living (eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence) without substantial help, for at least 90 days"},{"id":"ch05_2-s7","title":"6. Policy exclusions (when the insurer does NOT pay)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Suicide:</strong> excluded if death occurs within 2 years from the issue date.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>War:</strong> a policy may exclude death from declared or undeclared war, and death while serving in the military forces of a country at war, including within 6 months after that service ends.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Hazardous occupation or residence in a specified foreign country:</strong> these exclusions may apply only within the first 2 years from the issue date.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Aviation:</strong> a policy may exclude death from aviation under conditions the policy specifies.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Suicide: excluded if death occurs within 2 years from the issue date. War: a policy may exclude death from declared or undeclared war, and death while serving in the military forces of a country at war, including within 6 months after that service ends. Hazardous occupation or residence in a specified foreign country: these exclusions may apply only within the first 2 years from the issue date. Aviation: a policy may exclude death from aviation under conditions the policy specifies."},{"id":"ch05_2-s8","title":"7. Quick numbers (memorize)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incontestable period (life)</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Suicide exclusion</td><td>2 years from issue</td></tr><tr><td>Grace period (life)</td><td>30 days, or one month at the insurer's option</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement window (life)</td><td>Within 3 years of default</td></tr><tr><td>Free look</td><td>10 days from receipt of the policy</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term care trigger</td><td>Cannot perform 2 of 6 ADLs, for at least 90 days</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerated benefit (terminal illness)</td><td>Certified death within 24 months or less</td></tr><tr><td>Policy loan deferral</td><td>Up to 6 months (not for a loan used to pay premiums)</td></tr><tr><td>Cash surrender value deferral</td><td>Up to 6 months after demand</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507792\">La. R.S. 22:936</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B</a></p>","text":"Item Number Incontestable period (life) 2 years Suicide exclusion 2 years from issue Grace period (life) 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option Reinstatement window (life) Within 3 years of default Free look 10 days from receipt of the policy Long-term care trigger Cannot perform 2 of 6 ADLs, for at least 90 days Accelerated benefit (terminal illness) Certified death within 24 months or less Policy loan deferral Up to 6 months (not for a loan used to pay premiums) Cash surrender value deferral Up to 6 months after demand"},{"id":"ch05_2-s9","title":"8. Critical concepts and vocabulary","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dividends:</strong> dividends used to pay premiums are not taxable. Interest on dividends left with the insurer is taxable.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy loans:</strong> the insurer may defer a loan up to 6 months, except a loan used to pay premiums.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550\">IRS Publication 550</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Dividends: dividends used to pay premiums are not taxable. Interest on dividends left with the insurer is taxable. Policy loans: the insurer may defer a loan up to 6 months, except a loan used to pay premiums."},{"id":"ch05_2-s10","title":"9. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incontestable clause vs. suicide exclusion</td><td>Incontestable: after 2 years the insurer cannot contest the policy. Suicide: excluded only within the first 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends vs. interest on dividends</td><td>Dividends used to pay premiums: not taxable. Interest on dividends left with the insurer: taxable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550\">IRS Publication 550</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Incontestable clause vs. suicide exclusion Incontestable: after 2 years the insurer cannot contest the policy. Suicide: excluded only within the first 2 years Dividends vs. interest on dividends Dividends used to pay premiums: not taxable. Interest on dividends left with the insurer: taxable"},{"id":"ch05_2-s11","title":"10. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How long is the incontestable period for a life policy, and what are its main exceptions?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> The insured dies by suicide in month 18. How much does the insurer pay?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Are dividends that the insurer keeps to pay premiums taxable?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> How long is the grace period for a life policy premium?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550\">IRS Publication 550</a></p>","text":"1. How long is the incontestable period for a life policy, and what are its main exceptions? 2. The insured dies by suicide in month 18. How much does the insurer pay? 3. Are dividends that the insurer keeps to pay premiums taxable? 4. How long is the grace period for a life policy premium?"},{"id":"ch05_2-s12","title":"11. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Two years. Exceptions: nonpayment of premiums, military or naval service conditions, and (at the insurer's option) disability benefits and accidental death riders.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> At least the policy reserve, plus paid-up additions and dividends, less any debt.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> No. Dividends used to pay premiums are not taxable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550\">IRS Publication 550</a></p>","text":"1. Two years. Exceptions: nonpayment of premiums, military or naval service conditions, and (at the insurer's option) disability benefits and accidental death riders. 2. At least the policy reserve, plus paid-up additions and dividends, less any debt. 3. No. Dividends used to pay premiums are not taxable. 4. 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option."}]},{"id":"ch06","short":"Ch 6","title":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","label":"Chapter 6","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Group life = one master contract (employer = contract holder/owner), many certificate holders (employees). Know the participation rules, the conversion rules, the tax thresholds, and the special groups (SGLI, FSGLI, VGLI, FEGLI, franchise, credit, blanket).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch06-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The individual-policy standard provisions in R.S. 22:931 do not apply to group life (R.S. 22:931(C)).</li><li class=\"l0\">A member whose employment ends may convert to an individual policy within 31 days, without proof of insurability.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507795\">La. R.S. 22:942</a></p>","text":"The individual-policy standard provisions in R.S. 22:931 do not apply to group life (R.S. 22:931(C)). A member whose employment ends may convert to an individual policy within 31 days, without proof of insurability."},{"id":"ch06-s2","title":"1. Group vs. individual insurance","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Group life is not subject to the individual-policy standard provisions of R.S. 22:931 (R.S. 22:931(C)).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Group life is not subject to the individual-policy standard provisions of R.S. 22:931 (R.S. 22:931(C))."},{"id":"ch06-s3","title":"2. Contributory vs. noncontributory plans (CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The premium may be paid from the policyholder's funds, from funds contributed by the covered persons, or from both (R.S. 22:941).</li><li class=\"l0\">If the employer does not pay the entire premium, the insurer may, but is not required to, set a minimum percentage of eligible employees who must enroll (R.S. 22:941).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a></p>","text":"The premium may be paid from the policyholder's funds, from funds contributed by the covered persons, or from both (R.S. 22:941). If the employer does not pay the entire premium, the insurer may, but is not required to, set a minimum percentage of eligible employees who must enroll (R.S. 22:941)."},{"id":"ch06-s4","title":"3. Eligible groups and eligible employees","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Eligible persons are all employees of an employer, all members of an association or their employees, or any class defined by employment or membership conditions (R.S. 22:941).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a></p>","text":"Eligible persons are all employees of an employer, all members of an association or their employees, or any class defined by employment or membership conditions (R.S. 22:941)."},{"id":"ch06-s5","title":"4. Underwriting group life","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a></p>","text":""},{"id":"ch06-s6","title":"5. Conversion privileges (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A member whose coverage ends because employment ends may get an individual policy without evidence of insurability, if the application and first premium are paid within 31 days after termination (R.S. 22:942).</li><li class=\"l0\">The individual policy has no disability or other supplementary benefits. It is one of the forms the insurer then customarily issues, at the age and for the amount applied for (R.S. 22:942).</li><li class=\"l0\">Coverage stays in force until the end of the paid premium period, not exceeding 31 days (R.S. 22:942).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Master policy terminated:</strong> an individual insured for at least 5 years before termination is entitled to an individual policy (R.S. 22:942).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507795\">La. R.S. 22:942</a></p>","text":"A member whose coverage ends because employment ends may get an individual policy without evidence of insurability, if the application and first premium are paid within 31 days after termination (R.S. 22:942). The individual policy has no disability or other supplementary benefits. It is one of the forms the insurer then customarily issues, at the age and for the amount applied for (R.S. 22:942). Coverage stays in force until the end of the paid premium period, not exceeding 31 days (R.S. 22:942). Master policy terminated: an individual insured for at least 5 years before termination is entitled to an individual policy (R.S. 22:942)."},{"id":"ch06-s7","title":"6. Beneficiary designation and ownership","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a></p>","text":""},{"id":"ch06-s8","title":"7. Special types of group life","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507794\">La. R.S. 22:941</a></p>","text":""},{"id":"ch06-s9","title":"8. Taxation of group life","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Plan benefits 70% or more of all employees</strong></td><td>One of two alternative eligibility tests (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(3)(A))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>At least 85% of participants are not key employees</strong></td><td>The other alternative test. \"Key employee\" has the meaning in 26 U.S.C. 416(i)(1) (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(6))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>First $50,000 of coverage</strong></td><td>The cost of $50,000 of group term insurance is excluded from income (26 U.S.C. 79(a)(1))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Discriminatory plan</strong></td><td>The $50,000 exclusion does not apply to a key employee (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(1)(A))</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/79\">26 U.S.C. 79</a></p>","text":"Rule Detail Plan benefits 70% or more of all employees One of two alternative eligibility tests (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(3)(A)) At least 85% of participants are not key employees The other alternative test. \"Key employee\" has the meaning in 26 U.S.C. 416(i)(1) (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(6)) First $50,000 of coverage The cost of $50,000 of group term insurance is excluded from income (26 U.S.C. 79(a)(1)) Discriminatory plan The $50,000 exclusion does not apply to a key employee (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(1)(A))"},{"id":"ch06-s10","title":"9. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group conversion vs. master policy termination conversion</td><td>Leaving employee: 31 days after termination. Master policy terminated: members insured for at least 5 years</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507795\">La. R.S. 22:942</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Group conversion vs. master policy termination conversion Leaving employee: 31 days after termination. Master policy terminated: members insured for at least 5 years"},{"id":"ch06-s11","title":"10. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How long after employment ends can a member convert group life coverage?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Does conversion require evidence of insurability?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> How much group term coverage per employee is excluded from income?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What share of all employees must a plan benefit to pass the first eligibility test?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507795\">La. R.S. 22:942</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/79\">26 U.S.C. 79</a></p>","text":"1. How long after employment ends can a member convert group life coverage? 2. Does conversion require evidence of insurability? 3. How much group term coverage per employee is excluded from income? 4. What share of all employees must a plan benefit to pass the first eligibility test?"},{"id":"ch06-s12","title":"11. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 31 days after termination (R.S. 22:942).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> No (R.S. 22:942).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> $50,000 (26 U.S.C. 79(a)(1)). Cost above $50,000 is not excluded.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 70% (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(3)(A)).</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507795\">La. R.S. 22:942</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/79\">26 U.S.C. 79</a></p>","text":"1. 31 days after termination (R.S. 22:942). 2. No (R.S. 22:942). 3. $50,000 (26 U.S.C. 79(a)(1)). Cost above $50,000 is not excluded. 4. 70% (26 U.S.C. 79(d)(3)(A))."}]},{"id":"ch07","short":"Ch 7","title":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","label":"Chapter 7","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three things drive premiums (mortality, interest, expenses). Then: how premiums are funded, how policies are compared, who gets the money (beneficiaries), how the money is paid (settlement options), and how it is taxed. Most points are &quot;which way does it go?&quot; questions.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch07-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Death benefit proceeds paid because of the insured's death are generally income-tax-free.</strong></li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a></p>","text":"Death benefit proceeds paid because of the insured's death are generally income-tax-free."},{"id":"ch07-s5","title":"4. Paying premiums from policy values","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Modified endowment contract (MEC):</strong> A contract that fails the 7-pay test, meaning the amount paid in the first 7 contract years exceeds the net level premiums for that period.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A\">26 U.S.C. 7702A</a></p>","text":"Modified endowment contract (MEC): A contract that fails the 7-pay test, meaning the amount paid in the first 7 contract years exceeds the net level premiums for that period."},{"id":"ch07-s8","title":"7. Living benefits","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accelerated (death) benefit</strong></td><td>Paid to a terminally ill insured. Certified as expected to die within <strong>24 months</strong>. Tax-free under IRC 101(g)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a></p>","text":"Benefit Key facts Accelerated (death) benefit Paid to a terminally ill insured. Certified as expected to die within 24 months . Tax-free under IRC 101(g)"},{"id":"ch07-s9","title":"8. Viatical and life settlements","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Chronically ill:</strong> Certified as unable to perform at least <strong>2</strong> activities of daily living for at least 90 days without substantial assistance, OR requires substantial supervision because of severe cognitive impairment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Terminally ill:</strong> Expected to die within <strong>24 months</strong>.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a></p>","text":"Chronically ill: Certified as unable to perform at least 2 activities of daily living for at least 90 days without substantial assistance, OR requires substantial supervision because of severe cognitive impairment. Terminally ill: Expected to die within 24 months ."},{"id":"ch07-s12","title":"11. How shares are divided","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example (3 children; one dies with 2 kids)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Per stirpes</strong></td><td>Latin for &quot;by branches.&quot; If a beneficiary dies first, that beneficiary's heirs take the whole share and divide it among themselves</td><td>The 2 surviving children each receive 33.3%. The deceased child's 2 children split their parent's 33.3% (16.65% each)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-stirpes\">IRMI: per stirpes</a></p>","text":"Method Meaning Example (3 children; one dies with 2 kids) Per stirpes Latin for \"by branches.\" If a beneficiary dies first, that beneficiary's heirs take the whole share and divide it among themselves The 2 surviving children each receive 33.3%. The deceased child's 2 children split their parent's 33.3% (16.65% each)"},{"id":"ch07-s13","title":"12. Policy provisions affecting beneficiaries","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Common disaster</strong></td><td>The insured and a life insurance beneficiary appear to die at the same time, with no evidence of who died first</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/common-disaster\">IRMI: common disaster</a></p>","text":"Provision Key facts Common disaster The insured and a life insurance beneficiary appear to die at the same time, with no evidence of who died first"},{"id":"ch07-s14","title":"13. Tax consequences","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Tax treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Premiums</strong></td><td>NOT deductible when the taxpayer is directly or indirectly a beneficiary under the policy (IRC 264(a)(1))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Death proceeds</strong></td><td>Excluded from gross income when paid by reason of death (IRC 101(a)(1))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transfer-for-value rule</strong></td><td>Exception to tax-free proceeds: the exclusion is limited to the consideration paid plus later premiums (IRC 101(a)(2))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Estate tax</strong></td><td>Proceeds are included in the gross estate if the decedent held any incidents of ownership (IRC 2042)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accelerated death benefit</strong></td><td>Tax-free if terminally ill (IRC 101(g))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policy loans (MEC)</strong></td><td>For a modified endowment contract, loans are treated as amounts received and taxed interest-first (IRC 72(e)(4)(A) and 72(e)(10))</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1035 exchange</strong></td><td>No gain or loss is recognized when one life insurance contract is exchanged for another (IRC 1035(a))</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/264\">26 U.S.C. 264</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 U.S.C. 101</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/2042\">26 U.S.C. 2042</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">26 U.S.C. 72</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">26 U.S.C. 1035</a></p>","text":"Item Tax treatment Premiums NOT deductible when the taxpayer is directly or indirectly a beneficiary under the policy (IRC 264(a)(1)) Death proceeds Excluded from gross income when paid by reason of death (IRC 101(a)(1)) Transfer-for-value rule Exception to tax-free proceeds: the exclusion is limited to the consideration paid plus later premiums (IRC 101(a)(2)) Estate tax Proceeds are included in the gross estate if the decedent held any incidents of ownership (IRC 2042) Accelerated death benefit Tax-free if terminally ill (IRC 101(g)) Policy loans (MEC) For a modified endowment contract, loans are treated as amounts received and taxed interest-first (IRC 72(e)(4)(A) and 72(e)(10)) 1035 exchange No gain or loss is recognized when one life insurance contract is exchanged for another (IRC 1035(a))"},{"id":"ch07-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Per stirpes vs. per capita</td><td>Per stirpes = a deceased beneficiary's share passes to that beneficiary's heirs, who divide it. Per capita = Latin for &quot;per head&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-stirpes\">IRMI: per stirpes</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-capita\">IRMI: per capita</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Per stirpes vs. per capita Per stirpes = a deceased beneficiary's share passes to that beneficiary's heirs, who divide it. Per capita = Latin for \"per head\""},{"id":"ch07-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> If a beneficiary dies and there are two surviving children and two grandchildren from the deceased, how does per stirpes divide the share?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-stirpes\">IRMI: per stirpes</a></p>","text":"10. If a beneficiary dies and there are two surviving children and two grandchildren from the deceased, how does per stirpes divide the share?"},{"id":"ch07-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Per stirpes: each surviving child gets one-third (33.3%). The deceased child's two children split their parent's one-third (16.65% each).</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-stirpes\">IRMI: per stirpes</a></p>","text":"10. Per stirpes: each surviving child gets one-third (33.3%). The deceased child's two children split their parent's one-third (16.65% each)."}]},{"id":"ch08","short":"Ch 8","title":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","label":"Chapter 8","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three big parts. (1) <strong>How much life insurance</strong> a family needs (the Needs Approach is the one to know). (2) <strong>Business uses</strong> (key person, buy-sell agreements, COLI, executive bonus). (3) <strong>Annuity basics</strong> (deferred vs. immediate, fixed vs. variable, payout options).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch08-s3","title":"2. Methods for determining the amount of coverage","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Tax timing:</strong> The federal estate tax return is due 9 months after the decedent's death.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/6075\">IRC 6075 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Tax timing: The federal estate tax return is due 9 months after the decedent's death."},{"id":"ch08-s5","title":"4. Business uses: key person insurance","html":"<h4>Tax considerations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Death benefits are generally excluded from gross income when paid by reason of the insured's death.</li><li class=\"l0\">For employer-owned policies, the exclusion is generally capped at premiums paid unless the employee received written notice and gave consent.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">IRC 101 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Tax considerations Death benefits are generally excluded from gross income when paid by reason of the insured's death. For employer-owned policies, the exclusion is generally capped at premiums paid unless the employee received written notice and gave consent."},{"id":"ch08-s6","title":"5. Buy-sell agreements (business continuation)","html":"<h4>Close corporation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Section 303 redemption plan:</strong> The stock must be more than 35% of the gross estate after the deductions under IRC 2053 and 2054. The redemption can pay estate taxes and funeral and administration expenses.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/303\">IRC 303 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Close corporation Section 303 redemption plan: The stock must be more than 35% of the gross estate after the deductions under IRC 2053 and 2054. The redemption can pay estate taxes and funeral and administration expenses."},{"id":"ch08-s8","title":"7. Corporate-owned life insurance (COLI)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Tax treatment on death:</strong> COLI death proceeds are generally excluded from income. If the employer-owned notice and consent rules are not met, the exclusion is capped at premiums paid.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">IRC 101 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Tax treatment on death: COLI death proceeds are generally excluded from income. If the employer-owned notice and consent rules are not met, the exclusion is capped at premiums paid."},{"id":"ch08-s10","title":"9. Annuity basics","html":"<h4>Key annuity terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1035 exchange</strong></td><td>Exchanges a life insurance or annuity contract for another contract without recognizing gain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exclusion ratio</strong></td><td>Sets the tax-free share of each annuity payment (investment in the contract divided by expected return)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Variable annuities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The FINRA Series 6 license qualifies a candidate to sell variable annuities.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">IRC 1035 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.finra.org/registration-exams-ce/qualification-exams/series6\">FINRA Series 6</a></p>","text":"Key annuity terms Term Meaning 1035 exchange Exchanges a life insurance or annuity contract for another contract without recognizing gain Exclusion ratio Sets the tax-free share of each annuity payment (investment in the contract divided by expected return) Variable annuities The FINRA Series 6 license qualifies a candidate to sell variable annuities."},{"id":"ch08-s12","title":"11. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/303\">IRC 303 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"1. What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan?"},{"id":"ch08-s13","title":"12. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> To let the corporation redeem stock to pay estate taxes and funeral and administration expenses.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/303\">IRC 303 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"1. To let the corporation redeem stock to pay estate taxes and funeral and administration expenses."}]},{"id":"ch09","short":"Ch 9","title":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","label":"Chapter 9","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Life insurance CREATES an estate. An annuity LIQUIDATES one (it turns savings into income). Learn the two phases, fixed vs. variable, immediate vs. deferred, the payout options, the tax rules, and the suitability duties.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch09-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Early withdrawals:</strong> Earnings withdrawn before age 59½ can be subject to a 10% additional tax.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Early withdrawals: Earnings withdrawn before age 59½ can be subject to a 10% additional tax."},{"id":"ch09-s2","title":"1. What an annuity is","html":"<h4>Regulation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Variable annuities:</strong> The FINRA Series 6 license covers the sale of variable annuities.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.finra.org/registration-exams-ce/qualification-exams/series6\">FINRA Series 6</a></p>","text":"Regulation Variable annuities: The FINRA Series 6 license covers the sale of variable annuities."},{"id":"ch09-s13","title":"12. Surrender charges, penalties, and non-forfeiture","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Federal 10% tax penalty</strong></td><td>On the earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½. Exceptions include death, disability, an immediate annuity, and qualified plans</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Item Key facts Federal 10% tax penalty On the earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½. Exceptions include death, disability, an immediate annuity, and qualified plans"},{"id":"ch09-s14","title":"13. Taxation","html":"<h4>During accumulation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Earnings are not taxed until they are received.</li></ul><h4>Withdrawals</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>LIFO</strong> (last in, first out): earnings come out first and are taxed first. Then the principal comes back tax-free.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exception:</strong> Contracts entered into before August 14, 1982 are taxed only to the extent amounts exceed the investment in the contract.</li></ul><h4>At annuitization</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>exclusion ratio</strong> sets the tax-free share of each payment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Formula: <strong>Total investment ÷ expected return.</strong> That percentage of each payment is tax-free. The rest is ordinary income.</li></ul><h4>If the owner dies before annuitization</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The entire interest must be distributed within 5 years. If the beneficiary takes payments over their life, payments must begin within 1 year of death.</li></ul><h4>1035 exchanges (tax-free)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Allowed (no tax)</th><th>NOT allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Annuity → annuity</td><td>Annuity → life insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Life insurance → annuity</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Endowment → annuity</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">IRC 1035 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"During accumulation Earnings are not taxed until they are received. Withdrawals LIFO (last in, first out): earnings come out first and are taxed first. Then the principal comes back tax-free. Exception: Contracts entered into before August 14, 1982 are taxed only to the extent amounts exceed the investment in the contract. At annuitization The exclusion ratio sets the tax-free share of each payment. Formula: Total investment ÷ expected return. That percentage of each payment is tax-free. The rest is ordinary income. If the owner dies before annuitization The entire interest must be distributed within 5 years. If the beneficiary takes payments over their life, payments must begin within 1 year of death. 1035 exchanges (tax-free) Allowed (no tax) NOT allowed Annuity → annuity Annuity → life insurance Life insurance → annuity Endowment → annuity"},{"id":"ch09-s18","title":"17. Suitability","html":"<p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Regulation 89 (Suitability in Annuity Transactions) was amended effective September 20, 2024 to add a best-interest standard. Producers who sell annuities must complete a one-time four (4) hour annuity training course.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/education/annuity-training-requirements\">Louisiana DOI, Annuity Training Requirements</a></p>","text":"Louisiana: Regulation 89 (Suitability in Annuity Transactions) was amended effective September 20, 2024 to add a best-interest standard. Producers who sell annuities must complete a one-time four (4) hour annuity training course."},{"id":"ch09-s21","title":"20. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What does the 10% penalty apply to?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the exclusion ratio formula?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Within what time must payments begin after the owner dies, if the beneficiary takes payments over their life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Can an annuity be exchanged for life insurance tax-free?</p><h4>Scenario question</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario:</strong> Dan, age 45, withdraws $20,000 from a deferred annuity. Half of that is earnings. What extra tax applies, and on what amount?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">IRC 1035 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"1. In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)? 2. What does the 10% penalty apply to? 3. What is the exclusion ratio formula? 4. Within what time must payments begin after the owner dies, if the beneficiary takes payments over their life? 5. Can an annuity be exchanged for life insurance tax-free? Scenario question Scenario: Dan, age 45, withdraws $20,000 from a deferred annuity. Half of that is earnings. What extra tax applies, and on what amount?"},{"id":"ch09-s22","title":"21. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Earnings first (taxable), then principal (tax-free).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> The earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Total investment ÷ expected return.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Within 1 year of death, if the beneficiary takes payments over their life.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> No. Annuity-to-life-insurance exchanges are not allowed.</p><p>Scenario: Half of the $20,000 withdrawal ($10,000) is earnings, so the 10% additional tax is $1,000.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">IRC 72 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">IRC 1035 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"1. Earnings first (taxable), then principal (tax-free). 2. The earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½. 3. Total investment ÷ expected return. 4. Within 1 year of death, if the beneficiary takes payments over their life. 5. No. Annuity-to-life-insurance exchanges are not allowed. Scenario: Half of the $20,000 withdrawal ($10,000) is earnings, so the 10% additional tax is $1,000."}]},{"id":"ch10","short":"Ch 10","title":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","label":"Chapter 10","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Social Security (OASDHI/OASDI) is a federal social insurance program paid for by FICA taxes. Learn the three big ideas: <strong>insured status</strong> (who qualifies), <strong>benefit math</strong> (PIA, FRA, early and delayed claiming), and <strong>survivor and disability rules</strong>. Use the 2026 figures in this chapter.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch10-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fully insured = 40 quarters (10 years). Currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters.</strong> Currently insured gets survivor benefits ONLY.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>PIA</strong> = the full benefit at Full Retirement Age (FRA). <strong>AIME</strong> = the earnings average used to compute PIA.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disability</strong> = TOTAL disability, lasting 12+ months (or resulting in death), with a 5-month waiting period.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/Benefits.html\">SSA: Benefit Amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a></p>","text":"Fully insured = 40 quarters (10 years). Currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters. Currently insured gets survivor benefits ONLY. PIA = the full benefit at Full Retirement Age (FRA). AIME = the earnings average used to compute PIA. Disability = TOTAL disability, lasting 12+ months (or resulting in death), with a 5-month waiting period."},{"id":"ch10-s2","title":"1. What Social Security is","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Official name:</strong> OASDI (Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefits:</strong> Retirement income, disability income, survivor benefits, and a small lump-sum death payment.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html\">SSA: Contribution and Benefit Base</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/amount\">SSA: Survivor benefits</a></p>","text":"Official name: OASDI (Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance). Benefits: Retirement income, disability income, survivor benefits, and a small lump-sum death payment."},{"id":"ch10-s3","title":"2. Funding: FICA taxes","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Who pays</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employee</td><td>7.65% (6.2% Social Security + 1.45% Medicare)</td></tr><tr><td>Employer</td><td>7.65%</td></tr><tr><td>Self-employed</td><td>15.3% (12.4% + 2.9%, both halves)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The Social Security part applies only up to the <strong>taxable maximum</strong>, which changes each year with average wages.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>2026 taxable maximum: $184,500</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare (HI) tax has NO maximum.</strong></li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Self-employed = &quot;double&quot; (15.3%) because you are both the employer and the employee.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html\">SSA: Contribution and Benefit Base</a></p>","text":"Who pays Rate Employee 7.65% (6.2% Social Security + 1.45% Medicare) Employer 7.65% Self-employed 15.3% (12.4% + 2.9%, both halves) The Social Security part applies only up to the taxable maximum , which changes each year with average wages. 2026 taxable maximum: $184,500 . Medicare (HI) tax has NO maximum. Memory trick Self-employed = \"double\" (15.3%) because you are both the employer and the employee."},{"id":"ch10-s4","title":"3. Who is covered?","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Covered does NOT mean eligible.</strong> You must work in covered jobs and earn enough credits for each benefit.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a></p>","text":"Covered does NOT mean eligible. You must work in covered jobs and earn enough credits for each benefit."},{"id":"ch10-s5","title":"4. Insured status (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>FULLY INSURED</th><th>CURRENTLY INSURED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requirement</td><td><strong>40 quarters</strong> (credits), about 10 years of work</td><td><strong>6 of the last 13 quarters</strong>, about 3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Quarters</td><td>Need not be consecutive. Max <strong>4 per year</strong></td><td>Recent work</td></tr><tr><td>Retirement benefits</td><td>YES</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Disability benefits</td><td>YES (if the disability criteria are met)</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Survivor benefits</td><td>YES</td><td>YES (survivor benefits ONLY)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>FULLY = FULL retirement and disability. CURRENTLY = only the family gets death benefits.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/QC.html\">SSA: Quarter of Coverage</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a></p>","text":"FULLY INSURED CURRENTLY INSURED Requirement 40 quarters (credits), about 10 years of work 6 of the last 13 quarters , about 3 years Quarters Need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year Recent work Retirement benefits YES NO Disability benefits YES (if the disability criteria are met) NO Survivor benefits YES YES (survivor benefits ONLY) Memory trick FULLY = FULL retirement and disability. CURRENTLY = only the family gets death benefits."},{"id":"ch10-s6","title":"5. Benefit calculations","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>AIME</strong> (Average Indexed Monthly Earnings)</td><td>Average monthly earnings (up to 35 years, indexed for wage growth). Used to calculate the PIA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PIA</strong> (Primary Insurance Amount)</td><td>The benefit at full retirement age. Other benefits are based on the PIA</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>AIME = the INPUT (earnings). PIA = the OUTPUT (benefit).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/Benefits.html\">SSA: Benefit Amounts</a></p>","text":"Term Meaning AIME (Average Indexed Monthly Earnings) Average monthly earnings (up to 35 years, indexed for wage growth). Used to calculate the PIA PIA (Primary Insurance Amount) The benefit at full retirement age. Other benefits are based on the PIA Memory trick AIME = the INPUT (earnings). PIA = the OUTPUT (benefit)."},{"id":"ch10-s7","title":"6. Retirement benefits","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be fully insured and age 62 or older.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Must APPLY.</strong> Benefits are not automatic.</li></ul><h4>Full retirement age (FRA)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Birth year</th><th>FRA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1937 or earlier</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td>1938 to 1959</td><td>Increases gradually, from 65 and 2 months to 66 and 10 months</td></tr><tr><td>1960 or later</td><td><strong>67</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early and delayed claiming</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claim at 62:</strong> For FRA 67 (born 1960 or later), the benefit is about <strong>30% less than the PIA</strong>, so about <strong>70% of PIA</strong>. The reduction is permanent.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Delayed retirement credits:</strong> Benefits grow up to 8% a year after FRA. No credit is given after age 69, so benefits stop growing at 70.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/progdata/nra.html\">SSA: Normal Retirement Age</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/Benefits.html\">SSA: Benefit Amounts</a></p>","text":"Must be fully insured and age 62 or older. Must APPLY. Benefits are not automatic. Full retirement age (FRA) Birth year FRA 1937 or earlier 65 1938 to 1959 Increases gradually, from 65 and 2 months to 66 and 10 months 1960 or later 67 Early and delayed claiming Claim at 62: For FRA 67 (born 1960 or later), the benefit is about 30% less than the PIA , so about 70% of PIA . The reduction is permanent. Delayed retirement credits: Benefits grow up to 8% a year after FRA. No credit is given after age 69, so benefits stop growing at 70."},{"id":"ch10-s8","title":"7. Earnings test (retirement)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation in 2026</th><th>Rule</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under FRA for the whole year</td><td>$1 withheld for every $2 earned above the limit</td><td><strong>$24,480</strong> a year</td></tr><tr><td>Reaching FRA in 2026</td><td>$1 withheld for every $3 above the limit. Only earnings BEFORE the FRA month count</td><td><strong>$65,160</strong> a year</td></tr><tr><td>From the FRA month onward</td><td><strong>No earnings limit</strong></td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The year you reach FRA uses a higher limit and a $1-for-$3 rule, and counts only months before FRA.</li><li class=\"l0\">Withheld benefits are not lost. Your monthly benefit is increased permanently once you reach FRA.</li></ul><h4>Worked example (2026 limit)</h4><p>Jane is 63 and gets a $1,200 monthly benefit. She earns $25,000 in 2026.</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Limit: $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $25,000 − $24,480 = $520.</li><li class=\"l0\">Reduction: $520 ÷ 2 = $260 for the year.</li><li class=\"l0\">Annual benefit without reduction: $14,400. After reduction: $14,400 − $260 = $14,140.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html\">SSA: Earnings test exempt amounts</a></p>","text":"Situation in 2026 Rule Limit Under FRA for the whole year $1 withheld for every $2 earned above the limit $24,480 a year Reaching FRA in 2026 $1 withheld for every $3 above the limit. Only earnings BEFORE the FRA month count $65,160 a year From the FRA month onward No earnings limit None The year you reach FRA uses a higher limit and a $1-for-$3 rule, and counts only months before FRA. Withheld benefits are not lost. Your monthly benefit is increased permanently once you reach FRA. Worked example (2026 limit) Jane is 63 and gets a $1,200 monthly benefit. She earns $25,000 in 2026. Limit: $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $25,000 − $24,480 = $520. Reduction: $520 ÷ 2 = $260 for the year. Annual benefit without reduction: $14,400. After reduction: $14,400 − $260 = $14,140."},{"id":"ch10-s9","title":"8. Survivor and death benefits","html":"<h4>Lump-sum death benefit</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>$255</strong>, paid once.</li><li class=\"l0\">Paid to a spouse or some minor children.</li><li class=\"l0\">Requires a fully or currently insured deceased worker.</li></ul><h4>Monthly survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Surviving spouse with no children:</strong> Can start at <strong>age 60</strong> (age 50 if disabled). Starts at about 71.5% of the worker's benefit and rises to 100% at full retirement age for survivors.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spouse of any age caring for the worker's child under 16</strong> (or a disabled child): can receive benefits.</li></ul><h4>Who can receive survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Spouse (see above).</li><li class=\"l0\">Unmarried children under 18 (or 18 to 19 if in school full time through grade 12).</li><li class=\"l0\">Adult children disabled before age 22.</li><li class=\"l0\">Parents age 62 or older who got at least half their support from the worker.</li></ul><h4>Benefit amounts</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Recipient</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Child</td><td>75% of the worker's PIA</td></tr><tr><td>Parent (one dependent parent)</td><td>82.5% of PIA</td></tr><tr><td>Each of two dependent parents</td><td>75% of PIA each</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/amount\">SSA: Survivor benefit amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/eligibility\">SSA: Who can get Survivor benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a></p>","text":"Lump-sum death benefit $255 , paid once. Paid to a spouse or some minor children. Requires a fully or currently insured deceased worker. Monthly survivor benefits Surviving spouse with no children: Can start at age 60 (age 50 if disabled). Starts at about 71.5% of the worker's benefit and rises to 100% at full retirement age for survivors. Spouse of any age caring for the worker's child under 16 (or a disabled child): can receive benefits. Who can receive survivor benefits Spouse (see above). Unmarried children under 18 (or 18 to 19 if in school full time through grade 12). Adult children disabled before age 22. Parents age 62 or older who got at least half their support from the worker. Benefit amounts Recipient Amount Child 75% of the worker's PIA Parent (one dependent parent) 82.5% of PIA Each of two dependent parents 75% of PIA each"},{"id":"ch10-s10","title":"9. Blackout period","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> A time when a surviving spouse receives NO Social Security benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Starts:</strong> When the youngest child turns <strong>16</strong> (the spouse's caring-for-a-child benefit ends), OR immediately if there are no children.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Ends:</strong> When the surviving spouse reaches <strong>age 60</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">The children's own benefits continue until 18 (or 19 if in school).</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Blackout starts at 16 (spouse loses benefits), not 18. Children's benefits run to 18.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/eligibility\">SSA: Who can get Survivor benefits</a></p>","text":"Definition: A time when a surviving spouse receives NO Social Security benefits. Starts: When the youngest child turns 16 (the spouse's caring-for-a-child benefit ends), OR immediately if there are no children. Ends: When the surviving spouse reaches age 60 . The children's own benefits continue until 18 (or 19 if in school). Memory trick Blackout starts at 16 (spouse loses benefits), not 18. Children's benefits run to 18."},{"id":"ch10-s11","title":"10. Maximum family benefit","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A cap on the total benefits payable to one family on a worker's record.</li><li class=\"l0\">Calculated from the worker's PIA.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/familymax.html\">SSA: Family Maximum Benefit</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/amount\">SSA: Survivor benefit amounts</a></p>","text":"A cap on the total benefits payable to one family on a worker's record. Calculated from the worker's PIA."},{"id":"ch10-s12","title":"11. Dual benefit rule","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A person eligible for survivor benefits and another benefit chooses the better payment. The two are not added together.</li><li class=\"l0\">Example: a surviving spouse can take their own retirement or the survivor benefit, not both at once.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/amount\">SSA: Survivor benefit amounts</a></p>","text":"A person eligible for survivor benefits and another benefit chooses the better payment. The two are not added together. Example: a surviving spouse can take their own retirement or the survivor benefit, not both at once."},{"id":"ch10-s13","title":"12. Disability benefits","html":"<h4>Two federal programs</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Based on</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SSDI</strong> (Social Security Disability Insurance)</td><td>Work credits</td><td>Must have worked in jobs covered by Social Security</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSI</strong> (Supplemental Security Income)</td><td>Financial need</td><td>Little or no income and resources, and a disability, blindness, or age 65+</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>SSDI eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Work test (the 20/40 rule): generally <strong>40 credits, 20 of them in the last 10 years</strong>. Younger workers may qualify with fewer.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must meet SSA's strict definition of disability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>5-month waiting period.</strong> The first benefit is paid in the 6th full month after the disability began.</li></ul><h4>Definition of disability (SSA)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>TOTAL disability:</strong> Cannot do substantial gainful activity (SGA), cannot do past work, and cannot adjust to other work.</li><li class=\"l0\">Condition has lasted or is expected to last <strong>at least 12 months</strong>, or to result in death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No partial disability and no short-term disability benefits.</strong></li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>2026 SGA:</strong> <strong>$1,690 a month</strong> (non-blind); <strong>$2,830 a month</strong> (blind).</p></div><h4>Important limits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">At full retirement age, SSDI converts automatically to retirement benefits in the same amount.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/ssi\">SSA: SSI</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/sga.html\">SSA: SGA amounts</a></p>","text":"Two federal programs Program Based on Key facts SSDI (Social Security Disability Insurance) Work credits Must have worked in jobs covered by Social Security SSI (Supplemental Security Income) Financial need Little or no income and resources, and a disability, blindness, or age 65+ SSDI eligibility Work test (the 20/40 rule): generally 40 credits, 20 of them in the last 10 years . Younger workers may qualify with fewer. Must meet SSA's strict definition of disability. 5-month waiting period. The first benefit is paid in the 6th full month after the disability began. Definition of disability (SSA) TOTAL disability: Cannot do substantial gainful activity (SGA), cannot do past work, and cannot adjust to other work. Condition has lasted or is expected to last at least 12 months , or to result in death. No partial disability and no short-term disability benefits. 2026 SGA: $1,690 a month (non-blind); $2,830 a month (blind). Important limits At full retirement age, SSDI converts automatically to retirement benefits in the same amount."},{"id":"ch10-s14","title":"13. Medicare (health insurance piece)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Part A</strong> (Hospital Insurance)</td><td>Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility, hospice, home health</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part B</strong> (Medical Insurance)</td><td>Doctor services, outpatient care, preventive services, medical equipment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part C</strong> (Medicare Advantage)</td><td>Private plans approved by Medicare, an alternative to Original Medicare (Parts A and B)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part D</strong> (Prescription drug plans)</td><td>Prescription drugs through private plans</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> Age 65+, or younger with a disability, ESRD, or ALS.</li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare's Hospital Insurance tax (1.45% each for employee and employer) has no earnings cap.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/medicare-basics/parts-of-medicare\">Medicare.gov: Parts of Medicare</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare\">Medicare.gov: Get started</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html\">SSA: Contribution and Benefit Base</a></p>","text":"Part What it covers Part A (Hospital Insurance) Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility, hospice, home health Part B (Medical Insurance) Doctor services, outpatient care, preventive services, medical equipment Part C (Medicare Advantage) Private plans approved by Medicare, an alternative to Original Medicare (Parts A and B) Part D (Prescription drug plans) Prescription drugs through private plans Eligibility: Age 65+, or younger with a disability, ESRD, or ALS. Medicare's Hospital Insurance tax (1.45% each for employee and employer) has no earnings cap."},{"id":"ch10-s15","title":"14. Taxation of Social Security benefits","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Filing status</th><th>Combined income threshold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual</td><td>Over $25,000: part of the benefit may be taxable</td></tr><tr><td>Joint</td><td>Over $32,000: part of the benefit may be taxable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Combined income = 50% of benefits plus other income.</li><li class=\"l0\">Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable at higher income levels.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/manage-benefits/request-withhold-taxes\">SSA: Taxes on benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p915\">IRS Publication 915</a></p>","text":"Filing status Combined income threshold Individual Over $25,000: part of the benefit may be taxable Joint Over $32,000: part of the benefit may be taxable Combined income = 50% of benefits plus other income. Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable at higher income levels."},{"id":"ch10-s16","title":"15. 2026 figures reference","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>2026 figure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>COLA (December 2025 increase, paid in 2026)</td><td>2.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Taxable maximum (Social Security tax)</td><td>$184,500</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test, under FRA all year</td><td>$24,480 a year ($1 for $2)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test, year you reach FRA</td><td>$65,160 a year ($1 for $3, months before FRA only)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI SGA (non-blind)</td><td>$1,690 a month</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI SGA (blind)</td><td>$2,830 a month</td></tr><tr><td>SSI federal benefit rate (individual)</td><td>$994 a month</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/colaseries.html\">SSA: COLA history</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html\">SSA: Contribution and Benefit Base</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html\">SSA: Earnings test exempt amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/sga.html\">SSA: SGA amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/SSI.html\">SSA: SSI payment amounts</a></p>","text":"Item 2026 figure COLA (December 2025 increase, paid in 2026) 2.8% Taxable maximum (Social Security tax) $184,500 Earnings test, under FRA all year $24,480 a year ($1 for $2) Earnings test, year you reach FRA $65,160 a year ($1 for $3, months before FRA only) SSDI SGA (non-blind) $1,690 a month SSDI SGA (blind) $2,830 a month SSI federal benefit rate (individual) $994 a month"},{"id":"ch10-s17","title":"16. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fully vs. currently insured</td><td>40 quarters (all benefits) vs. 6 of last 13 quarters (survivor only)</td></tr><tr><td>AIME vs. PIA</td><td>AIME = average earnings (input). PIA = benefit at FRA (output)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI vs. SSI</td><td>SSDI = work credits. SSI = financial need</td></tr><tr><td>Part A vs. Part B</td><td>Hospital insurance vs. medical insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Part C vs. Part D</td><td>Medicare Advantage (alternative to Original Medicare) vs. prescription drug coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Early vs. full vs. delayed</td><td>Reduced permanently before FRA, full at FRA, increased after FRA (to age 70)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test before vs. after FRA</td><td>Limit and $1-for-$2 rule before FRA. $1-for-$3 rule in the year of FRA. No limit after FRA</td></tr><tr><td>Blackout vs. child benefit</td><td>Spouse loses benefits from child's 16th birthday to spouse's 60th. Child benefits run to 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html\">SSA: Earnings test exempt amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Fully vs. currently insured 40 quarters (all benefits) vs. 6 of last 13 quarters (survivor only) AIME vs. PIA AIME = average earnings (input). PIA = benefit at FRA (output) SSDI vs. SSI SSDI = work credits. SSI = financial need Part A vs. Part B Hospital insurance vs. medical insurance Part C vs. Part D Medicare Advantage (alternative to Original Medicare) vs. prescription drug coverage Early vs. full vs. delayed Reduced permanently before FRA, full at FRA, increased after FRA (to age 70) Earnings test before vs. after FRA Limit and $1-for-$2 rule before FRA. $1-for-$3 rule in the year of FRA. No limit after FRA Blackout vs. child benefit Spouse loses benefits from child's 16th birthday to spouse's 60th. Child benefits run to 18"},{"id":"ch10-s18","title":"17. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How many quarters are needed to be fully insured?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What does a currently insured person receive?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the difference between AIME and PIA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What is the lump-sum death benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When does the blackout period start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Can a person collect both their own retirement and a survivor benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the SSA's definition of total disability?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the waiting period for SSDI?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Which Medicare part is for medical (outpatient) care?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Priya is 40 and has worked 30 quarters. Is she fully insured? Currently insured?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> A widow has a child who is 15. She is 45 and not working. Are her benefits paid now? When does the blackout end?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html\">SSA: Earnings test exempt amounts</a></p>","text":"1. How many quarters are needed to be fully insured? 2. What does a currently insured person receive? 3. What is the difference between AIME and PIA? 4. At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits? 5. What is the lump-sum death benefit? 6. When does the blackout period start? 7. Can a person collect both their own retirement and a survivor benefit? 8. What is the SSA's definition of total disability? 9. What is the waiting period for SSDI? 10. Which Medicare part is for medical (outpatient) care? Scenario questions Scenario A: Priya is 40 and has worked 30 quarters. Is she fully insured? Currently insured? Scenario B: A widow has a child who is 15. She is 45 and not working. Are her benefits paid now? When does the blackout end? Scenario C: Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?"},{"id":"ch10-s19","title":"18. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 40 quarters (about 10 years). Quarters need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Survivor benefits only.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> AIME is the average indexed monthly earnings used as input. PIA is the benefit at FRA.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Age 60.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> $255.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When the youngest child turns 16 (or immediately if no children).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> No. The person chooses the better payment. The two are not added together.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Inability to do substantial gainful activity because of a medical condition expected to last at least 12 months or to result in death.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> 5 months. The first benefit is paid in the 6th full month.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Part B.</p><p>Scenario A: She has 30 quarters, which is fewer than 40, so she is NOT fully insured. She is currently insured only if she has 6 quarters in the last 13 quarters.</p><p>Scenario B: Yes, she receives benefits now, because she is caring for a child under 16. The blackout starts when the child turns 16 (no benefits for her then, although the child's benefits continue to 18). The blackout ends when she reaches age 60.</p><p>Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 10:</strong> Make a single card with these numbers: 40 quarters, 6 of 13, 5-month wait, 12 months, $255, 75%, 82.5%, age 60, age 16, FRA 67. Recite them out loud each day.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0214.htm\">Social Security Act s.214</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/ssact/title02/0202.htm\">Social Security Act s.202</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/survivor/amount\">SSA: Survivor benefit amounts</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA: Disability eligibility</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html\">SSA: Earnings test exempt amounts</a></p>","text":"1. 40 quarters (about 10 years). Quarters need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year. 2. Survivor benefits only. 3. AIME is the average indexed monthly earnings used as input. PIA is the benefit at FRA. 4. Age 60. 5. $255. 6. When the youngest child turns 16 (or immediately if no children). 7. No. The person chooses the better payment. The two are not added together. 8. Inability to do substantial gainful activity because of a medical condition expected to last at least 12 months or to result in death. 9. 5 months. The first benefit is paid in the 6th full month. 10. Part B. Scenario A: She has 30 quarters, which is fewer than 40, so she is NOT fully insured. She is currently insured only if she has 6 quarters in the last 13 quarters. Scenario B: Yes, she receives benefits now, because she is caring for a child under 16. The blackout starts when the child turns 16 (no benefits for her then, although the child's benefits continue to 18). The blackout ends when she reaches age 60. Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year. Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 10: Make a single card with these numbers: 40 quarters, 6 of 13, 5-month wait, 12 months, $255, 75%, 82.5%, age 60, age 16, FRA 67. Recite them out loud each day."}]},{"id":"ch11","short":"Ch 11","title":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","label":"Chapter 11","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Retirement plans are sorted by three questions: (1) <strong>Qualified or nonqualified?</strong> (2) <strong>Defined contribution or defined benefit?</strong> (3) <strong>Who is the employer?</strong> Then learn the IRA family (Traditional vs. Roth vs. rollover) and the small-business plans (SEP, SIMPLE, Keogh).</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> Limits, age rules, and inherited-IRA rules changed with the SECURE Act (2019) and SECURE 2.0 (2022). This chapter uses the current rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch11-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Qualified</strong> = meets IRS and ERISA rules, gets tax breaks. <strong>Nonqualified</strong> = does not have to meet qualified-plan rules.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Defined contribution</strong> = the CONTRIBUTION is set. <strong>Defined benefit</strong> = the BENEFIT is set (like a pension).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Traditional IRA</strong> = tax-deferred, RMDs later. <strong>Roth IRA</strong> = after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, NO RMDs for the owner.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Direct rollover = no withholding. Indirect rollover from a plan = 20% withheld.</strong></li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\">IRS Publication 590-B</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS: Rollovers</a></p>","text":"Qualified = meets IRS and ERISA rules, gets tax breaks. Nonqualified = does not have to meet qualified-plan rules. Defined contribution = the CONTRIBUTION is set. Defined benefit = the BENEFIT is set (like a pension). Traditional IRA = tax-deferred, RMDs later. Roth IRA = after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, NO RMDs for the owner. Direct rollover = no withholding. Indirect rollover from a plan = 20% withheld."},{"id":"ch11-s2","title":"1. Qualified vs. nonqualified plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>QUALIFIED</th><th>NONQUALIFIED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rules</td><td>Meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA</td><td>Does not meet the qualified-plan requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>Deferred money is generally not taxed until distributed</td><td>No qualified-plan tax treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Discrimination</td><td>Cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees</td><td>Not bound by that qualified-plan rule</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>401(k), 403(b), pension, profit-sharing</td><td>Executive deferred compensation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/401\">26 U.S.C. 401 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/choosing-a-retirement-plan-401k-plan\">IRS: 401(k) plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a></p>","text":"QUALIFIED NONQUALIFIED Rules Meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA Does not meet the qualified-plan requirements Tax Deferred money is generally not taxed until distributed No qualified-plan tax treatment Discrimination Cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees Not bound by that qualified-plan rule Examples 401(k), 403(b), pension, profit-sharing Executive deferred compensation"},{"id":"ch11-s3","title":"2. ERISA (1974)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974:</strong> Federal law setting minimum standards for most voluntary retirement and health plans in private industry.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key rules:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Participation:</strong> Age 21 with 1 year of service. If the plan gives 100% vesting after no more than 2 years, it may require 2 years of service.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Also covers:</strong> vesting, benefit accrual, funding, fiduciary duties, and claims procedures.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Reporting:</strong> Annual <strong>Form 5500</strong>.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Summary plan description:</strong> Given to each participant within 90 days after becoming a participant.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Not covered by ERISA:</strong> governmental plans and church plans.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/retirement/erisa\">DOL: ERISA</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1052\">29 U.S.C. 1052 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1024\">29 U.S.C. 1024 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/employers-and-advisers/plan-administration-and-compliance/reporting-and-filing/form-5500\">DOL: Form 5500</a></p>","text":"Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974: Federal law setting minimum standards for most voluntary retirement and health plans in private industry. Key rules: Participation: Age 21 with 1 year of service. If the plan gives 100% vesting after no more than 2 years, it may require 2 years of service. Also covers: vesting, benefit accrual, funding, fiduciary duties, and claims procedures. Reporting: Annual Form 5500 . Summary plan description: Given to each participant within 90 days after becoming a participant. Not covered by ERISA: governmental plans and church plans."},{"id":"ch11-s4","title":"3. Key ERISA concepts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vesting</strong></td><td>Employee's right to the retirement funds. Employer contributions may vest on a graduated schedule</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alienation of benefits</strong></td><td>Plan benefits may not be assigned or alienated. Exception: qualified domestic relations orders (QDROs)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Top-heavy plan</strong></td><td>More than <strong>60%</strong> of benefits belong to key employees, measured on the last day of the prior plan year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Summary plan description</strong></td><td>Given to participants within 90 days after they become participants</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/401\">26 U.S.C. 401 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/416\">26 U.S.C. 416 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1024\">29 U.S.C. 1024 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/choosing-a-retirement-plan-401k-plan\">IRS: 401(k) plan</a></p>","text":"Concept Meaning Vesting Employee's right to the retirement funds. Employer contributions may vest on a graduated schedule Alienation of benefits Plan benefits may not be assigned or alienated. Exception: qualified domestic relations orders (QDROs) Top-heavy plan More than 60% of benefits belong to key employees, measured on the last day of the prior plan year Summary plan description Given to participants within 90 days after they become participants"},{"id":"ch11-s5","title":"4. Two main plan categories (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>DEFINED CONTRIBUTION</th><th>DEFINED BENEFIT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What is defined</td><td>The CONTRIBUTIONS</td><td>The BENEFIT at retirement</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Profit-sharing, money purchase, 401(k)</td><td>Traditional pension</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>DC = Dollars Contributed (the account is what's left). DB = Dollars Benefit (the pension is promised).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/choosing-a-retirement-plan-401k-plan\">IRS: 401(k) plan</a></p>","text":"DEFINED CONTRIBUTION DEFINED BENEFIT What is defined The CONTRIBUTIONS The BENEFIT at retirement Examples Profit-sharing, money purchase, 401(k) Traditional pension Memory trick DC = Dollars Contributed (the account is what's left). DB = Dollars Benefit (the pension is promised)."},{"id":"ch11-s6","title":"5. Specific plan types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>401(k)</strong></td><td>Employees choose to defer part of their salary. Deferred money is generally not taxed until distributed. <strong>2026 employee limit: $24,500</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>403(b)</strong> (tax-sheltered annuity)</td><td>Sponsored by public schools, colleges, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations. Ministers can participate. Deferred salary is generally not taxed until distributed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Profit-sharing</strong></td><td>Defined contribution qualified plan. Early withdrawal before 59½: 10% additional tax plus income tax, unless an exception applies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Money purchase</strong></td><td>Defined contribution qualified plan</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500\">IRS: 2026 retirement limits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/choosing-a-retirement-plan-401k-plan\">IRS: 401(k) plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/irc-403b-tax-sheltered-annuity-plans\">IRS: 403(b) plans</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-exceptions-to-tax-on-early-distributions\">IRS: Early distribution exceptions</a></p>","text":"Plan Key facts 401(k) Employees choose to defer part of their salary. Deferred money is generally not taxed until distributed. 2026 employee limit: $24,500 403(b) (tax-sheltered annuity) Sponsored by public schools, colleges, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations. Ministers can participate. Deferred salary is generally not taxed until distributed Profit-sharing Defined contribution qualified plan. Early withdrawal before 59½: 10% additional tax plus income tax, unless an exception applies Money purchase Defined contribution qualified plan"},{"id":"ch11-s7","title":"6. Plans for small employers","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Keogh (HR-10)</strong></td><td>Qualified plan covering self-employed individuals. Can be defined contribution or defined benefit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SEP</strong> (simplified employee pension)</td><td>Only the employer contributes, to employees' traditional IRAs. Up to 25% of pay, same rate for all eligible employees. Contributions are always 100% vested</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SARSEP</strong> (salary reduction SEP)</td><td>A SEP set up before 1997 with a salary reduction feature. No new SARSEPs after 1996</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SIMPLE</strong> (Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees)</td><td>For employers with 100 or fewer employees who earned at least $5,000. Employer cannot have another retirement plan. SIMPLE IRA or SIMPLE 401(k). Contributions <strong>always 100% vested</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>SIMPLE catch-up (age 50+):</strong> $4,000 for 2026.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (small-business plans):</strong> SEP = Simple Employer Pension. SARSEP = &quot;Salary&quot; + &quot;pre-1997.&quot; SIMPLE = 100 or fewer employees, instant 100% vesting.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-sponsor/simplified-employee-pension-plan-sep\">IRS: SEP plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-sponsor/simple-ira-plan\">IRS: SIMPLE IRA plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500\">IRS: 2026 retirement limits</a></p>","text":"Plan Key facts Keogh (HR-10) Qualified plan covering self-employed individuals. Can be defined contribution or defined benefit SEP (simplified employee pension) Only the employer contributes, to employees' traditional IRAs. Up to 25% of pay, same rate for all eligible employees. Contributions are always 100% vested SARSEP (salary reduction SEP) A SEP set up before 1997 with a salary reduction feature. No new SARSEPs after 1996 SIMPLE (Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees) For employers with 100 or fewer employees who earned at least $5,000. Employer cannot have another retirement plan. SIMPLE IRA or SIMPLE 401(k). Contributions always 100% vested SIMPLE catch-up (age 50+): $4,000 for 2026. Memory trick Memory trick (small-business plans): SEP = Simple Employer Pension. SARSEP = \"Salary\" + \"pre-1997.\" SIMPLE = 100 or fewer employees, instant 100% vesting."},{"id":"ch11-s8","title":"7. Traditional IRA","html":"<h4>Eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Anyone with taxable compensation. No age limit for contributions (tax years after 2019).</li><li class=\"l0\">Contribution: up to the annual limit or your taxable compensation, whichever is less.</li><li class=\"l0\">A spouse with little or no pay can also contribute on a joint return (<strong>spousal IRA</strong>).</li><li class=\"l0\">IRA money cannot buy a life insurance policy.</li></ul><h4>Tax treatment</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Amounts are generally taxed only when withdrawn.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Deductibility:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">Neither spouse covered by a workplace plan: deductible up to the limit.</li><li class=\"l1\">Covered by a workplace plan: deduction phases out at higher incomes.</li></ul><h4>Required minimum distributions (RMDs)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>RMD age:</strong> <strong>73</strong> for people born 1951 to 1958, and <strong>75</strong> for people born 1960 or later.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>RMD penalty:</strong> <strong>25%</strong> of the amount not withdrawn, or <strong>10%</strong> if corrected within two years.</li></ul><h4>Early withdrawals (before 59½)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Subject to income tax PLUS a <strong>10% additional tax</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Exceptions include: death; disability; unreimbursed medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI; higher education; first-time home purchase (up to <strong>$10,000</strong>); health insurance premiums while unemployed.</li></ul><h4>Distributions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">With nondeductible contributions, not all of a distribution is taxable.</li></ul><h4>Inherited IRAs (after death)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">For owners who die after 2019, most beneficiaries must empty the inherited IRA within <strong>10 years</strong>. Exceptions: surviving spouse, minor child of the owner, disabled or chronically ill person, and a person not more than 10 years younger than the owner.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a\">IRS Publication 590-A</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\">IRS Publication 590-B</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs\">IRS: RMD FAQs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/section-1.401(a)(9)-2\">26 CFR 1.401(a)(9)-2 (eCFR)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-exceptions-to-tax-on-early-distributions\">IRS: Early distribution exceptions</a></p>","text":"Eligibility Anyone with taxable compensation. No age limit for contributions (tax years after 2019). Contribution: up to the annual limit or your taxable compensation, whichever is less. A spouse with little or no pay can also contribute on a joint return ( spousal IRA ). IRA money cannot buy a life insurance policy. Tax treatment Amounts are generally taxed only when withdrawn. Deductibility: Neither spouse covered by a workplace plan: deductible up to the limit. Covered by a workplace plan: deduction phases out at higher incomes. Required minimum distributions (RMDs) RMD age: 73 for people born 1951 to 1958, and 75 for people born 1960 or later. RMD penalty: 25% of the amount not withdrawn, or 10% if corrected within two years. Early withdrawals (before 59½) Subject to income tax PLUS a 10% additional tax . Exceptions include: death; disability; unreimbursed medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI; higher education; first-time home purchase (up to $10,000 ); health insurance premiums while unemployed. Distributions With nondeductible contributions, not all of a distribution is taxable. Inherited IRAs (after death) For owners who die after 2019, most beneficiaries must empty the inherited IRA within 10 years . Exceptions: surviving spouse, minor child of the owner, disabled or chronically ill person, and a person not more than 10 years younger than the owner."},{"id":"ch11-s9","title":"8. Roth IRA","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Contributions are made with AFTER-TAX dollars. Not deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Qualified withdrawals are tax-free.</strong> No age limit for contributions.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NO required minimum distributions</strong> while the owner is alive.</li><li class=\"l0\">Income limits can reduce or eliminate the allowed contribution.</li></ul><h4>Qualified withdrawal (tax-free)</h4><p>Must meet BOTH:</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> It is made after the <strong>5-year period</strong> that starts with the first tax year a contribution was made, AND</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> One qualifying event: age <strong>59½</strong>, death, disability, or a first home purchase (<strong>$10,000</strong> lifetime limit).</p><h4>Nonqualified withdrawals</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Contributions come out first. The earnings part may be taxable, and the 10% additional tax can apply to the taxable part.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\">IRS Publication 590-B</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a\">IRS Publication 590-A</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/roth-iras\">IRS: Roth IRAs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500\">IRS: 2026 retirement limits</a></p>","text":"Contributions are made with AFTER-TAX dollars. Not deductible. Qualified withdrawals are tax-free. No age limit for contributions. NO required minimum distributions while the owner is alive. Income limits can reduce or eliminate the allowed contribution. Qualified withdrawal (tax-free) Must meet BOTH: 1. It is made after the 5-year period that starts with the first tax year a contribution was made, AND 2. One qualifying event: age 59½ , death, disability, or a first home purchase ( $10,000 lifetime limit). Nonqualified withdrawals Contributions come out first. The earnings part may be taxable, and the 10% additional tax can apply to the taxable part."},{"id":"ch11-s10","title":"9. Rollover IRA","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">You can roll over all or part of an eligible distribution to another plan or IRA.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>60-day rule:</strong> A rollover must be completed within 60 days of receiving the distribution (the IRS may waive it in some cases).</li></ul><h4>The 20% withholding rule (CRITICAL)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Direct rollover</strong> (trustee to trustee)</td><td>No withholding</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Indirect rollover</strong> (paid to the owner)</td><td>20% is withheld from a retirement plan distribution. To roll over the full amount, the owner must replace the 20% from other money. Otherwise the part not rolled over may be taxable, plus a 10% additional tax before 59½</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Distributions paid to you from an IRA have 10% withheld by default.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-spouse beneficiaries:</strong> See the inherited IRA rules above (generally 10 years).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS: Rollovers</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs\">IRS: RMD FAQs</a></p>","text":"You can roll over all or part of an eligible distribution to another plan or IRA. 60-day rule: A rollover must be completed within 60 days of receiving the distribution (the IRS may waive it in some cases). The 20% withholding rule (CRITICAL) Method Result Direct rollover (trustee to trustee) No withholding Indirect rollover (paid to the owner) 20% is withheld from a retirement plan distribution. To roll over the full amount, the owner must replace the 20% from other money. Otherwise the part not rolled over may be taxable, plus a 10% additional tax before 59½ Distributions paid to you from an IRA have 10% withheld by default. Non-spouse beneficiaries: See the inherited IRA rules above (generally 10 years)."},{"id":"ch11-s11","title":"10. Other IRA types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Spousal IRA</strong></td><td>On a joint return, a spouse with little or no pay can contribute, based on the couple's combined compensation. The usual annual limit still applies to each spouse</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a\">IRS Publication 590-A</a></p>","text":"Type Key facts Spousal IRA On a joint return, a spouse with little or no pay can contribute, based on the couple's combined compensation. The usual annual limit still applies to each spouse"},{"id":"ch11-s12","title":"11. Pension Protection Act (2006)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Includes rules on investment advice for plan participants and beneficiaries.</li><li class=\"l0\">Includes automatic contribution arrangements (QACAs and EACAs).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/laws-and-regulations/laws/pension-protection-act\">DOL: Pension Protection Act</a></p>","text":"Includes rules on investment advice for plan participants and beneficiaries. Includes automatic contribution arrangements (QACAs and EACAs)."},{"id":"ch11-s13","title":"12. Education savings plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Coverdell Education Savings Account</strong></td><td>Nondeductible cash contributions, total up to <strong>$2,000 per beneficiary</strong> a year. Beneficiary must be under 18 (or special needs) when the account is set up. Covers higher education and elementary/secondary education. Leftover funds must be distributed within 30 days after the beneficiary turns 30, unless transferred to a family member</td></tr><tr><td><strong>529 plans</strong></td><td>Qualified tuition programs run by a state or educational institution. Created by Congress in 1996. Two types: <strong>savings plans</strong> and <strong>prepaid tuition</strong> (only states offer prepaid). Earnings are not federally taxed when used for qualified education expenses. The account owner controls the funds and can change the beneficiary to another family member</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc310\">IRS: Topic 310 Coverdell ESAs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/529-plans-questions-and-answers\">IRS: 529 Plans Q&amp;A</a></p>","text":"Plan Key facts Coverdell Education Savings Account Nondeductible cash contributions, total up to $2,000 per beneficiary a year. Beneficiary must be under 18 (or special needs) when the account is set up. Covers higher education and elementary/secondary education. Leftover funds must be distributed within 30 days after the beneficiary turns 30, unless transferred to a family member 529 plans Qualified tuition programs run by a state or educational institution. Created by Congress in 1996. Two types: savings plans and prepaid tuition (only states offer prepaid). Earnings are not federally taxed when used for qualified education expenses. The account owner controls the funds and can change the beneficiary to another family member"},{"id":"ch11-s14","title":"13. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qualified vs. nonqualified</td><td>Tax-favored and ERISA-governed vs. not subject to qualified-plan rules</td></tr><tr><td>Defined contribution vs. defined benefit</td><td>Contributions are defined vs. benefit is defined</td></tr><tr><td>401(k) vs. 403(b)</td><td>Employee salary deferrals in general vs. public schools, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations</td></tr><tr><td>SARSEP vs. SIMPLE</td><td>Pre-1997 SEP with salary reduction vs. 100 or fewer employees, 100% vesting</td></tr><tr><td>Traditional vs. Roth IRA</td><td>Deductible/tax-deferred, RMDs vs. after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, no RMDs for the owner</td></tr><tr><td>Direct vs. indirect rollover</td><td>No withholding vs. 20% withheld from a plan distribution</td></tr><tr><td>Coverdell vs. 529</td><td>$2,000 a year per beneficiary vs. state or institution plan</td></tr><tr><td>Spouse vs. non-spouse beneficiary</td><td>Spouse is an exception to the 10-year rule vs. non-spouse generally must empty the account within 10 years</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/irc-403b-tax-sheltered-annuity-plans\">IRS: 403(b) plans</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS: Rollovers</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs\">IRS: RMD FAQs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc310\">IRS: Topic 310 Coverdell ESAs</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Qualified vs. nonqualified Tax-favored and ERISA-governed vs. not subject to qualified-plan rules Defined contribution vs. defined benefit Contributions are defined vs. benefit is defined 401(k) vs. 403(b) Employee salary deferrals in general vs. public schools, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations SARSEP vs. SIMPLE Pre-1997 SEP with salary reduction vs. 100 or fewer employees, 100% vesting Traditional vs. Roth IRA Deductible/tax-deferred, RMDs vs. after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, no RMDs for the owner Direct vs. indirect rollover No withholding vs. 20% withheld from a plan distribution Coverdell vs. 529 $2,000 a year per beneficiary vs. state or institution plan Spouse vs. non-spouse beneficiary Spouse is an exception to the 10-year rule vs. non-spouse generally must empty the account within 10 years"},{"id":"ch11-s15","title":"14. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What makes a plan qualified?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Do qualified plans have to follow a nondiscrimination rule?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Which plans are for nonprofits and public school employees?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the difference between defined contribution and defined benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Which ERISA form is filed each year?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Which small-employer plan has 100 or fewer employees and always 100% vested contributions?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> A 45-year-old employee of a for-profit company asks whether she can use a 403(b) plan. What is the answer?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A self-employed consultant with no employees wants a plan with simple administration. Which plan fits?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560\">IRS Publication 560</a></p>","text":"1. What makes a plan qualified? 2. Do qualified plans have to follow a nondiscrimination rule? 3. Which plans are for nonprofits and public school employees? 4. What is the difference between defined contribution and defined benefit? 5. Which ERISA form is filed each year? 6. How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description? 7. What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½? 8. What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free? 9. How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover? 10. Which small-employer plan has 100 or fewer employees and always 100% vested contributions? Scenario questions Scenario A: Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done? Scenario B: A 45-year-old employee of a for-profit company asks whether she can use a 403(b) plan. What is the answer? Scenario C: A self-employed consultant with no employees wants a plan with simple administration. Which plan fits?"},{"id":"ch11-s16","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> It meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA, and gets favorable tax treatment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Yes. Qualified plans cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> 403(b) plans.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Defined contribution sets the contribution. Defined benefit sets the benefit.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Form 5500.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Within 90 days after becoming a participant.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> 10% additional tax, plus income tax on the taxable portion (unless an exception applies).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The withdrawal must come after the 5-year period AND be made because of a qualifying event (age 59½, death, disability, or first home purchase up to $10,000).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it from other money within 60 days to roll over the full amount.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> SIMPLE plans.</p><p>Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To roll over the full amount, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding.</p><p>Scenario B: No. A 403(b) is for public schools, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations. A for-profit employer can use a 401(k) or similar plan.</p><p>Scenario C: A SEP fits. The employer (here the owner) contributes to a traditional IRA, and a SEP generally has no Form 5500 filing.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 11:</strong> Make a one-page table: Plan name, who can sponsor it, contribution or benefit defined, and vesting. Then write the Roth vs. Traditional comparison in four words each.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS: Rollovers</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-sponsor/simplified-employee-pension-plan-sep\">IRS: SEP plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-sponsor/simple-ira-plan\">IRS: SIMPLE IRA plan</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/irc-403b-tax-sheltered-annuity-plans\">IRS: 403(b) plans</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\">IRS Publication 590-B</a>; <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/employers-and-advisers/plan-administration-and-compliance/reporting-and-filing/form-5500\">DOL: Form 5500</a></p>","text":"1. It meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA, and gets favorable tax treatment. 2. Yes. Qualified plans cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees. 3. 403(b) plans. 4. Defined contribution sets the contribution. Defined benefit sets the benefit. 5. Form 5500. 6. Within 90 days after becoming a participant. 7. 10% additional tax, plus income tax on the taxable portion (unless an exception applies). 8. The withdrawal must come after the 5-year period AND be made because of a qualifying event (age 59½, death, disability, or first home purchase up to $10,000). 9. A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it from other money within 60 days to roll over the full amount. 10. SIMPLE plans. Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To roll over the full amount, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding. Scenario B: No. A 403(b) is for public schools, churches, and 501(c)(3) organizations. A for-profit employer can use a 401(k) or similar plan. Scenario C: A SEP fits. The employer (here the owner) contributes to a traditional IRA, and a SEP generally has no Form 5500 filing. Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 11: Make a one-page table: Plan name, who can sponsor it, contribution or benefit defined, and vesting. Then write the Roth vs. Traditional comparison in four words each."}]},{"id":"ch12","short":"Ch 12","title":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","label":"Chapter 12","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Health insurance has three main types (medical expense, disability income, AD&amp;D) and a lot of rules. The big exam topics: <strong>renewability</strong> (least to most favorable), <strong>COBRA</strong> (who gets how many months), <strong>HIPAA and ACA</strong> rules, <strong>group health</strong> basics, <strong>AD&amp;D</strong>, <strong>HSA</strong>, and <strong>disability income</strong> planning.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch12-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>COBRA</strong> = keep group health coverage after a qualifying event, such as leaving a job, at your own expense (up to 102% of the premium).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra\">U.S. Department of Labor, COBRA</a></p>","text":"COBRA = keep group health coverage after a qualifying event, such as leaving a job, at your own expense (up to 102% of the premium)."},{"id":"ch12-s2","title":"1. The three main categories","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>AD&amp;D</strong> (accidental death and dismemberment): the covered loss must result from an accident.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/accidental-means\">IRMI glossary, accidental means</a></p>","text":"AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment): the covered loss must result from an accident."},{"id":"ch12-s4","title":"3. Cost sharing","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annual limits:</strong> Under the ACA, annual dollar limits apply to cost-sharing for essential health benefits.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/18022\">42 U.S.C. 18022 (ACA essential health benefits)</a></p>","text":"Annual limits: Under the ACA, annual dollar limits apply to cost-sharing for essential health benefits."},{"id":"ch12-s6","title":"5. Group health essentials","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare coordination:</strong> For employers with 20 or more employees, the group plan must cover employees 65 and older on the same terms as younger employees, and the group plan is primary. For employers with fewer than 20 employees, Medicare is primary.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395y\">42 U.S.C. 1395y (Medicare Secondary Payer)</a></p>","text":"Medicare coordination: For employers with 20 or more employees, the group plan must cover employees 65 and older on the same terms as younger employees, and the group plan is primary. For employers with fewer than 20 employees, Medicare is primary."},{"id":"ch12-s7","title":"6. COBRA (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Full name:</strong> Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1985.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>What it does:</strong> Lets people who lose group coverage keep it, at their own expense, for a set time.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Who must offer it:</strong> Employers with 20 or more employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cost:</strong> Up to <strong>102%</strong> of the cost to the plan.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Time to elect:</strong> <strong>at least 60 days</strong> after the later of loss of coverage or the notice date.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Qualifying event</th><th>Coverage period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terminated (not for gross misconduct)</td><td><strong>18 months</strong> (29 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Reduction in hours</td><td><strong>18 months</strong> (29 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Employee dies</td><td><strong>36 months</strong> (for dependents)</td></tr><tr><td>Divorce or legal separation</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dependent child no longer qualifies</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Employee becomes eligible for Medicare</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>18 for the EMPLOYEE leaving. 36 for the FAMILY (death, divorce, child aging out, Medicare). Add 11 months if disabled (18 to 29).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra\">U.S. Department of Labor, COBRA</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1162\">29 U.S.C. 1162</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1163\">29 U.S.C. 1163</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1165\">29 U.S.C. 1165</a></p>","text":"Full name: Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1985. What it does: Lets people who lose group coverage keep it, at their own expense, for a set time. Who must offer it: Employers with 20 or more employees. Cost: Up to 102% of the cost to the plan. Time to elect: at least 60 days after the later of loss of coverage or the notice date. Qualifying event Coverage period Employment terminated (not for gross misconduct) 18 months (29 if disabled) Reduction in hours 18 months (29 if disabled) Employee dies 36 months (for dependents) Divorce or legal separation 36 months Dependent child no longer qualifies 36 months Employee becomes eligible for Medicare 36 months Memory trick 18 for the EMPLOYEE leaving. 36 for the FAMILY (death, divorce, child aging out, Medicare). Add 11 months if disabled (18 to 29)."},{"id":"ch12-s8","title":"7. HIPAA and the ACA","html":"<h4>Preexisting conditions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The ACA bans preexisting-condition exclusions in group and individual health plans, for plan years starting on or after January 1, 2014.</li></ul><h4>ACA (Affordable Care Act) essential health benefits</h4><p>Ten essential health benefits must be covered:</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Outpatient care</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Emergency care</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Inpatient hospital care</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Pregnancy and newborn care</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Mental health and substance use treatment</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Prescription drugs</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Lab services</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Preventive and wellness services, including chronic disease management</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Pediatric services, including dental and vision care</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dependent coverage:</strong> Children must be allowed to stay on a parent's plan until <strong>age 26</strong>, regardless of marital status.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Metal tiers (actuarial value):</strong></li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Plan pays</th><th>Insured pays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bronze</td><td>60%</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>Silver</td><td>70%</td><td>30%</td></tr><tr><td>Gold</td><td>80%</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>Platinum</td><td>90%</td><td>10%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>COBRA</strong> applies to employers with 20 or more employees.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-3\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-3</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/18022\">42 U.S.C. 18022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-14\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-14</a>; <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra\">U.S. Department of Labor, COBRA</a></p>","text":"Preexisting conditions The ACA bans preexisting-condition exclusions in group and individual health plans, for plan years starting on or after January 1, 2014. ACA (Affordable Care Act) essential health benefits Ten essential health benefits must be covered: 1. Outpatient care 2. Emergency care 3. Inpatient hospital care 4. Pregnancy and newborn care 5. Mental health and substance use treatment 6. Prescription drugs 7. Rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices 8. Lab services 9. Preventive and wellness services, including chronic disease management 10. Pediatric services, including dental and vision care Dependent coverage: Children must be allowed to stay on a parent's plan until age 26 , regardless of marital status. Metal tiers (actuarial value): Tier Plan pays Insured pays Bronze 60% 40% Silver 70% 30% Gold 80% 20% Platinum 90% 10% COBRA applies to employers with 20 or more employees."},{"id":"ch12-s9","title":"8. Coordination of benefits (COB)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Workers 65+ with a group plan:</strong> The group plan is primary when the employer has 20 or more employees, and Medicare is secondary.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395y\">42 U.S.C. 1395y (Medicare Secondary Payer)</a></p>","text":"Workers 65+ with a group plan: The group plan is primary when the employer has 20 or more employees, and Medicare is secondary."},{"id":"ch12-s11","title":"10. Waiting period vs. probationary period","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Waiting period</th><th>Probationary period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it applies to</td><td>Preexisting conditions</td><td>Sickness only, not accidents</td></tr><tr><td>Status today</td><td>Preexisting-condition exclusions are banned for group and individual plans (plan years starting on or after January 1, 2014)</td><td>A waiting period for sickness coverage in disability policies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-3\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-3</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/probationary-period\">IRMI glossary, probationary period</a></p>","text":"Waiting period Probationary period What it applies to Preexisting conditions Sickness only, not accidents Status today Preexisting-condition exclusions are banned for group and individual plans (plan years starting on or after January 1, 2014) A waiting period for sickness coverage in disability policies"},{"id":"ch12-s12","title":"11. Health Savings Accounts (HSA)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Contributions</strong></td><td>Limited by the IRS annual limit. <strong>2026 limits: $4,400 self-only; $8,750 family</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Catch-up</strong></td><td>Age <strong>55 and older</strong>, $1,000 a year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Withdrawals</strong></td><td>Tax-free for qualified medical expenses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Penalty</strong></td><td>20% additional tax plus income tax on distributions not used for qualified medical expenses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Death</strong></td><td>If the spouse is the beneficiary, the HSA is treated as the spouse's HSA</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Qualified expenses</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Medicare premiums (age 65 or older). <strong>NOT Medigap.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">COBRA coverage.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969\">IRS Publication 969</a></p>","text":"Feature Key facts Contributions Limited by the IRS annual limit. 2026 limits: $4,400 self-only; $8,750 family Catch-up Age 55 and older , $1,000 a year Withdrawals Tax-free for qualified medical expenses Penalty 20% additional tax plus income tax on distributions not used for qualified medical expenses Death If the spouse is the beneficiary, the HSA is treated as the spouse's HSA Qualified expenses Medicare premiums (age 65 or older). NOT Medigap. COBRA coverage."},{"id":"ch12-s13","title":"12. Cafeteria plans (Section 125)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Regulated by Section 125 of the Internal Revenue Code.</li><li class=\"l0\">Employees choose among benefits. Choosing a benefit does not make it taxable income to the employee.</li><li class=\"l0\">Participants can choose between cash and qualified benefits. Long-term care insurance is not a qualified benefit.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/125\">26 U.S.C. 125</a></p>","text":"Regulated by Section 125 of the Internal Revenue Code. Employees choose among benefits. Choosing a benefit does not make it taxable income to the employee. Participants can choose between cash and qualified benefits. Long-term care insurance is not a qualified benefit."},{"id":"ch12-s14","title":"13. Tax treatment","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disability benefits:</strong> Tax-free when the employee paid the premiums with after-tax money. Taxable when the employer paid the premiums.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/104\">26 U.S.C. 104</a></p>","text":"Disability benefits: Tax-free when the employee paid the premiums with after-tax money. Taxable when the employer paid the premiums."},{"id":"ch12-s20","title":"19. AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accidental means:</strong> The covered loss must result from an accident, not merely be the accidental result of a nonaccidental event.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accidental results:</strong> Only the result must be unintended.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Per stirpes:</strong> The benefit passes to the beneficiary's heirs (bloodline) if the beneficiary dies first.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/accidental-means\">IRMI glossary, accidental means</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/per-stirpes\">IRMI glossary, per stirpes</a></p>","text":"Accidental means: The covered loss must result from an accident, not merely be the accidental result of a nonaccidental event. Accidental results: Only the result must be unintended. Per stirpes: The benefit passes to the beneficiary's heirs (bloodline) if the beneficiary dies first."},{"id":"ch12-s21","title":"20. Federal and civil acts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pregnancy Discrimination Act</strong></td><td>Women affected by pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions are treated the same for employment-related purposes, including fringe benefits, as others similar in their ability or inability to work. The employer is not required to pay for abortion coverage, except where the mother's life would be endangered or medical complications have arisen from an abortion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ADEA</strong> (Age Discrimination in Employment Act)</td><td>Employers with 20+ employees. Protects workers 40 and older</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Employees 65 and older who keep working must get the same group health benefits, under the same conditions, as younger employees (for employers with 20+ employees).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/2000e\">42 U.S.C. 2000e(k)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/631\">29 U.S.C. 631</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395y\">42 U.S.C. 1395y</a></p>","text":"Law Key facts Pregnancy Discrimination Act Women affected by pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions are treated the same for employment-related purposes, including fringe benefits, as others similar in their ability or inability to work. The employer is not required to pay for abortion coverage, except where the mother's life would be endangered or medical complications have arisen from an abortion ADEA (Age Discrimination in Employment Act) Employers with 20+ employees. Protects workers 40 and older Employees 65 and older who keep working must get the same group health benefits, under the same conditions, as younger employees (for employers with 20+ employees)."},{"id":"ch12-s24","title":"23. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accidental means vs. accidental results</td><td>The cause must be an accident vs. only the result must be unintended</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting period vs. probationary period</td><td>Preexisting conditions (exclusions now banned) vs. sickness-only delay</td></tr><tr><td>Dependent coverage vs. COBRA</td><td>Child to age 26 (ACA) vs. departing employee's continuation</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare primary vs. secondary</td><td>Employer under 20: Medicare primary. 20+: group plan primary</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/accidental-means\">IRMI glossary, accidental means</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/probationary-period\">IRMI glossary, probationary period</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-3\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-3</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-14\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-14</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395y\">42 U.S.C. 1395y</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Accidental means vs. accidental results The cause must be an accident vs. only the result must be unintended Waiting period vs. probationary period Preexisting conditions (exclusions now banned) vs. sickness-only delay Dependent coverage vs. COBRA Child to age 26 (ACA) vs. departing employee's continuation Medicare primary vs. secondary Employer under 20: Medicare primary. 20+: group plan primary"},{"id":"ch12-s25","title":"24. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Name the ten ACA essential health benefits.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the difference between accidental means and accidental results?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost?</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1162\">29 U.S.C. 1162</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1163\">29 U.S.C. 1163</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1165\">29 U.S.C. 1165</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/18022\">42 U.S.C. 18022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-14\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-14</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/accidental-means\">IRMI glossary, accidental means</a></p>","text":"1. Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled? 2. How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA? 3. What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged? 4. Name the ten ACA essential health benefits. 5. How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA? 6. What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution? 7. What is the difference between accidental means and accidental results? Scenario questions Scenario A: Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost?"},{"id":"ch12-s26","title":"25. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 18 months (29 if disabled).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> At least 60 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> 102% of the premium.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Outpatient care, emergency care, inpatient hospital care, pregnancy and newborn care, mental health and substance use treatment, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices, lab services, preventive and wellness services (including chronic disease management), pediatric services (including dental and vision care).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Until age 26.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 55.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Accidental means: the covered loss must result from an accident. Accidental results: only the result must be unintended.</p><p>Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 12:</strong> Make two cards. Card 1: COBRA (18/29 employee, 36 family, at least 60 days to elect, 102%, 20+ employees). Card 2: ACA (10 essential benefits, dependents to age 26, metal tiers Bronze 60%, Silver 70%, Gold 80%, Platinum 90%). Recite both before the exam.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra\">U.S. Department of Labor, COBRA</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1162\">29 U.S.C. 1162</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1163\">29 U.S.C. 1163</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/1165\">29 U.S.C. 1165</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/18022\">42 U.S.C. 18022</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-14\">42 U.S.C. 300gg-14</a></p>","text":"1. 18 months (29 if disabled). 2. At least 60 days. 3. 102% of the premium. 4. Outpatient care, emergency care, inpatient hospital care, pregnancy and newborn care, mental health and substance use treatment, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices, lab services, preventive and wellness services (including chronic disease management), pediatric services (including dental and vision care). 5. Until age 26. 6. 55. 7. Accidental means: the covered loss must result from an accident. Accidental results: only the result must be unintended. Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies. Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 12: Make two cards. Card 1: COBRA (18/29 employee, 36 family, at least 60 days to elect, 102%, 20+ employees). Card 2: ACA (10 essential benefits, dependents to age 26, metal tiers Bronze 60%, Silver 70%, Gold 80%, Platinum 90%). Recite both before the exam."}]},{"id":"ch13","short":"Ch 13","title":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","label":"Chapter 13","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Two big sections. (1) <strong>Who provides health care coverage</strong>: commercial insurers (reimbursement), service providers (prepaid), HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. (2) <strong>Government programs</strong>: Medicare (Parts A, B, C, D), Medicaid, Social Security disability, workers' compensation, TRICARE, FEHB. <strong>Medicare Part A vs. Part B</strong> and <strong>HMO vs. PPO</strong> are heavily tested.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch13-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s2","title":"1. Commercial insurers vs. service providers","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s3","title":"2. Managed care cost-control tools","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s4","title":"3. Provider organizations (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s5","title":"4. HMO structures","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare HMOs:</strong> open enrollment for at least 30 consecutive days each contract year.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Emergency services:</strong> must be available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/417.426\">42 CFR 417.426</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/417.106\">42 CFR 417.106</a></p>","text":"Medicare HMOs: open enrollment for at least 30 consecutive days each contract year. Emergency services: must be available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week."},{"id":"ch13-s6","title":"5. PPOs","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s7","title":"6. Medicare (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Part A vs. Part B</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>PART A (Hospital Insurance)</th><th>PART B (Medical Insurance)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Premium</td><td>$0 for most people (premium-free)</td><td>Monthly premium, paid each month even with no Part B services</td></tr><tr><td>Enrollment</td><td>—</td><td>Sign up when first eligible; a late-enrollment penalty applies if you wait</td></tr><tr><td>Cost sharing</td><td>—</td><td>After the deductible, you usually pay 20% of the cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/medicare-basics/what-does-medicare-cost\">Medicare.gov: What does Medicare cost?</a></p>","text":"Part A vs. Part B PART A (Hospital Insurance) PART B (Medical Insurance) Premium $0 for most people (premium-free) Monthly premium, paid each month even with no Part B services Enrollment — Sign up when first eligible; a late-enrollment penalty applies if you wait Cost sharing — After the deductible, you usually pay 20% of the cost"},{"id":"ch13-s8","title":"7. Social Security disability (OASDI)","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s9","title":"8. Medicaid","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicaid</strong> is the medical assistance program established under Title XIX of the Social Security Act.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1396\">42 U.S.C. 1396 (LII)</a></p>","text":"Medicaid is the medical assistance program established under Title XIX of the Social Security Act."},{"id":"ch13-s10","title":"9. Workers' compensation","html":"<h4>Four types of disability benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Temporary total</li><li class=\"l0\">Temporary partial</li><li class=\"l0\">Permanent total: the worker cannot do <strong>any</strong> work due to the injury</li><li class=\"l0\">Permanent partial</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/owcp/dlhwc/FAQ/lsfaqs\">DOL OWCP LHWCA FAQ (federal Longshore Act)</a></p>","text":"Four types of disability benefits Temporary total Temporary partial Permanent total: the worker cannot do any work due to the injury Permanent partial"},{"id":"ch13-s11","title":"10. Other programs and arrangements","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>MEWA</strong> (multiple employer welfare arrangement)</td><td>Provides benefits to the employees of two or more employers, or to their beneficiaries (ERISA section 3(40)(A))</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/information-letters/05-08-2006\">DOL Information Letter 05-08-2006</a></p>","text":"Program Key facts MEWA (multiple employer welfare arrangement) Provides benefits to the employees of two or more employers, or to their beneficiaries (ERISA section 3(40)(A))"},{"id":"ch13-s12","title":"11. Tax treatment (health programs)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Social Security benefits can be taxable. Base amounts: <strong>$25,000</strong> (single) and <strong>$32,000</strong> (married filing jointly). Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p915\">IRS Publication 915</a></p>","text":"Social Security benefits can be taxable. Base amounts: $25,000 (single) and $32,000 (married filing jointly). Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable."},{"id":"ch13-s13","title":"12. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s14","title":"13. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":""},{"id":"ch13-s15","title":"14. Answer key","html":"<div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 13:</strong> Draw a 4-column table: HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. Fill in PCP, referrals, out-of-network, cost. Then draw Medicare Part A and Part B side by side with premium and enrollment.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> none; no claim in this section could be sourced from an opened page</p>","text":"Memory trick Memory tip for Chapter 13: Draw a 4-column table: HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. Fill in PCP, referrals, out-of-network, cost. Then draw Medicare Part A and Part B side by side with premium and enrollment."}]},{"id":"ch14","short":"Ch 14","title":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","label":"Chapter 14","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Medical expense policies pay after the insured's share. Learn <strong>basic vs. major medical</strong>, the <strong>6 deductible types</strong>, <strong>coinsurance and stop-loss</strong>, the <strong>claims math</strong> (deductible first, then coinsurance), the <strong>surgical payment methods</strong>, and the <strong>HSA / HRA / MSA / FSA</strong> accounts.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch14-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>HSA:</strong> requires a high deductible health plan (HDHP). Unused funds carry over each year.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>HRA:</strong> employer contributions only.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Health FSA:</strong> unused amounts are generally forfeited.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Hospital indemnity:</strong> pays a fixed dollar amount per day (or other period), not a percentage of expenses.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969\">IRS Pub. 969</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/45/146.145\">45 CFR 146.145 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"HSA: requires a high deductible health plan (HDHP). Unused funds carry over each year. HRA: employer contributions only. Health FSA: unused amounts are generally forfeited. Hospital indemnity: pays a fixed dollar amount per day (or other period), not a percentage of expenses."},{"id":"ch14-s6","title":"5. Hospital expense policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Indemnity basis:</strong> Fixed dollars per day, regardless of actual expenses.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/45/146.145\">45 CFR 146.145 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Indemnity basis: Fixed dollars per day, regardless of actual expenses."},{"id":"ch14-s8","title":"6. Tax-advantaged accounts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Funded by</th><th>Requires HDHP?</th><th>Rollover?</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HSA</strong> (health savings account)</td><td>Individual, employer, or others</td><td>YES</td><td>YES (unused funds carry over)</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HRA</strong> (health reimbursement arrangement)</td><td>EMPLOYER ONLY</td><td>—</td><td>Depends on the plan</td><td>Employer plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Archer MSA</strong> (medical savings account)</td><td>Individual or employer (not both in the same year)</td><td>—</td><td>YES (balance carries over)</td><td>Individual (small employer: 50 or fewer employees)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Health FSA</strong> (flexible spending arrangement)</td><td>Employee salary reduction; employer may add</td><td>—</td><td>Generally NO (use it or lose it)</td><td>Employee</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> A health FSA plan may allow a limited carryover or a grace period, but not both.</p></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>HSA = needs an HDHP, carries over. HRA = employer funds it. FSA = use it or lose it.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969\">IRS Pub. 969</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b\">IRS Pub. 15-B</a></p>","text":"Account Funded by Requires HDHP? Rollover? Owner HSA (health savings account) Individual, employer, or others YES YES (unused funds carry over) Individual HRA (health reimbursement arrangement) EMPLOYER ONLY — Depends on the plan Employer plan Archer MSA (medical savings account) Individual or employer (not both in the same year) — YES (balance carries over) Individual (small employer: 50 or fewer employees) Health FSA (flexible spending arrangement) Employee salary reduction; employer may add — Generally NO (use it or lose it) Employee Note: A health FSA plan may allow a limited carryover or a grace period, but not both. Memory trick HSA = needs an HDHP, carries over. HRA = employer funds it. FSA = use it or lose it."},{"id":"ch14-s9","title":"7. Specialty policies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hospital indemnity</strong></td><td>Pays a fixed dollar amount per day (or other period) of hospitalization or illness, regardless of expenses incurred</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Specified disease</strong> (single-disease, such as cancer-only)</td><td>Treated as an excepted benefit only if it pays on the same fixed-indemnity basis, under a separate policy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/45/146.145\">45 CFR 146.145 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Policy Key facts Hospital indemnity Pays a fixed dollar amount per day (or other period) of hospitalization or illness, regardless of expenses incurred Specified disease (single-disease, such as cancer-only) Treated as an excepted benefit only if it pays on the same fixed-indemnity basis, under a separate policy"},{"id":"ch14-s13","title":"11. Tax treatment","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reimbursed expenses</td><td>NOT deductible (expenses paid by insurance cannot be included)</td></tr><tr><td>Unreimbursed expenses</td><td>Deductible only the part above 7.5% of AGI</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term care premiums</td><td>Count as medical expenses within age-based limits set by the IRS</td></tr><tr><td>Benefits for medical expenses</td><td>Employer-provided coverage and reimbursements are generally excluded from income</td></tr><tr><td>Disability income benefits</td><td>Tax-free if you paid the premiums; generally taxable if your employer paid</td></tr><tr><td>Self-employed</td><td>May deduct health premiums paid (Form 7206), but not for months eligible for an employer-subsidized plan</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502\">IRS Pub. 502</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525\">IRS Pub. 525</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i7206\">IRS Form 7206 instructions</a></p>","text":"Item Treatment Reimbursed expenses NOT deductible (expenses paid by insurance cannot be included) Unreimbursed expenses Deductible only the part above 7.5% of AGI Long-term care premiums Count as medical expenses within age-based limits set by the IRS Benefits for medical expenses Employer-provided coverage and reimbursements are generally excluded from income Disability income benefits Tax-free if you paid the premiums; generally taxable if your employer paid Self-employed May deduct health premiums paid (Form 7206), but not for months eligible for an employer-subsidized plan"},{"id":"ch14-s15","title":"13. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hospital indemnity vs. reimbursement</td><td>Fixed payment per day vs. actual expenses</td></tr><tr><td>HSA vs. HRA vs. FSA</td><td>Needs HDHP and carries over vs. employer-only vs. generally use-it-or-lose-it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/45/146.145\">45 CFR 146.145 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969\">IRS Pub. 969</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Hospital indemnity vs. reimbursement Fixed payment per day vs. actual expenses HSA vs. HRA vs. FSA Needs HDHP and carries over vs. employer-only vs. generally use-it-or-lose-it"},{"id":"ch14-s17","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> HRA.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> HSA.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> No. They are generally forfeited (a plan may offer a carryover or grace period).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> No. They pay a fixed amount per day regardless of expenses.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> $945 (70% of $1,350).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> No.</p><p>Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.)</p><p>Scenario B: An HSA.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969\">IRS Pub. 969</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502\">IRS Pub. 502</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/45/146.145\">45 CFR 146.145 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"1. HRA. 2. HSA. 3. No. They are generally forfeited (a plan may offer a carryover or grace period). 4. No. They pay a fixed amount per day regardless of expenses. 5. $945 (70% of $1,350). 6. No. Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.) Scenario B: An HSA."}]},{"id":"ch15","short":"Ch 15","title":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","label":"Chapter 15","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Disability income insurance replaces income when you can't work. The most tested idea is <strong>own occupation vs. any occupation</strong>. Next: total vs. partial disability, the two waiting periods (probationary vs. elimination), flat vs. residual benefits, and the riders.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch15-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Own-occupation</strong> (Louisiana option): the insurer may require complete inability to perform all substantial and material duties of your regular occupation.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Any-occupation</strong> (general definition): cannot work in any employment you are qualified for by education, training, or experience that gives substantially the same earning capacity as before.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Elimination period:</strong> a waiting period that must pass before benefits become payable.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507827\">La. R.S. 22:990</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/elimination-period\">IRMI: Elimination period</a></p>","text":"Own-occupation (Louisiana option): the insurer may require complete inability to perform all substantial and material duties of your regular occupation. Any-occupation (general definition): cannot work in any employment you are qualified for by education, training, or experience that gives substantially the same earning capacity as before. Elimination period: a waiting period that must pass before benefits become payable."},{"id":"ch15-s3","title":"2. Own occupation vs. any occupation (MOST TESTED)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>OWN OCCUPATION (own-occ)</th><th>ANY OCCUPATION (any-occ)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>Complete inability to perform all substantial and material duties of your regular occupation</td><td>Cannot work in any employment you are qualified for by education, training, or experience that gives substantially the same earning capacity as before</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>OWN = &quot;Only my job.&quot; ANY = &quot;Any job I am qualified for at similar earnings.&quot;</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507827\">La. R.S. 22:990</a></p>","text":"OWN OCCUPATION (own-occ) ANY OCCUPATION (any-occ) Definition Complete inability to perform all substantial and material duties of your regular occupation Cannot work in any employment you are qualified for by education, training, or experience that gives substantially the same earning capacity as before Memory trick OWN = \"Only my job.\" ANY = \"Any job I am qualified for at similar earnings.\""},{"id":"ch15-s7","title":"6. Probationary vs. elimination period (CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Elimination (waiting) period:</strong> the time that must pass before disability benefits become payable.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/elimination-period\">IRMI: Elimination period</a></p>","text":"Elimination (waiting) period: the time that must pass before disability benefits become payable."},{"id":"ch15-s11","title":"10. Major riders and provisions","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Waiver of premium:</strong> the insurer gives up its right to collect premium from the insured.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver-of-premium\">IRMI: Waiver of premium</a></p>","text":"Waiver of premium: the insurer gives up its right to collect premium from the insured."},{"id":"ch15-s14","title":"13. Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA): major life activities","html":"<p>Major life activities include: caring for oneself; performing manual tasks; seeing, hearing, eating, sleeping; walking, standing, lifting, bending; speaking, breathing; learning, reading, concentrating, thinking, communicating; and working. The list is not exhaustive.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/12102\">42 U.S.C. 12102 (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Major life activities include: caring for oneself; performing manual tasks; seeing, hearing, eating, sleeping; walking, standing, lifting, bending; speaking, breathing; learning, reading, concentrating, thinking, communicating; and working. The list is not exhaustive."},{"id":"ch15-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Own-occ vs. any-occ</td><td>Inability to do your regular occupation vs. inability to do any work you are qualified for at substantially the same earning capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Waiver of premium vs. disability income</td><td>Insurer gives up collecting premium vs. income paid to the insured</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507827\">La. R.S. 22:990</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver-of-premium\">IRMI: Waiver of premium</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Own-occ vs. any-occ Inability to do your regular occupation vs. inability to do any work you are qualified for at substantially the same earning capacity Waiver of premium vs. disability income Insurer gives up collecting premium vs. income paid to the insured"},{"id":"ch15-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Own-occ: cannot perform the substantial and material duties of your regular occupation. Any-occ: cannot do any work you are qualified for at substantially the same earning capacity.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> A waiting period before benefits become payable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> The insurer gives up its right to collect premium.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Yes.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Generally yes. They are tax-free if you paid the premiums.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507827\">La. R.S. 22:990</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/elimination-period\">IRMI: Elimination period</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver-of-premium\">IRMI: Waiver of premium</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/12102\">42 U.S.C. 12102 (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525\">IRS Pub. 525</a></p>","text":"1. Own-occ: cannot perform the substantial and material duties of your regular occupation. Any-occ: cannot do any work you are qualified for at substantially the same earning capacity. 2. A waiting period before benefits become payable. 3. The insurer gives up its right to collect premium. 4. Yes. 5. Generally yes. They are tax-free if you paid the premiums."}]},{"id":"ch16","short":"Ch 16","title":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","label":"Chapter 16","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three private products for people 65+: <strong>Medigap</strong> (fills Medicare gaps), <strong>Medicare Advantage / Part C</strong> (private alternative to Original Medicare), and <strong>Part D</strong> (prescription drugs). Then <strong>long-term care insurance</strong>, which is NOT covered by Medicare. Focus on the time periods, the ADL trigger, and the enrollment windows.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch16-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medigap</strong> = private insurance that helps pay Original Medicare (A and B) cost sharing.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Advantage (Part C)</strong> = used instead of Original Medicare. <strong>Not combined</strong> with Medigap.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term care</strong>: <strong>Medicare doesn't pay for it.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>LTC trigger:</strong> <strong>2 ADLs</strong> for <strong>90+ days</strong>, or severe cognitive impairment.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/how-medigap-works\">Medicare.gov: Learn How Medigap Works</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care\">Medicare.gov: Long-term care</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Medigap = private insurance that helps pay Original Medicare (A and B) cost sharing. Medicare Advantage (Part C) = used instead of Original Medicare. Not combined with Medigap. Long-term care : Medicare doesn't pay for it. LTC trigger: 2 ADLs for 90+ days , or severe cognitive impairment."},{"id":"ch16-s2","title":"1. Medicare supplement (Medigap) policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>What it is:</strong> Private insurance that helps pay your share of Original Medicare costs (copays, coinsurance, deductibles). You generally need Parts A and B to buy it.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>One person per policy.</strong> Spouses each buy their own.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Standardized:</strong> 10 plans in most states: <strong>A–D, F, G, K–N</strong>. Same letter = same benefits; only price differs. Massachusetts, Minnesota, and Wisconsin standardize differently.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Plans C and F</strong> can't be sold to people newly eligible for Medicare on or after <strong>January 1, 2020</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No drug coverage</strong> in Medigap plans sold after 2005. Use a separate Part D plan.</li></ul><h4>Open enrollment (CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>6 months</strong>, starting the first month you have <strong>Part B and are 65 or older</strong>. One-time only.</li><li class=\"l0\">During it, the insurer <strong>can't deny coverage</strong> for pre-existing health problems.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana birthday rule:</strong> a policyholder gets <strong>63 days</strong> from each birthday to buy any Medigap policy from the same issuer. No health underwriting if the new plan letter has equal or fewer benefits.</li></ul><h4>Louisiana minimum standards</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preexisting conditions:</strong> 6-month look-back; no claim denied for a loss more than <strong>6 months</strong> after the effective date.</li><li class=\"l0\">No benefits that <strong>duplicate Medicare</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Outline of coverage</strong> delivered at application.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>30-day free look</strong> for a premium refund.</li></ul><h4>Benefits by plan</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Which plans</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part A coinsurance + hospital costs up to <strong>365 extra days</strong></td><td>All</td></tr><tr><td>Part B coinsurance; <strong>first 3 pints of blood</strong>; hospice coinsurance</td><td>All (K pays 50%, L pays 75%)</td></tr><tr><td>Part B <strong>excess charges</strong></td><td>F, G</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign travel emergency (80%)</td><td>C, D, F, G, M, N</td></tr><tr><td>Out-of-pocket limit (2026)</td><td>K $8,000; L $4,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Excess charge:</strong> The amount a provider may legally charge above the Medicare-approved amount.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Plan M</strong> pays 50% of the Part A deductible. <strong>Plan N</strong> may charge copays up to $20 for some office visits and $50 for ER visits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Renewal:</strong> Automatic. Insurer can drop you only for nonpayment, an untruthful application, or the insurer going out of business.</li></ul><h4>Medicaid suspension</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Benefits and premiums suspended on request for up to <strong>24 months</strong> while on Medicaid, if the insurer is notified within <strong>90 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Reinstated automatically if the insured gives notice within <strong>90 days</strong> of losing Medicaid.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits\">Medicare.gov: Compare Medigap Plan Benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/how-medigap-works\">Medicare.gov: Learn How Medigap Works</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507922\">La. R.S. 22:1111</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=1296623\">La. R.S. 22:1112</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395ss\">42 U.S.C. 1395ss (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"What it is: Private insurance that helps pay your share of Original Medicare costs (copays, coinsurance, deductibles). You generally need Parts A and B to buy it. One person per policy. Spouses each buy their own. Standardized: 10 plans in most states: A–D, F, G, K–N . Same letter = same benefits; only price differs. Massachusetts, Minnesota, and Wisconsin standardize differently. Plans C and F can't be sold to people newly eligible for Medicare on or after January 1, 2020 . No drug coverage in Medigap plans sold after 2005. Use a separate Part D plan. Open enrollment (CRITICAL) 6 months , starting the first month you have Part B and are 65 or older . One-time only. During it, the insurer can't deny coverage for pre-existing health problems. Louisiana birthday rule: a policyholder gets 63 days from each birthday to buy any Medigap policy from the same issuer. No health underwriting if the new plan letter has equal or fewer benefits. Louisiana minimum standards Preexisting conditions: 6-month look-back; no claim denied for a loss more than 6 months after the effective date. No benefits that duplicate Medicare . Outline of coverage delivered at application. 30-day free look for a premium refund. Benefits by plan Benefit Which plans Part A coinsurance + hospital costs up to 365 extra days All Part B coinsurance; first 3 pints of blood ; hospice coinsurance All (K pays 50%, L pays 75%) Part B excess charges F, G Foreign travel emergency (80%) C, D, F, G, M, N Out-of-pocket limit (2026) K $8,000; L $4,000 Excess charge: The amount a provider may legally charge above the Medicare-approved amount. Plan M pays 50% of the Part A deductible. Plan N may charge copays up to $20 for some office visits and $50 for ER visits. Renewal: Automatic. Insurer can drop you only for nonpayment, an untruthful application, or the insurer going out of business. Medicaid suspension Benefits and premiums suspended on request for up to 24 months while on Medicaid, if the insurer is notified within 90 days . Reinstated automatically if the insured gives notice within 90 days of losing Medicaid."},{"id":"ch16-s3","title":"2. Medicare Select","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>Medigap policy</strong> that may require network hospitals (sometimes doctors) for full benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exception:</strong> Urgent, unforeseen care out of network gets full benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>12-month right</strong> to switch to a standard Medigap policy.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395ss\">42 U.S.C. 1395ss (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"A Medigap policy that may require network hospitals (sometimes doctors) for full benefits. Exception: Urgent, unforeseen care out of network gets full benefits. 12-month right to switch to a standard Medigap policy."},{"id":"ch16-s4","title":"3. Medicare and employer coverage","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation (age 65+, current job)</th><th>Who pays first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employer has <strong>20 or more</strong> employees</td><td>Group health plan pays FIRST; Medicare pays second</td></tr><tr><td>Employer has <strong>fewer than 20</strong> employees</td><td>Medicare pays FIRST; group plan pays second</td></tr><tr><td>Retiree coverage or COBRA</td><td>Medicare pays first</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/coordination/who-pays-first\">Medicare.gov: Who pays first</a></p>","text":"Situation (age 65+, current job) Who pays first Employer has 20 or more employees Group health plan pays FIRST; Medicare pays second Employer has fewer than 20 employees Medicare pays FIRST; group plan pays second Retiree coverage or COBRA Medicare pays first"},{"id":"ch16-s5","title":"4. Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Also known as Medicare Advantage, \"Part C,\" or \"MA Plans.\"</li><li class=\"l0\">Offered by <strong>private companies</strong> that follow rules set by Medicare.</li><li class=\"l0\">Gives almost all of your Part A and Part B benefits. Most plans include Part D drug coverage.</li></ul><h4>Types of Part C plans</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HMO</strong></td><td>Care generally from network providers only (except emergencies and certain urgent or dialysis care). Many require referrals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PPO</strong></td><td>Network, but out-of-network providers can be used, usually at a higher cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PFFS</strong></td><td>Private fee-for-service. The plan decides what it pays providers and what you owe. You can see any Medicare-approved provider that accepts the plan's payment terms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SNP</strong></td><td>Special needs plan for people with certain chronic conditions, institutional needs, or Medicaid. Must include Part D</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Part C eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must have BOTH Part A and Part B.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must live in the plan's service area.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must be a U.S. citizen or lawfully present in the U.S.</li><li class=\"l0\">You <strong>can</strong> join even if you have end-stage renal disease (ESRD).</li></ul><h4>Part C key points</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">If you join an HMO or PPO without drug coverage, you cannot join a separate Medicare drug plan.</li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare Medical Savings Account (MSA) plans combine a high-deductible plan with a savings account.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/publications/12026-understanding-medicare-advantage-plans.pdf\">Medicare.gov: Understanding Medicare Advantage Plans</a></p>","text":"Also known as Medicare Advantage, \"Part C,\" or \"MA Plans.\" Offered by private companies that follow rules set by Medicare. Gives almost all of your Part A and Part B benefits. Most plans include Part D drug coverage. Types of Part C plans Plan Key facts HMO Care generally from network providers only (except emergencies and certain urgent or dialysis care). Many require referrals PPO Network, but out-of-network providers can be used, usually at a higher cost PFFS Private fee-for-service. The plan decides what it pays providers and what you owe. You can see any Medicare-approved provider that accepts the plan's payment terms SNP Special needs plan for people with certain chronic conditions, institutional needs, or Medicaid. Must include Part D Part C eligibility Must have BOTH Part A and Part B. Must live in the plan's service area. Must be a U.S. citizen or lawfully present in the U.S. You can join even if you have end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Part C key points If you join an HMO or PPO without drug coverage, you cannot join a separate Medicare drug plan. Medicare Medical Savings Account (MSA) plans combine a high-deductible plan with a savings account."},{"id":"ch16-s6","title":"5. Medicare Part D (prescription drugs)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Most Medicare Advantage plans include drug coverage (Part D). Standalone drug plans are also available.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Late enrollment penalty:</strong> <strong>1% per month</strong> of the national base beneficiary premium for each full month you were eligible but went without Part D or other creditable drug coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\">The penalty can apply after your Initial Enrollment Period ends if you go <strong>63 or more days in a row</strong> without creditable coverage. It is added to your monthly premium.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d/basics/costs\">Medicare.gov: Part D costs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/publications/12026-understanding-medicare-advantage-plans.pdf\">Medicare.gov: Understanding Medicare Advantage Plans</a></p>","text":"Most Medicare Advantage plans include drug coverage (Part D). Standalone drug plans are also available. Late enrollment penalty: 1% per month of the national base beneficiary premium for each full month you were eligible but went without Part D or other creditable drug coverage. The penalty can apply after your Initial Enrollment Period ends if you go 63 or more days in a row without creditable coverage. It is added to your monthly premium."},{"id":"ch16-s7","title":"6. MACRA (2015)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act</strong> of 2015 (MACRA): creates the Quality Payment Program, which can raise or lower Medicare payments to clinicians based on performance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>MIPS</strong> categories: Quality, Improvement Activities, Advancing Care Information, and Cost.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key impact for this chapter:</strong> Since Jan. 1, 2020, Medigap policies that cover the Part B deductible (Plans C and F) cannot be sold to newly eligible Medicare beneficiaries (42 U.S.C. 1395ss(z)).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cms.gov/files/document/qppmipsparticipationfactsheetpdf\">CMS: MIPS participation fact sheet</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395ss\">42 U.S.C. 1395ss (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act of 2015 (MACRA): creates the Quality Payment Program, which can raise or lower Medicare payments to clinicians based on performance. MIPS categories: Quality, Improvement Activities, Advancing Care Information, and Cost. Key impact for this chapter: Since Jan. 1, 2020, Medigap policies that cover the Part B deductible (Plans C and F) cannot be sold to newly eligible Medicare beneficiaries (42 U.S.C. 1395ss(z))."},{"id":"ch16-s8","title":"7. Long-term care (LTC) insurance","html":"<h4>Purpose and basics</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">LTC (also called <strong>custodial care</strong>) is medical and non-medical care for people with a chronic illness or disability. Most of it helps with activities of daily living.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare and Medigap don't pay for LTC.</strong> Medicaid may, if you qualify, or you can buy private LTC insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\">LTC is different from <strong>skilled nursing facility</strong> care, which Part A covers short-term after a 3-day inpatient stay.</li><li class=\"l0\">Most policies pay a <strong>specific dollar amount</strong> per day in a facility or per home-care visit.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> LTC insurance covers at least <strong>12 consecutive months</strong>, in a setting other than an acute care hospital unit.</li><li class=\"l0\">Policies can include home health, respite, hospice, assisted living, and adult day care.</li><li class=\"l0\">Younger buyers typically pay lower premiums.</li></ul><h4>Benefit trigger: tax-qualified (EXAM CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Certified as unable to perform <strong>at least 2 ADLs</strong> without substantial assistance for <strong>at least 90 days</strong>, OR</li><li class=\"l0\">Needing substantial supervision due to <strong>severe cognitive impairment</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Recertified within the past 12 months.</li></ul><p><strong>The six ADLs:</strong> eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence. A qualified contract must use at least 5.</p><h4>Louisiana LTC policy rules</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Can't be canceled or nonrenewed for <strong>age or declining health</strong>. Tax-qualified contracts must be <strong>guaranteed renewable</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preexisting:</strong> 6-month look-back; exclusion only for losses within <strong>6 months</strong> after the effective date.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No prior hospitalization</strong> requirement. Can't cover skilled care only.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>30-day free look.</strong> Policy delivered within 30 days after approval.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Contestability:</strong> under 6 months, material misrepresentation; 6 months to 2 years, it must also relate to the claim; after <strong>2 years</strong>, only knowing, intentional misrepresentation.</li><li class=\"l0\">No <strong>field issue</strong> based on health. Benefits paid can't be recovered after rescission.</li></ul><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Respite care:</strong> Temporary care that gives a family caregiver time off.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Skilled nursing care:</strong> Care only a licensed nurse or therapist can safely perform.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care\">Medicare.gov: Long-term care</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care\">Medicare.gov: Skilled nursing facility care</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/long-term-care-insurance\">IRMI: Long Term Care Insurance</a>; <a href=\"https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/long-term-care-insurance\">NAIC: Long-Term Care Insurance</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507976\">La. R.S. 22:1184</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507979\">La. R.S. 22:1187</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/respite-care\">IRMI: Respite Care</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/skilled-nursing-care\">IRMI: Skilled Nursing Care</a></p>","text":"Purpose and basics LTC (also called custodial care ) is medical and non-medical care for people with a chronic illness or disability. Most of it helps with activities of daily living. Medicare and Medigap don't pay for LTC. Medicaid may, if you qualify, or you can buy private LTC insurance. LTC is different from skilled nursing facility care, which Part A covers short-term after a 3-day inpatient stay. Most policies pay a specific dollar amount per day in a facility or per home-care visit. Louisiana: LTC insurance covers at least 12 consecutive months , in a setting other than an acute care hospital unit. Policies can include home health, respite, hospice, assisted living, and adult day care. Younger buyers typically pay lower premiums. Benefit trigger: tax-qualified (EXAM CRITICAL) Certified as unable to perform at least 2 ADLs without substantial assistance for at least 90 days , OR Needing substantial supervision due to severe cognitive impairment . Recertified within the past 12 months. The six ADLs: eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence. A qualified contract must use at least 5. Louisiana LTC policy rules Can't be canceled or nonrenewed for age or declining health . Tax-qualified contracts must be guaranteed renewable . Preexisting: 6-month look-back; exclusion only for losses within 6 months after the effective date. No prior hospitalization requirement. Can't cover skilled care only. 30-day free look. Policy delivered within 30 days after approval. Contestability: under 6 months, material misrepresentation; 6 months to 2 years, it must also relate to the claim; after 2 years , only knowing, intentional misrepresentation. No field issue based on health. Benefits paid can't be recovered after rescission. Respite care: Temporary care that gives a family caregiver time off. Skilled nursing care: Care only a licensed nurse or therapist can safely perform."},{"id":"ch16-s9","title":"8. Long-term care partnership programs","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Federal Medicaid law lets a state set up a long-term care partnership.</li><li class=\"l0\">Assets are disregarded in an amount equal to the insurance benefits paid. Example: if the policy pays $100,000, $100,000 of assets is disregarded.</li><li class=\"l0\">The policy must be a tax-qualified LTC policy bought by a state resident.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1396p\">42 U.S.C. 1396p (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"Federal Medicaid law lets a state set up a long-term care partnership. Assets are disregarded in an amount equal to the insurance benefits paid. Example: if the policy pays $100,000, $100,000 of assets is disregarded. The policy must be a tax-qualified LTC policy bought by a state resident."},{"id":"ch16-s10","title":"9. Association and group LTC requirements","html":"<h4>Who counts as a group (Louisiana)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Employers, labor groups, and trade or professional associations.</li><li class=\"l0\">Other associations file proof with the commissioner first: <strong>100+ members</strong>, active <strong>1+ year</strong>, annual meetings, dues, voting.</li><li class=\"l0\">The association qualifies <strong>30 days after filing</strong> unless the commissioner finds otherwise.</li><li class=\"l0\">Association policies' benefits and underwriting are usually closer to <strong>individual</strong> policies. Employer groups often use less strict underwriting.</li></ul><h4>Required LTC disclosures</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Outline of coverage</strong> at first solicitation; a producer must deliver it before the application.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>30-day free look</strong> with a full refund.</li><li class=\"l0\">On written request, reasons for a <strong>claim denial</strong> within 60 days.</li><li class=\"l0\">Only compliant policies may be advertised as LTC or nursing home insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\">The NAIC publishes <strong>A Shopper's Guide to Long-Term Care Insurance</strong>.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507976\">La. R.S. 22:1184</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/long-term-care-insurance\">NAIC: Long-Term Care Insurance</a></p>","text":"Who counts as a group (Louisiana) Employers, labor groups, and trade or professional associations. Other associations file proof with the commissioner first: 100+ members , active 1+ year , annual meetings, dues, voting. The association qualifies 30 days after filing unless the commissioner finds otherwise. Association policies' benefits and underwriting are usually closer to individual policies. Employer groups often use less strict underwriting. Required LTC disclosures Outline of coverage at first solicitation; a producer must deliver it before the application. 30-day free look with a full refund. On written request, reasons for a claim denial within 60 days. Only compliant policies may be advertised as LTC or nursing home insurance. The NAIC publishes A Shopper's Guide to Long-Term Care Insurance ."},{"id":"ch16-s11","title":"10. LTC in life insurance and hybrid plans","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Hybrids:</strong> Louisiana LTC insurance includes life policies, riders, and annuities paying LTC benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\">A summary must show how LTC benefits affect the death benefit; <strong>monthly reports</strong> while paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Not LTC:</strong> Terminal-illness-only acceleration riders.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507976\">La. R.S. 22:1184</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a></p>","text":"Hybrids: Louisiana LTC insurance includes life policies, riders, and annuities paying LTC benefits. A summary must show how LTC benefits affect the death benefit; monthly reports while paid. Not LTC: Terminal-illness-only acceleration riders."},{"id":"ch16-s12","title":"11. Taxation of LTC","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A qualified LTC insurance contract is treated as an accident and health insurance contract.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefits:</strong> Generally not taxed, but periodic payments above the greater of <strong>$175 per day</strong> (inflation-adjusted) or actual qualified LTC costs are taxable.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer plans:</strong> An employer LTC plan is treated as an accident and health plan.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B (Cornell LII)</a></p>","text":"A qualified LTC insurance contract is treated as an accident and health insurance contract. Benefits: Generally not taxed, but periodic payments above the greater of $175 per day (inflation-adjusted) or actual qualified LTC costs are taxable. Employer plans: An employer LTC plan is treated as an accident and health plan."},{"id":"ch16-s13","title":"12. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage</td><td>Supplements Original Medicare vs. used instead of it. Can't have both</td></tr><tr><td>Plan F vs. Plan G</td><td>Both cover Part B excess charges. F (and C) closed to people newly eligible on or after 1/1/2020</td></tr><tr><td>Plan K vs. Plan L</td><td>50% vs. 75% of most cost sharing. 2026 limit: $8,000 vs. $4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare SELECT vs. standard Medigap</td><td>Network for full benefits (emergencies excepted) vs. no network. 12 months to switch</td></tr><tr><td>Open enrollment vs. LA birthday rule</td><td>6 months, one time vs. 63 days every birthday</td></tr><tr><td>Skilled nursing vs. long-term care</td><td>Part A covers short-term vs. Medicare doesn't pay</td></tr><tr><td>Medicaid suspension vs. reinstatement</td><td>Notify within 90 days of Medicaid vs. within 90 days of losing it</td></tr><tr><td>LTC preexisting look-back vs. exclusion</td><td>6 months before vs. 6 months after the effective date</td></tr><tr><td>Medigap/LTC free look vs. LA health free look</td><td>30 days vs. 10 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/how-medigap-works\">Medicare.gov: Learn How Medigap Works</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits\">Medicare.gov: Compare Medigap Plan Benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395ss\">42 U.S.C. 1395ss (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=1296623\">La. R.S. 22:1112</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care\">Medicare.gov: Skilled nursing facility care</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care\">Medicare.gov: Long-term care</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507810\">La. R.S. 22:973</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage Supplements Original Medicare vs. used instead of it. Can't have both Plan F vs. Plan G Both cover Part B excess charges. F (and C) closed to people newly eligible on or after 1/1/2020 Plan K vs. Plan L 50% vs. 75% of most cost sharing. 2026 limit: $8,000 vs. $4,000 Medicare SELECT vs. standard Medigap Network for full benefits (emergencies excepted) vs. no network. 12 months to switch Open enrollment vs. LA birthday rule 6 months, one time vs. 63 days every birthday Skilled nursing vs. long-term care Part A covers short-term vs. Medicare doesn't pay Medicaid suspension vs. reinstatement Notify within 90 days of Medicaid vs. within 90 days of losing it LTC preexisting look-back vs. exclusion 6 months before vs. 6 months after the effective date Medigap/LTC free look vs. LA health free look 30 days vs. 10 days"},{"id":"ch16-s14","title":"13. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How long is Medigap Open Enrollment, and what starts it?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Which Medigap plans are closed to people newly eligible on or after January 1, 2020?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What does Louisiana's Medigap birthday rule allow?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> How is the Part D late enrollment penalty figured?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Name the five Medicare Advantage plan types listed by Medicare.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What makes someone chronically ill under a tax-qualified LTC contract?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> List the six ADLs.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> How long is the Louisiana LTC free look?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What are Louisiana's LTC preexisting-condition limits?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> When can an LTC policy be contested only for intentional misrepresentation?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits\">Medicare.gov: Compare Medigap Plan Benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=1296623\">La. R.S. 22:1112</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d/basics/costs\">Medicare.gov: Costs for Medicare drug coverage</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans/your-health-plan-options\">Medicare.gov: Your health plan options</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507979\">La. R.S. 22:1187</a></p>","text":"1. How long is Medigap Open Enrollment, and what starts it? 2. Which Medigap plans are closed to people newly eligible on or after January 1, 2020? 3. What does Louisiana's Medigap birthday rule allow? 4. How is the Part D late enrollment penalty figured? 5. Name the five Medicare Advantage plan types listed by Medicare. 6. What makes someone chronically ill under a tax-qualified LTC contract? 7. List the six ADLs. 8. How long is the Louisiana LTC free look? 9. What are Louisiana's LTC preexisting-condition limits? 10. When can an LTC policy be contested only for intentional misrepresentation?"},{"id":"ch16-s15","title":"14. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 6 months, starting the first month you have Part B and are 65 or older. It happens once.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Plans C and F.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> For 63 days from each birthday, a policyholder can buy any Medigap policy from the same issuer, with no underwriting for an equal or lesser plan letter.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 1% × the national base beneficiary premium ($38.99 in 2026) × full months without creditable coverage. Added for as long as you have Part D.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> HMO, PPO, Special Needs Plan, Medical Savings Account, Private Fee-for-Service.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Can't perform at least 2 ADLs without substantial assistance for at least 90 days, or needs substantial supervision due to severe cognitive impairment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> 30 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> 6-month look-back; exclusion only for losses within 6 months after the effective date.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> After 2 years in force.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics\">Medicare.gov: Get Medigap Basics</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits\">Medicare.gov: Compare Medigap Plan Benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=1296623\">La. R.S. 22:1112</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d/basics/costs\">Medicare.gov: Costs for Medicare drug coverage</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans/your-health-plan-options\">Medicare.gov: Your health plan options</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702B\">26 U.S.C. 7702B (Cornell LII)</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507979\">La. R.S. 22:1187</a></p>","text":"1. 6 months, starting the first month you have Part B and are 65 or older. It happens once. 2. Plans C and F. 3. For 63 days from each birthday, a policyholder can buy any Medigap policy from the same issuer, with no underwriting for an equal or lesser plan letter. 4. 1% × the national base beneficiary premium ($38.99 in 2026) × full months without creditable coverage. Added for as long as you have Part D. 5. HMO, PPO, Special Needs Plan, Medical Savings Account, Private Fee-for-Service. 6. Can't perform at least 2 ADLs without substantial assistance for at least 90 days, or needs substantial supervision due to severe cognitive impairment. 7. Eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing, continence. 8. 30 days. 9. 6-month look-back; exclusion only for losses within 6 months after the effective date. 10. After 2 years in force."}]},{"id":"ch17","short":"Ch 17","title":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","label":"Chapter 17","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Louisiana R.S. 22:975 lists <strong>14 required provisions</strong> and <strong>10 optional provisions</strong> for individual health and accident policies. Most exam questions here are time limits: notice of claim, claim forms, proof of loss, and legal actions.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","sections":[{"id":"ch17-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana R.S. 22:975(A) lists the provisions every health and accident policy must contain. R.S. 22:975(B) lists optional ones.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Grace period (Louisiana):</strong> Health: 30 days. Life: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Clock rules (health):</strong> Notice of claim 20 days. Claim forms 15 days. Proof of loss 90 days. Wait 60 days before suing. No suit after 1 year from when proof of loss was due.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Louisiana R.S. 22:975(A) lists the provisions every health and accident policy must contain. R.S. 22:975(B) lists optional ones. Grace period (Louisiana): Health: 30 days. Life: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option. Clock rules (health): Notice of claim 20 days. Claim forms 15 days. Proof of loss 90 days. Wait 60 days before suing. No suit after 1 year from when proof of loss was due."},{"id":"ch17-s2","title":"1. NAIC model law","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana's required and optional health and accident policy provisions are in <strong>R.S. 22:975</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Group and blanket health policies are exempt from R.S. 22:975 only if their notice, proof-of-loss, payment-timing, and suit-limitation terms are not less favorable to insureds than the individual rules (R.S. 22:986).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"Louisiana's required and optional health and accident policy provisions are in R.S. 22:975 . Group and blanket health policies are exempt from R.S. 22:975 only if their notice, proof-of-loss, payment-timing, and suit-limitation terms are not less favorable to insureds than the individual rules (R.S. 22:986)."},{"id":"ch17-s3","title":"2. Required provisions: the time limits","html":"<h4>1. Entire contract</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The policy, endorsements, and attached papers make up the whole contract (for industrial insurance, the written application too).</li><li class=\"l0\">Producers CANNOT change the policy or waive its terms.</li><li class=\"l0\">A change is valid only when approved by an executive officer of the insurer and endorsed on or attached to the policy.</li></ul><h4>2. Time limit on certain defenses (incontestability)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana health and accident: after <strong>3 years</strong>, misstatements in the application (other than fraudulent ones) cannot void the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">After 3 years, a claim cannot be reduced or denied because of a preexisting condition that was not excluded by name or specific description.</li><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana life insurance: incontestable after <strong>2 years</strong> from issue during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931).</li></ul><h4>3. Grace period (CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana life:</strong> <strong>30 days, or one month at the insurer's option</strong>, for premiums after the first (R.S. 22:931).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana health and accident:</strong> <strong>30 days</strong> from the premium due date (R.S. 22:977).</li><li class=\"l0\">For individual health policies, if the premium is still unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer must mail a first-class notice that the policy will lapse and can be reinstated.</li><li class=\"l0\">If a life claim arises during the grace period, the unpaid premium (plus interest of up to 6% a year) may be deducted from the payout.</li></ul><h4>4. Reinstatement</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life:</strong> Within <strong>3 years</strong> of default. Requires an application, evidence of insurability (including good health), payment of overdue premiums, and repayment of policy debts with interest (R.S. 22:931).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Health:</strong> If the insurer accepts an overdue premium, the policy is reinstated. It covers accidental injury after reinstatement and sickness that begins more than <strong>10 days</strong> after the premium is accepted.</li></ul><h4>5. Notice of claim</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Written notice within <strong>20 days</strong> after the accident or the start of disability (10 days for industrial policies).</li><li class=\"l0\">Late notice does not invalidate the claim if timely notice was not reasonably possible and notice is given as soon as reasonably possible.</li></ul><h4>6. Claim forms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">After notice, the insurer must furnish proof-of-loss forms.</li><li class=\"l0\">If it does not within <strong>15 days</strong>, the claimant can instead submit written proof of the loss.</li></ul><h4>7. Proof of loss</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Due within <strong>90 days</strong> after the loss (for loss-of-time disability, after the end of the period of liability).</li><li class=\"l0\">Late proof does not invalidate the claim if timely proof was not reasonably possible, but it must be given no later than <strong>1 year</strong> after it was otherwise required.</li></ul><h4>8. Time of payment of claims</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Indemnity other than disability loss of time is paid immediately on receipt of written proof.</li><li class=\"l0\">Disability loss-of-time benefits are paid at least <strong>monthly</strong>.</li></ul><h4>9. Payment of claims</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Life indemnity goes to the beneficiary; if none survives, to the insured's estate. Other indemnity goes to the insured.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the beneficiary or insured is a minor or cannot give a valid release, the policy may allow payment of up to <strong>$1,000</strong> to a relative.</li></ul><h4>10. Physical exam and autopsy</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may examine the insured as often as reasonably required while a claim is pending.</li><li class=\"l0\">It may perform an autopsy after death where the law allows.</li></ul><h4>11. Legal actions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">No suit until <strong>60 days</strong> after proof of loss is filed.</li><li class=\"l0\">No suit after <strong>1 year</strong> from the time proof of loss was required to be filed.</li></ul><h4>12. Change of beneficiary</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The beneficiary's consent is not required for a change of beneficiary, or for surrender or assignment of the policy.</li></ul><h4>Quick reference (Louisiana health and accident)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grace period</td><td>Health 30 days; life 30 days or one month</td></tr><tr><td>Incontestability</td><td>Health 3 years; life 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Notice of claim</td><td>20 days</td></tr><tr><td>Claim forms</td><td>If not furnished in 15 days, claimant may submit written proof</td></tr><tr><td>Proof of loss</td><td>90 days (no later than 1 year if late for good cause)</td></tr><tr><td>Legal actions</td><td>Wait 60 days; no suit after 1 year from when proof was due</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement (health)</td><td>Sickness covered if it begins more than 10 days after premium accepted</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (Louisiana health clocks):</strong> 20 (notice), 15 (forms), 90 (proof), 60 (wait to sue), 1 year (suit limit).</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"1. Entire contract The policy, endorsements, and attached papers make up the whole contract (for industrial insurance, the written application too). Producers CANNOT change the policy or waive its terms. A change is valid only when approved by an executive officer of the insurer and endorsed on or attached to the policy. 2. Time limit on certain defenses (incontestability) Louisiana health and accident: after 3 years , misstatements in the application (other than fraudulent ones) cannot void the policy. After 3 years, a claim cannot be reduced or denied because of a preexisting condition that was not excluded by name or specific description. Louisiana life insurance: incontestable after 2 years from issue during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931). 3. Grace period (CRITICAL) Louisiana life: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option , for premiums after the first (R.S. 22:931). Louisiana health and accident: 30 days from the premium due date (R.S. 22:977). For individual health policies, if the premium is still unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer must mail a first-class notice that the policy will lapse and can be reinstated. If a life claim arises during the grace period, the unpaid premium (plus interest of up to 6% a year) may be deducted from the payout. 4. Reinstatement Life: Within 3 years of default. Requires an application, evidence of insurability (including good health), payment of overdue premiums, and repayment of policy debts with interest (R.S. 22:931). Health: If the insurer accepts an overdue premium, the policy is reinstated. It covers accidental injury after reinstatement and sickness that begins more than 10 days after the premium is accepted. 5. Notice of claim Written notice within 20 days after the accident or the start of disability (10 days for industrial policies). Late notice does not invalidate the claim if timely notice was not reasonably possible and notice is given as soon as reasonably possible. 6. Claim forms After notice, the insurer must furnish proof-of-loss forms. If it does not within 15 days , the claimant can instead submit written proof of the loss. 7. Proof of loss Due within 90 days after the loss (for loss-of-time disability, after the end of the period of liability). Late proof does not invalidate the claim if timely proof was not reasonably possible, but it must be given no later than 1 year after it was otherwise required. 8. Time of payment of claims Indemnity other than disability loss of time is paid immediately on receipt of written proof. Disability loss-of-time benefits are paid at least monthly . 9. Payment of claims Life indemnity goes to the beneficiary; if none survives, to the insured's estate. Other indemnity goes to the insured. If the beneficiary or insured is a minor or cannot give a valid release, the policy may allow payment of up to $1,000 to a relative. 10. Physical exam and autopsy The insurer may examine the insured as often as reasonably required while a claim is pending. It may perform an autopsy after death where the law allows. 11. Legal actions No suit until 60 days after proof of loss is filed. No suit after 1 year from the time proof of loss was required to be filed. 12. Change of beneficiary The beneficiary's consent is not required for a change of beneficiary, or for surrender or assignment of the policy. Quick reference (Louisiana health and accident) Provision Key number Grace period Health 30 days; life 30 days or one month Incontestability Health 3 years; life 2 years Notice of claim 20 days Claim forms If not furnished in 15 days, claimant may submit written proof Proof of loss 90 days (no later than 1 year if late for good cause) Legal actions Wait 60 days; no suit after 1 year from when proof was due Reinstatement (health) Sickness covered if it begins more than 10 days after premium accepted Memory trick Memory trick (Louisiana health clocks): 20 (notice), 15 (forms), 90 (proof), 60 (wait to sue), 1 year (suit limit)."},{"id":"ch17-s4","title":"3. The 10 optional provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Provision</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Change of occupation</strong></td><td>After a move to a more hazardous occupation, coverage is limited to what the premium would have bought at that occupation's rates</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Misstatement of age</strong></td><td>Benefits are adjusted to the correct age</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Other insurance with this insurer</strong></td><td>Excess coverage under concurrent policies from the same insurer may be void, with premiums refunded</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Other insurance with other insurers</strong></td><td>Without prior written notice, liability is prorated among all policies covering the loss</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Relation of earnings</strong></td><td>Loss-of-time benefits may be limited when total monthly disability benefits across policies exceed the insured's earnings</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Unpaid premiums</strong></td><td>Premium due and unpaid may be deducted when a claim is paid</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Cancellation</strong></td><td>By written notice delivered to the insured or mailed to the last address; unearned premium is refunded</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Conformity with state statutes</strong></td><td>A policy provision that conflicts with state law on the issue date is treated as amended to conform</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Illegal occupation</strong></td><td>No liability for a loss to which the insured's illegal occupation was a contributing cause</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>Intoxicants and narcotics</strong></td><td>No liability for losses caused by intoxication or narcotics, unless given on a physician's advice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (change of occupation):</strong> MORE hazardous job = coverage limited to what the premium would buy at the higher-risk rate.</p></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"# Provision What it does 1 Change of occupation After a move to a more hazardous occupation, coverage is limited to what the premium would have bought at that occupation's rates 2 Misstatement of age Benefits are adjusted to the correct age 3 Other insurance with this insurer Excess coverage under concurrent policies from the same insurer may be void, with premiums refunded 4 Other insurance with other insurers Without prior written notice, liability is prorated among all policies covering the loss 5 Relation of earnings Loss-of-time benefits may be limited when total monthly disability benefits across policies exceed the insured's earnings 6 Unpaid premiums Premium due and unpaid may be deducted when a claim is paid 7 Cancellation By written notice delivered to the insured or mailed to the last address; unearned premium is refunded 8 Conformity with state statutes A policy provision that conflicts with state law on the issue date is treated as amended to conform 9 Illegal occupation No liability for a loss to which the insured's illegal occupation was a contributing cause 10 Intoxicants and narcotics No liability for losses caused by intoxication or narcotics, unless given on a physician's advice Memory trick Memory trick (change of occupation): MORE hazardous job = coverage limited to what the premium would buy at the higher-risk rate."},{"id":"ch17-s5","title":"4. Other important provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Indemnity</strong></td><td>Compensation for a loss that has occurred</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Free look (Louisiana)</strong></td><td>Health policy must give notice of <strong>10 days</strong> from receipt to examine it. If sold deceptively, it can be surrendered then for a premium refund. Medigap and LTC: <strong>30 days</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Form filing and approval</strong></td><td>Forms filed with LDI. Can't be used until <strong>60 days</strong> after filing unless approved sooner. Aggrieved party may demand a hearing. LDI may withdraw approval after <strong>20 days'</strong> notice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Insuring agreement</strong></td><td>The insurer's promise to pay. Narrowed by exclusions and definitions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consideration</strong></td><td>Value each party exchanges. Louisiana: the consideration and the start and end times must be stated in the policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assignment</strong></td><td>Transfer of policy rights, usually with insurer's written consent. Policy may pay providers directly</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Probationary period</strong></td><td>In some disability income policies, sickness starting soon after inception (e.g., 15–30 days) isn't covered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Elimination period</strong></td><td>Waiting period before disability benefits are payable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/indemnity\">IRMI: Indemnity</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507810\">La. R.S. 22:973</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507922\">La. R.S. 22:1111</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507809\">La. R.S. 22:972</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/insuring-agreement\">IRMI: Insuring Agreement</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/consideration\">IRMI: Consideration</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/assignment\">IRMI: Assignment</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/probationary-period\">IRMI: Probationary Period</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/elimination-period\">IRMI: Elimination Period</a></p>","text":"Provision Key facts Indemnity Compensation for a loss that has occurred Free look (Louisiana) Health policy must give notice of 10 days from receipt to examine it. If sold deceptively, it can be surrendered then for a premium refund. Medigap and LTC: 30 days Form filing and approval Forms filed with LDI. Can't be used until 60 days after filing unless approved sooner. Aggrieved party may demand a hearing. LDI may withdraw approval after 20 days' notice Insuring agreement The insurer's promise to pay. Narrowed by exclusions and definitions Consideration Value each party exchanges. Louisiana: the consideration and the start and end times must be stated in the policy Assignment Transfer of policy rights, usually with insurer's written consent. Policy may pay providers directly Probationary period In some disability income policies, sickness starting soon after inception (e.g., 15–30 days) isn't covered Elimination period Waiting period before disability benefits are payable"},{"id":"ch17-s6","title":"5. Policy renewability types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cancelable</strong></td><td>Insurer or insured can end it at any time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Optionally renewable</strong></td><td>Insurer may refuse renewal on the anniversary; no midterm cancellation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conditionally renewable</strong></td><td>Renewable under conditions in the contract</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed renewable</strong></td><td>Insurer must renew. Premium can change for the whole class</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Noncancelable</strong></td><td>The other usual label for an individual policy that isn't cancelable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> A policy the insured can keep to at least <strong>age 50</strong> (or <strong>5 years</strong> if issued after 44) may use a 3-year incontestable clause.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Grace period:</strong> 30 days. Notice mailed if unpaid 15 days before it ends.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/cancelable\">IRMI: Cancelable</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/optionally-renewable\">IRMI: Optionally Renewable</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/conditionally-renewable\">IRMI: Conditionally Renewable</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/guaranteed-renewable\">IRMI: Guaranteed Renewable</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a></p>","text":"Type What it means Cancelable Insurer or insured can end it at any time Optionally renewable Insurer may refuse renewal on the anniversary; no midterm cancellation Conditionally renewable Renewable under conditions in the contract Guaranteed renewable Insurer must renew. Premium can change for the whole class Noncancelable The other usual label for an individual policy that isn't cancelable Louisiana: A policy the insured can keep to at least age 50 (or 5 years if issued after 44) may use a 3-year incontestable clause. Grace period: 30 days. Notice mailed if unpaid 15 days before it ends."},{"id":"ch17-s7","title":"6. Preexisting conditions","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preexisting:</strong> A health problem you had before coverage starts.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Marketplace plans</strong> must cover it (grandfathered plans excepted).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Health policies can't exclude them.</li><li class=\"l0\">After 3 years, only conditions excluded by name can reduce a claim.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/pre-existing-condition/\">HealthCare.gov glossary: Pre-existing condition</a>; <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/pre-existing-conditions/\">HealthCare.gov: Coverage for pre-existing conditions</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507925\">La. R.S. 22:1123</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"Preexisting: A health problem you had before coverage starts. Marketplace plans must cover it (grandfathered plans excepted). Louisiana: Health policies can't exclude them. After 3 years, only conditions excluded by name can reduce a claim."},{"id":"ch17-s8","title":"7. Common riders","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Waiver of premium</strong></td><td>Insurer gives up its right to collect premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payor benefit</strong></td><td>Premiums waived if the payor dies or is disabled. Often used for a child or spouse</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Living benefits</strong></td><td>Discounted proceeds paid to a terminally ill insured</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accidental death benefit</strong></td><td>Extra death benefit for accidental death. <strong>Double indemnity</strong> pays 2× the face amount</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed insurability</strong></td><td>Buy more coverage later <strong>without</strong> proof of good health</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Return of premium</strong></td><td>Pays the face amount plus premiums at death, usually via increasing term</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LTC exclusion riders</strong></td><td>Louisiana bars riders excluding named conditions beyond the 6-month preexisting period</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver-of-premium\">IRMI: Waiver Of Premium</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/payor-benefit\">IRMI: Payor Benefit</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/living-benefits\">IRMI: Living Benefits</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/accidental-death-benefit\">IRMI: Accidental Death Benefit</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/double-indemnity\">IRMI: Double Indemnity</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/guaranteed-insurability\">IRMI: Guaranteed Insurability</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/return-of-premium\">IRMI: Return Of Premium</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a></p>","text":"Rider Key facts Waiver of premium Insurer gives up its right to collect premium Payor benefit Premiums waived if the payor dies or is disabled. Often used for a child or spouse Living benefits Discounted proceeds paid to a terminally ill insured Accidental death benefit Extra death benefit for accidental death. Double indemnity pays 2× the face amount Guaranteed insurability Buy more coverage later without proof of good health Return of premium Pays the face amount plus premiums at death, usually via increasing term LTC exclusion riders Louisiana bars riders excluding named conditions beyond the 6-month preexisting period"},{"id":"ch17-s11","title":"10. Policy loans and cash value","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Insurers may defer a policy loan for up to <strong>6 months</strong> after the application (term policies of 20 years or less are exempt).</li><li class=\"l0\">At least <strong>30 days'</strong> prior notice is required before a policy terminates because loan debt exceeds the cash value.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></p>","text":"Insurers may defer a policy loan for up to 6 months after the application (term policies of 20 years or less are exempt). At least 30 days' prior notice is required before a policy terminates because loan debt exceeds the cash value."},{"id":"ch17-s12","title":"11. Common exclusions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exclusion</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Suicide and hazardous occupation (life)</strong></td><td>These exclusions may apply only within the first 2 years</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Illegal occupation (health)</strong></td><td>Optional provision: no liability for a loss to which the insured's illegal occupation was a contributing cause</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intoxicants and narcotics (health)</strong></td><td>Optional provision: no liability for a loss from intoxication or narcotics, unless given on a physician's advice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"Exclusion Key facts Suicide and hazardous occupation (life) These exclusions may apply only within the first 2 years Illegal occupation (health) Optional provision: no liability for a loss to which the insured's illegal occupation was a contributing cause Intoxicants and narcotics (health) Optional provision: no liability for a loss from intoxication or narcotics, unless given on a physician's advice"},{"id":"ch17-s13","title":"12. Beneficiary types","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana health and accident: life indemnity goes to the beneficiary; if none survives, to the insured's estate. Other indemnity goes to the insured.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></p>","text":"Louisiana health and accident: life indemnity goes to the beneficiary; if none survives, to the insured's estate. Other indemnity goes to the insured."},{"id":"ch17-s14","title":"13. Dependent coverage","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>ACA dependent rule:</strong> If a plan covers dependents, a young adult can usually stay on a parent's plan until age <strong>26</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Marketplace plans: coverage lasts through December 31 of the year you turn 26.</li><li class=\"l0\">Job-based plans: you can generally join and stay on a parent's plan even if you married.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/young-adults/children-under-26/\">HealthCare.gov: Coverage under 26</a></p>","text":"ACA dependent rule: If a plan covers dependents, a young adult can usually stay on a parent's plan until age 26 . Marketplace plans: coverage lasts through December 31 of the year you turn 26. Job-based plans: you can generally join and stay on a parent's plan even if you married."},{"id":"ch17-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Required vs. optional provisions (LA)</td><td>14 required vs. 10 optional</td></tr><tr><td>Grace period (LA)</td><td>30 days for health and accident</td></tr><tr><td>Free look: health vs. Medigap/LTC</td><td>10 days vs. 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Guaranteed renewable vs. cancelable</td><td>Must renew vs. can end anytime</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement: accident vs. sickness</td><td>Covered after acceptance vs. sickness starting 10+ days later</td></tr><tr><td>Notice of claim vs. proof of loss</td><td>20 days vs. 90 days (1-year outer limit)</td></tr><tr><td>Legal action: earliest vs. latest</td><td>60 days after proof vs. 1 year after proof is due</td></tr><tr><td>Change of occupation vs. cancellation by insured</td><td>More hazardous: benefit cut vs. less hazardous: premium cut or refund</td></tr><tr><td>Payor benefit vs. waiver of premium</td><td>Payor dies or is disabled vs. insurer waives premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507810\">La. R.S. 22:973</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507922\">La. R.S. 22:1111</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/guaranteed-renewable\">IRMI: Guaranteed Renewable</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/cancelable\">IRMI: Cancelable</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/payor-benefit\">IRMI: Payor Benefit</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/waiver-of-premium\">IRMI: Waiver Of Premium</a></p>","text":"Pair Key difference Required vs. optional provisions (LA) 14 required vs. 10 optional Grace period (LA) 30 days for health and accident Free look: health vs. Medigap/LTC 10 days vs. 30 days Guaranteed renewable vs. cancelable Must renew vs. can end anytime Reinstatement: accident vs. sickness Covered after acceptance vs. sickness starting 10+ days later Notice of claim vs. proof of loss 20 days vs. 90 days (1-year outer limit) Legal action: earliest vs. latest 60 days after proof vs. 1 year after proof is due Change of occupation vs. cancellation by insured More hazardous: benefit cut vs. less hazardous: premium cut or refund Payor benefit vs. waiver of premium Payor dies or is disabled vs. insurer waives premium"},{"id":"ch17-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> In Louisiana, how long a grace period must a health and accident policy give?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> How soon after a loss is written notice of claim due?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What happens if the insurer doesn't send claim forms in time?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> When is proof of loss due, and what is the outer limit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What waiting rules apply to a lawsuit on the policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> How long is the free look for a Louisiana health policy? For Medigap and LTC?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> After reinstatement, when is sickness covered?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Which optional provision cuts benefits after a move to riskier work?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Who can approve a change to the policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Until what age can a young adult stay on a parent's plan?</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507810\">La. R.S. 22:973</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507922\">La. R.S. 22:1111</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/young-adults/children-under-26/\">HealthCare.gov: People under 30</a></p>","text":"1. In Louisiana, how long a grace period must a health and accident policy give? 2. How soon after a loss is written notice of claim due? 3. What happens if the insurer doesn't send claim forms in time? 4. When is proof of loss due, and what is the outer limit? 5. What waiting rules apply to a lawsuit on the policy? 6. How long is the free look for a Louisiana health policy? For Medigap and LTC? 7. After reinstatement, when is sickness covered? 8. Which optional provision cuts benefits after a move to riskier work? 9. Who can approve a change to the policy? 10. Until what age can a young adult stay on a parent's plan?"},{"id":"ch17-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 30 days from the due date.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Within 20 days, or as soon as reasonably possible.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> After 15 days, the claimant can file written proof without them.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Within 90 days; if not reasonably possible, no later than 1 year after it was due.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Not before 60 days after proof is filed; not after 1 year from when proof is due.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 10 days; 30 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Only for sickness starting more than 10 days after the premium is accepted.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Change of occupation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> An executive officer of the insurer, endorsed on the policy. Producers can't.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Usually until 26.</p><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">La. R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507810\">La. R.S. 22:973</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507922\">La. R.S. 22:1111</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507978\">La. R.S. 22:1186</a>; <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/young-adults/children-under-26/\">HealthCare.gov: People under 30</a></p>","text":"1. 30 days from the due date. 2. Within 20 days, or as soon as reasonably possible. 3. After 15 days, the claimant can file written proof without them. 4. Within 90 days; if not reasonably possible, no later than 1 year after it was due. 5. Not before 60 days after proof is filed; not after 1 year from when proof is due. 6. 10 days; 30 days. 7. Only for sickness starting more than 10 days after the premium is accepted. 8. Change of occupation. 9. An executive officer of the insurer, endorsed on the policy. Producers can't. 10. Usually until 26."}]},{"id":"ch18","short":"Ch 18","title":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","label":"Chapter 18","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> This chapter is the health version of Chapter 4. Know the <strong>application</strong> (3 parts), <strong>representations vs. warranties</strong>, the <strong>MIB</strong>, the <strong>risk classes</strong>, the <strong>premium receipt types</strong>, <strong>delivery</strong> (constructive delivery, free look), and <strong>premium and rating</strong> basics. Expect questions on receipts and on representation vs. warranty.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch18-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Underwriting protects against adverse selection.</strong> People who expect losses buy insurance more often than people who expect few, which unbalances the risk pool.</li><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>warranty</strong> is a factual statement made to the insurer. If it is false, the policy may be voided.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/warranty\">IRMI: Warranty</a></p>","text":"Underwriting protects against adverse selection. People who expect losses buy insurance more often than people who expect few, which unbalances the risk pool. A warranty is a factual statement made to the insurer. If it is false, the policy may be voided."},{"id":"ch18-s2","title":"1. Underwriting overview","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evaluate and select risks. A primary goal is protection against <strong>adverse selection</strong>.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection\">IRMI: Adverse selection</a></p>","text":"Purpose: Evaluate and select risks. A primary goal is protection against adverse selection ."},{"id":"ch18-s4","title":"3. Representations vs. warranties (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Warranty</td><td>A factual statement given to the insurer about the risk. If it is false, the policy may be voided</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/warranty\">IRMI: Warranty</a></p>","text":"Term Key fact Warranty A factual statement given to the insurer about the risk. If it is false, the policy may be voided"},{"id":"ch18-s5","title":"4. Medical Information Bureau (MIB)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">MIB lets consumers request their MIB Consumer File and learn how to dispute it.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mib.com/\">MIB</a></p>","text":"MIB lets consumers request their MIB Consumer File and learn how to dispute it."},{"id":"ch18-s7","title":"6. Laws and requirements","html":"<h4>FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Users of reports for credit, insurance, or employment must notify the consumer when they take adverse action based on a report.</li><li class=\"l0\">The notice must name the consumer reporting agency and say the agency did not make the decision.</li><li class=\"l0\">The consumer can get a free copy of the report within 60 days and dispute its accuracy or completeness with the agency.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act\">FTC: Fair Credit Reporting Act</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1681m\">15 U.S.C. 1681m</a></p>","text":"FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act) Users of reports for credit, insurance, or employment must notify the consumer when they take adverse action based on a report. The notice must name the consumer reporting agency and say the agency did not make the decision. The consumer can get a free copy of the report within 60 days and dispute its accuracy or completeness with the agency."},{"id":"ch18-s11","title":"10. Policy delivery","html":"<h4>Free look</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana requires a notice that <strong>10 days</strong> are allowed, from actual receipt of the contract, to examine it.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the contract is not as explained or understood, it may be surrendered within the 10 days, and any advance premium must be returned immediately.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507805\">La. R.S. 22:951</a></p>","text":"Free look Louisiana requires a notice that 10 days are allowed, from actual receipt of the contract, to examine it. If the contract is not as explained or understood, it may be surrendered within the 10 days, and any advance premium must be returned immediately."},{"id":"ch18-s13","title":"12. Premium calculations","html":"<h4>Earned vs. unearned premium</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Unearned premium</strong> is the part of the premium not yet earned because coverage still has time to run. It is recorded as a <strong>liability</strong>, since part may be refunded if the policy is canceled.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/unearned-premium\">IRMI: Unearned premium</a></p>","text":"Earned vs. unearned premium Unearned premium is the part of the premium not yet earned because coverage still has time to run. It is recorded as a liability , since part may be refunded if the policy is canceled."},{"id":"ch18-s16","title":"15. Tax treatment (health)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Employer-paid premiums</strong></td><td>Employer-provided accident or health coverage is generally NOT in the employee's gross income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employer-paid benefits</strong></td><td>Benefits attributable to employer contributions are generally INCLUDED in the employee's gross income</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/106\">26 U.S.C. 106</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/105\">26 U.S.C. 105</a></p>","text":"Item Treatment Employer-paid premiums Employer-provided accident or health coverage is generally NOT in the employee's gross income Employer-paid benefits Benefits attributable to employer contributions are generally INCLUDED in the employee's gross income"}]},{"id":"ch19","short":"Bonus","title":"Bonus - Social Insurance Study Tool Slides (25 screenshots)","label":"Bonus: Study Tool Slides","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>What this is:</strong> A summary of the 25-slide &quot;Social Insurance Study Tool&quot; (Medicare Parts A to D, Medicaid, and Social Security). The topics overlap with other chapters, so this section doubles as a fast review.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch19-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare:</strong> Part A is usually premium-free with about 10 years of Medicare-taxed work. Part B has a 20% coinsurance after the deductible. Part C (Medicare Advantage) is a private plan that requires both A and B. Part D covers drugs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Initial enrollment window = 7 months</strong> (3 before the 65th birthday month, the birthday month, and 3 after).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs\">Medicare.gov: Medicare costs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options\">Medicare.gov: Your coverage options</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start\">Medicare.gov: When does coverage start</a></p>","text":"Medicare: Part A is usually premium-free with about 10 years of Medicare-taxed work. Part B has a 20% coinsurance after the deductible. Part C (Medicare Advantage) is a private plan that requires both A and B. Part D covers drugs. Initial enrollment window = 7 months (3 before the 65th birthday month, the birthday month, and 3 after)."},{"id":"ch19-s2","title":"1. Slides 1 to 2: Welcome and Medicare introduction","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Medicare entitlement starts at <strong>age 65</strong>, or for people under 65 after <strong>24 months</strong> of disability benefits. The 24-month wait is waived for ALS.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/426\">42 U.S.C. 426</a></p>","text":"Medicare entitlement starts at age 65 , or for people under 65 after 24 months of disability benefits. The 24-month wait is waived for ALS."},{"id":"ch19-s3","title":"2. Slides 3 to 6: Medicare Parts A, B, C, D","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Eligibility and costs</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong> (hospital)</td><td>Usually premium-free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes for about <strong>10 years</strong></td><td>Per benefit period: coinsurance starts on day <strong>61</strong> in a hospital and day <strong>21</strong> in a skilled nursing facility</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong> (medical)</td><td>Monthly premium and annual deductible</td><td><strong>20% coinsurance</strong> after the deductible. Late enrollment: premium rises <strong>10% for each full 12 months</strong> of delay</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong> (Medicare Advantage)</td><td>You must have both Part A and Part B</td><td>Private plan alternative to Original Medicare. Most include Part D. Many add dental, vision, hearing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong> (drugs)</td><td>Available to everyone with Medicare</td><td>Annual out-of-pocket cap ($2,100 in 2026). Late penalty if 63 days or more without creditable drug coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Exam tips</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Initial enrollment window = 7 months</strong> (3 before, the month of, and 3 after the 65th birthday).</li><li class=\"l0\">Late penalties apply to Parts B and D.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs\">Medicare.gov: Medicare costs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options\">Medicare.gov: Your coverage options</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start\">Medicare.gov: When does coverage start</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/408.22\">42 CFR 408.22</a></p>","text":"Part Eligibility and costs Key facts A (hospital) Usually premium-free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes for about 10 years Per benefit period: coinsurance starts on day 61 in a hospital and day 21 in a skilled nursing facility B (medical) Monthly premium and annual deductible 20% coinsurance after the deductible. Late enrollment: premium rises 10% for each full 12 months of delay C (Medicare Advantage) You must have both Part A and Part B Private plan alternative to Original Medicare. Most include Part D. Many add dental, vision, hearing D (drugs) Available to everyone with Medicare Annual out-of-pocket cap ($2,100 in 2026). Late penalty if 63 days or more without creditable drug coverage Exam tips Initial enrollment window = 7 months (3 before, the month of, and 3 after the 65th birthday). Late penalties apply to Parts B and D."},{"id":"ch19-s5","title":"4. Slides 18 to 19: Social Security retirement","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Retirement:</strong> earliest age <strong>62</strong>. <strong>Full retirement age (FRA):</strong> 65 if born before 1938; 66 for 1943 to 1954; 67 for 1960 and later. It rises by months in between.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fully insured</strong> = <strong>40 quarters</strong> of coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spouse benefit:</strong> up to one-half of the worker's primary insurance amount (it can be reduced for early claiming).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/416\">42 U.S.C. 416</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/414\">42 U.S.C. 414</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/402\">42 U.S.C. 402</a></p>","text":"Retirement: earliest age 62 . Full retirement age (FRA): 65 if born before 1938; 66 for 1943 to 1954; 67 for 1960 and later. It rises by months in between. Fully insured = 40 quarters of coverage. Spouse benefit: up to one-half of the worker's primary insurance amount (it can be reduced for early claiming)."},{"id":"ch19-s6","title":"5. Slides 20 to 21: Disability and survivor benefits (Social Security)","html":"<h4>Disability (SSDI)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Disability must last or be expected to last <strong>12 months or more</strong>, or result in death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>5 consecutive calendar months</strong> waiting period. For ALS, benefits begin with the first month of disability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare starts after 24 months</strong> of disability benefits. ALS is exempt.</li></ul><h4>Survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Widow(er):</strong> age <strong>60</strong>, or age <strong>50</strong> if disabled.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Children:</strong> under 18, or under 19 if a full-time elementary or secondary student.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disabled children:</strong> disability began before age 22.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/423\">42 U.S.C. 423</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/426\">42 U.S.C. 426</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/402\">42 U.S.C. 402</a></p>","text":"Disability (SSDI) Disability must last or be expected to last 12 months or more , or result in death. 5 consecutive calendar months waiting period. For ALS, benefits begin with the first month of disability. Medicare starts after 24 months of disability benefits. ALS is exempt. Survivor benefits Widow(er): age 60 , or age 50 if disabled. Children: under 18, or under 19 if a full-time elementary or secondary student. Disabled children: disability began before age 22."},{"id":"ch19-s8","title":"7. Slides 23 to 24: Social Security comparison chart and exam tips","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Key notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Retirement</strong></td><td>Earliest age 62; 40 quarters = fully insured</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability (SSDI)</strong></td><td>5-month waiting period; Medicare after 24 months</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Survivor</strong></td><td>Widow(er) at 60 (50 if disabled); children under 18 (19 if in school)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/416\">42 U.S.C. 416</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/414\">42 U.S.C. 414</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/423\">42 U.S.C. 423</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/426\">42 U.S.C. 426</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/402\">42 U.S.C. 402</a></p>","text":"Benefit Key notes Retirement Earliest age 62; 40 quarters = fully insured Disability (SSDI) 5-month waiting period; Medicare after 24 months Survivor Widow(er) at 60 (50 if disabled); children under 18 (19 if in school)"},{"id":"ch19-s10","title":"9. Quick comparison","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part A premium</td><td>Usually free (about 10 years of Medicare-taxed work)</td></tr><tr><td>Part B late penalty</td><td>10% increase for each full 12 months</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare enrollment</td><td>7-month window (3 months before, birth month, 3 months after)</td></tr><tr><td>FRA</td><td>65 if born before 1938; 66 for 1943 to 1954; 67 for 1960 and later</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI waiting period</td><td>5 consecutive calendar months</td></tr><tr><td>Widow(er) early benefits</td><td>Age 60, or 50 if disabled</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs\">Medicare.gov: Medicare costs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start\">Medicare.gov: When does coverage start</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/42/408.22\">42 CFR 408.22</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/416\">42 U.S.C. 416</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/423\">42 U.S.C. 423</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/402\">42 U.S.C. 402</a></p>","text":"Topic Key fact Part A premium Usually free (about 10 years of Medicare-taxed work) Part B late penalty 10% increase for each full 12 months Medicare enrollment 7-month window (3 months before, birth month, 3 months after) FRA 65 if born before 1938; 66 for 1943 to 1954; 67 for 1960 and later SSDI waiting period 5 consecutive calendar months Widow(er) early benefits Age 60, or 50 if disabled"}]},{"id":"addendum","short":"Addendum","title":"Addendum: Exam Blueprint and Louisiana Regulation","label":"Addendum: Louisiana Regulation and Exam Blueprint","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>About this addendum:</strong> Louisiana regulation is the largest single domain on the combined Series 103 exam. This section gives the exam blueprint and the Louisiana rules you need to know.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"addendum-s1","title":"1. Exam blueprint (PSI)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exam:</strong> A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score: <strong>at least 70%</strong> (R.S. 22:1545(A)).</li><li class=\"l0\">The exam tests knowledge of the lines of authority, producer duties and responsibilities, and Louisiana insurance laws and regulations (R.S. 22:1545(A)).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508519\">R.S. 22:1545</a></p>","text":"Exam: A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score: at least 70% (R.S. 22:1545(A)). The exam tests knowledge of the lines of authority, producer duties and responsibilities, and Louisiana insurance laws and regulations (R.S. 22:1545(A))."},{"id":"addendum-s2","title":"2. Louisiana regulation (Series 103: 23%)","html":"<h4>2.1 Where the rules live</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana's insurance statutes are in <strong>Title 22 of the Louisiana Revised Statutes</strong> (the Insurance Code). Administrative rules are in <strong>Title 37 of the Louisiana Administrative Code</strong>, Part XIII (insurance regulations).</li></ul><h4>2.2 Licensing and continuing education</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exam:</strong> A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score <strong>70%</strong> (R.S. 22:1545(A)).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lines of authority:</strong> R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists <strong>variable life and variable annuity</strong> as a separate line of authority.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Continuing education (life, accident, health producers):</strong> <strong>24 hours</strong> every <strong>2 years</strong>, including <strong>at least 3 ethics hours</strong> (R.S. 22:1573(C); cycle R.S. 22:1547(C)). Combination license holders must include at least <strong>2 hours</strong> on legislative updates.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuity training:</strong> Producers who sell annuities complete a <strong>one-time, four-credit</strong> department-approved course (LAC 37:XIII-11712).</li></ul><h4>2.3 Louisiana policy rules (life and health)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Louisiana value</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Life free look</td><td><strong>10 days from receipt</strong> of the policy</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Life incontestability</td><td><strong>2 years</strong> in force during the insured's lifetime</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Life grace period</td><td><strong>30 days, or one month</strong> at the insurer's option</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Life reinstatement</td><td>Within <strong>3 years</strong> of default, with evidence of insurability and payment</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(9)</td></tr><tr><td>Health incontestability</td><td><strong>3 years</strong></td><td>R.S. 22:975</td></tr><tr><td>Health grace period</td><td><strong>30 days</strong></td><td>R.S. 22:977</td></tr><tr><td>Health premium notice</td><td>If unpaid <strong>15 days</strong> before the grace period ends, the insurer mails a first-class notice of lapse and reinstatement rights</td><td>R.S. 22:977</td></tr><tr><td>Health reinstatement</td><td>Accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness covered again after 10 days</td><td>R.S. 22:975(A)(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum age to contract</td><td>A minor <strong>15 or older</strong> (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance</td><td>R.S. 22:852(B)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>2.4 Guaranty association</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life:</strong> death benefits <strong>$300,000</strong>; cash surrender and withdrawal values <strong>$100,000</strong>; health benefits <strong>$500,000</strong>; annuity present value <strong>$250,000</strong>. Aggregate cap <strong>$500,000</strong> for any one individual.</li></ul><h4>2.5 Replacement and annuity sales</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Replacement:</strong> Louisiana Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 89) requires a replacement notice (Appendix C), signed by the applicant.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuity sales standard:</strong> Louisiana Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a <strong>best-interest</strong> standard for annuity sales and recommendations.</li></ul><h4>2.6 Unfair and deceptive practices</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:1964</strong> lists unfair or deceptive acts. Examples: misrepresenting policy provisions, false advertising, boycott, coercion and intimidation, and unfair claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14, examples):</strong> not responding promptly to communications; denying a claim without a reasonable investigation; not affirming or denying coverage within a reasonable time after proof of loss; not explaining a denial; not providing claim forms within 15 days; not giving the claim file on written request.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024.</strong> The good-faith duty is now in <strong>R.S. 22:1892(I)</strong>: the insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing.</li></ul><h4>2.7 Federal tax items</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Standard federal tax points for life and annuities are summarized in Section 3.5 below.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507812\">R.S. 22:975</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507814\">R.S. 22:977</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507733\">R.S. 22:852</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509204\">R.S. 22:2083</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509078\">R.S. 22:1964</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509041\">R.S. 22:1892</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509087\">R.S. 22:1973</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508519\">R.S. 22:1545</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508521\">R.S. 22:1547</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508549\">R.S. 22:1573</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/louisiana/La-Admin-Code-tit-37-SS-XIII-8925\">LAC 37:XIII-8925</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/louisiana/La-Admin-Code-tit-37-SS-XIII-11711\">LAC 37:XIII-11711</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/louisiana/La-Admin-Code-tit-37-SS-XIII-11712\">LAC 37:XIII-11712</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/louisiana/title-37/part-XIII\">LAC Title 37, Part XIII</a></p>","text":"2.1 Where the rules live Louisiana's insurance statutes are in Title 22 of the Louisiana Revised Statutes (the Insurance Code). Administrative rules are in Title 37 of the Louisiana Administrative Code , Part XIII (insurance regulations). 2.2 Licensing and continuing education Exam: A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score 70% (R.S. 22:1545(A)). Lines of authority: R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists variable life and variable annuity as a separate line of authority. Continuing education (life, accident, health producers): 24 hours every 2 years , including at least 3 ethics hours (R.S. 22:1573(C); cycle R.S. 22:1547(C)). Combination license holders must include at least 2 hours on legislative updates. Annuity training: Producers who sell annuities complete a one-time, four-credit department-approved course (LAC 37:XIII-11712). 2.3 Louisiana policy rules (life and health) Rule Louisiana value Statute Life free look 10 days from receipt of the policy R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a) Life incontestability 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime R.S. 22:931(A)(2) Life grace period 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option R.S. 22:931(A)(1) Life reinstatement Within 3 years of default, with evidence of insurability and payment R.S. 22:931(A)(9) Health incontestability 3 years R.S. 22:975 Health grace period 30 days R.S. 22:977 Health premium notice If unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer mails a first-class notice of lapse and reinstatement rights R.S. 22:977 Health reinstatement Accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness covered again after 10 days R.S. 22:975(A)(2) Minimum age to contract A minor 15 or older (nearest birthday) may contract for life, health, or accident insurance R.S. 22:852(B) 2.4 Guaranty association R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life: death benefits $300,000 ; cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000 ; health benefits $500,000 ; annuity present value $250,000 . Aggregate cap $500,000 for any one individual. 2.5 Replacement and annuity sales Replacement: Louisiana Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 89) requires a replacement notice (Appendix C), signed by the applicant. Annuity sales standard: Louisiana Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a best-interest standard for annuity sales and recommendations. 2.6 Unfair and deceptive practices R.S. 22:1964 lists unfair or deceptive acts. Examples: misrepresenting policy provisions, false advertising, boycott, coercion and intimidation, and unfair claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14). Claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14, examples): not responding promptly to communications; denying a claim without a reasonable investigation; not affirming or denying coverage within a reasonable time after proof of loss; not explaining a denial; not providing claim forms within 15 days; not giving the claim file on written request. R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024. The good-faith duty is now in R.S. 22:1892(I) : the insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing. 2.7 Federal tax items Standard federal tax points for life and annuities are summarized in Section 3.5 below."},{"id":"addendum-s3","title":"3. Additional topics","html":"<p>Each topic below has a pointer to the chapter or a short summary.</p><h4>3.1 Louisiana regulation and producer ethics</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Covered in Section 2 above. Producer ethics and fiduciary duty: see Chapter 1 (&quot;Ethical agent practices&quot;) and Chapter 3 (agency law).</li></ul><h4>3.2 Replacement rules and annuity best interest</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Covered in Section 2.5 above.</li></ul><h4>3.3 Affordable Care Act basics (not Louisiana-specific)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Issuers must accept every applicant (guaranteed issue, with limited exceptions) and cannot impose preexisting-condition exclusions. This is why preexisting-condition waiting periods are no longer used (Chapter 12).</li><li class=\"l0\">Premium tax credits depend on household income. Essential health benefits and metal tiers are in Chapter 12.</li></ul><h4>3.4 Group health participation and funding</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Participation rules, COBRA, and HIPAA are in Chapter 12. Self-funding and stop-loss are in Chapter 13.</li></ul><h4>3.5 Tax summary (standard federal rules)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life insurance death proceeds:</strong> generally income-tax-free to the beneficiary (exceptions: transfer for value; interest on installments is taxable).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Modified endowment contracts (MECs):</strong> withdrawals and loans taxed <strong>LIFO</strong> (gains first), plus a <strong>10% additional tax</strong> on the taxable part before age 59½.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Section 1035 exchanges:</strong> no gain or loss recognized when life insurance is exchanged for life insurance, an endowment, or an annuity. Annuity to life insurance is not covered.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuities:</strong> earnings taxed first on withdrawal (LIFO); exclusion ratio on annuitized payments.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Group term life:</strong> the cost of the first <strong>$50,000</strong> of coverage is not taxed to the employee (Chapter 6).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical expense deduction:</strong> only expenses above <strong>7.5%</strong> of adjusted gross income.</li></ul><h4>3.6 Claims handling</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana claim-settlement duties are in Section 2.6. Claim forms within 15 days and proof-of-loss timing are in Chapter 17.</li></ul><h4>3.7 Exam blueprint and exam-day</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Domain weights and format are in Section 1. Practice under timed conditions and review every miss.</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/101\">26 USC 101</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72\">26 USC 72</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A\">26 USC 7702A</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1035\">26 USC 1035</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/79\">26 USC 79</a>; <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc502\">IRS Topic 502</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-1\">42 USC 300gg-1</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/300gg-3\">42 USC 300gg-3</a>; <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/36B\">26 USC 36B</a></p>","text":"Each topic below has a pointer to the chapter or a short summary. 3.1 Louisiana regulation and producer ethics Covered in Section 2 above. Producer ethics and fiduciary duty: see Chapter 1 (\"Ethical agent practices\") and Chapter 3 (agency law). 3.2 Replacement rules and annuity best interest Covered in Section 2.5 above. 3.3 Affordable Care Act basics (not Louisiana-specific) Issuers must accept every applicant (guaranteed issue, with limited exceptions) and cannot impose preexisting-condition exclusions. This is why preexisting-condition waiting periods are no longer used (Chapter 12). Premium tax credits depend on household income. Essential health benefits and metal tiers are in Chapter 12. 3.4 Group health participation and funding Participation rules, COBRA, and HIPAA are in Chapter 12. Self-funding and stop-loss are in Chapter 13. 3.5 Tax summary (standard federal rules) Life insurance death proceeds: generally income-tax-free to the beneficiary (exceptions: transfer for value; interest on installments is taxable). Modified endowment contracts (MECs): withdrawals and loans taxed LIFO (gains first), plus a 10% additional tax on the taxable part before age 59½. Section 1035 exchanges: no gain or loss recognized when life insurance is exchanged for life insurance, an endowment, or an annuity. Annuity to life insurance is not covered. Annuities: earnings taxed first on withdrawal (LIFO); exclusion ratio on annuitized payments. Group term life: the cost of the first $50,000 of coverage is not taxed to the employee (Chapter 6). Medical expense deduction: only expenses above 7.5% of adjusted gross income. 3.6 Claims handling Louisiana claim-settlement duties are in Section 2.6. Claim forms within 15 days and proof-of-loss timing are in Chapter 17. 3.7 Exam blueprint and exam-day Domain weights and format are in Section 1. Practice under timed conditions and review every miss."},{"id":"addendum-s4","title":"Louisiana producer requirements and fraud (verified from statute)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term care training:</strong> A one-time training of at least 8 hours, then at least 4 hours every 2 years. Insurers must confirm completion (R.S. 22:1575).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Appointments:</strong> The insurer files a notice of appointment within 15 days of signing the agency contract (R.S. 22:1558).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Temporary licenses:</strong> A temporary producer license lasts up to 180 days and needs no exam (R.S. 22:1553).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Discipline:</strong> The commissioner may place on probation, suspend, revoke, or refuse a license, and may fine up to $500 per violation and $10,000 per year (R.S. 22:1554).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurance fraud:</strong> A felony carrying up to 5 years in prison or a $5,000 fine per count. If the benefit is $1,000 or less, up to 6 months or a $1,000 fine (R.S. 22:1924). Suspected fraud must be reported to the fraud office within 60 days (R.S. 22:1926). Good-faith reporters have civil immunity (R.S. 22:1928).</li></ul><p class=\"src\"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=727171\">R.S. 22:1575</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508532\">R.S. 22:1558</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508527\">R.S. 22:1553</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=508528\">R.S. 22:1554</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509062\">R.S. 22:1924</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509064\">R.S. 22:1926</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=509066\">R.S. 22:1928</a></p>","text":"Long-term care training: A one-time training of at least 8 hours, then at least 4 hours every 2 years. Insurers must confirm completion (R.S. 22:1575). Appointments: The insurer files a notice of appointment within 15 days of signing the agency contract (R.S. 22:1558). Temporary licenses: A temporary producer license lasts up to 180 days and needs no exam (R.S. 22:1553). Discipline: The commissioner may place on probation, suspend, revoke, or refuse a license, and may fine up to $500 per violation and $10,000 per year (R.S. 22:1554). Insurance fraud: A felony carrying up to 5 years in prison or a $5,000 fine per count. If the benefit is $1,000 or less, up to 6 months or a $1,000 fine (R.S. 22:1924). Suspected fraud must be reported to the fraud office within 60 days (R.S. 22:1926). Good-faith reporters have civil immunity (R.S. 22:1928)."}]}];
window.REFERENCE = [{"id":"ref-how-to-use-this-guide","title":"How to Use This Guide","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Read this first:</strong> Every number on this page links to an official source. Recite the table for 5 minutes a day: cover the right column and say the answer.</p></div>","text":"Read this first: Every number on this page links to an official source. Recite the table for 5 minutes a day: cover the right column and say the answer."},{"id":"ref-study-plan","title":"Your 25-Day Plan (Nov 27 to Dec 22, 2026)","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Goal:</strong> 70% or higher on the 150-question mock, ideally 75–80% twice in a row before exam day. Study in 15-minute blocks.</p></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dates</th><th>Focus</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Nov 27 – Nov 28</strong></td><td>Addendum (Louisiana law, 23% of the exam) + Reference table</td><td>Read, then quiz the Addendum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nov 29 – Dec 1</strong></td><td>Chapters 1 – 4</td><td>1–2 chapters a day: read, quiz, 15-minute session</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 2 – Dec 4</strong></td><td>Chapters 5.1, 5.2, 6, 7</td><td>Same routine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 5</strong></td><td>First 150-question mock</td><td>Shows your weak spots. The score does not matter yet</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 6 – Dec 12</strong></td><td>Chapters 8 – 18</td><td>Same routine; skip the Bonus chapter if short on time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 13 – Dec 19</strong></td><td>Mock every other day</td><td>Other days: drill “Where to focus” on the dashboard</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 20 – Dec 21</strong></td><td>Light review</td><td>Reference table, flashcards, one last mock</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dec 22</strong></td><td>Exam day</td><td>Rest the night before</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Goal: 70% or higher on the 150-question mock, ideally 75–80% twice in a row before exam day. Study in 15-minute blocks. Dates Focus How Nov 27 – Nov 28 Addendum (Louisiana law, 23% of the exam) + Reference table Read, then quiz the Addendum Nov 29 – Dec 1 Chapters 1 – 4 1–2 chapters a day: read, quiz, 15-minute session Dec 2 – Dec 4 Chapters 5.1, 5.2, 6, 7 Same routine Dec 5 First 150-question mock Shows your weak spots. The score does not matter yet Dec 6 – Dec 12 Chapters 8 – 18 Same routine; skip the Bonus chapter if short on time Dec 13 – Dec 19 Mock every other day Other days: drill “Where to focus” on the dashboard Dec 20 – Dec 21 Light review Reference table, flashcards, one last mock Dec 22 Exam day Rest the night before"},{"id":"ref-master-cheat-sheet-key-numbers-and-rules","title":"Key Numbers and Rules (sourced)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Exam (Series 103)</td><td>150 questions, 160 minutes, 70% to pass</td><td><a href=\"http://proctor2.psionline.com/programs/lains/Life%20Health%20and%20Acc%20103.pdf\">PSI Series 103 outline</a></td></tr><tr><td>Testing location</td><td>PSI test centers only. Remote testing ended July 8, 2024</td><td><a href=\"https://test-takers.psiexams.com/ladi\">PSI Louisiana page</a></td></tr><tr><td>Free look (individual life)</td><td>10 days from receipt of the policy</td><td><a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></td></tr><tr><td>Grace period (life)</td><td>30 days, or one month at the insurer's option</td><td><a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></td></tr><tr><td>Incontestability</td><td>Life: 2 years. Health and accident: 3 years</td><td><a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a>; <a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/LawPrint.aspx?d=507812\">La. R.S. 22:975</a></td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement (life)</td><td>Within 3 years from the date of default</td><td><a href=\"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787\">La. R.S. 22:931</a></td></tr><tr><td>Producer CE (renewal cycle)</td><td>24 hours, including 3 ethics. From July 2027 renewals, 2 of the 24 hours must be legislative updates</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/education/continuing-education-information/continuing-education-requirements\">LDI CE requirements</a></td></tr><tr><td>CE carryover</td><td>Up to 10 excess general hours</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/education/continuing-education-information/continuing-education-requirements\">LDI CE requirements</a></td></tr><tr><td>Annuity training</td><td>One-time 4 hours. Regulation 89 best-interest amendments effective September 20, 2024</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/education/annuity-training-requirements\">LDI annuity training</a></td></tr><tr><td>Long-term care training</td><td>8 hours initial, then 4 hours every 2 years</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/education/long-term-care-training-requirements-for-producers\">LDI LTC training</a></td></tr><tr><td>License renewal</td><td>Last day of the birth month. Even-numbered license numbers renew in even years. Producer renewal fee $75 (effective January 2026)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ldi.la.gov/industry/producer-adjuster/renewals\">LDI renewals</a></td></tr><tr><td>Social Security earnings base 2027</td><td>$192,300</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html\">SSA contribution and benefit base</a></td></tr><tr><td>SSDI disability definition</td><td>Unable to work for 12 consecutive months or more (or expected to end in death)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html\">SSA disability eligibility</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Rule Number or answer Source Exam (Series 103) 150 questions, 160 minutes, 70% to pass PSI Series 103 outline Testing location PSI test centers only. Remote testing ended July 8, 2024 PSI Louisiana page Free look (individual life) 10 days from receipt of the policy La. R.S. 22:931 Grace period (life) 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option La. R.S. 22:931 Incontestability Life: 2 years. Health and accident: 3 years La. R.S. 22:931 ; La. R.S. 22:975 Reinstatement (life) Within 3 years from the date of default La. R.S. 22:931 Producer CE (renewal cycle) 24 hours, including 3 ethics. From July 2027 renewals, 2 of the 24 hours must be legislative updates LDI CE requirements CE carryover Up to 10 excess general hours LDI CE requirements Annuity training One-time 4 hours. Regulation 89 best-interest amendments effective September 20, 2024 LDI annuity training Long-term care training 8 hours initial, then 4 hours every 2 years LDI LTC training License renewal Last day of the birth month. Even-numbered license numbers renew in even years. Producer renewal fee $75 (effective January 2026) LDI renewals Social Security earnings base 2027 $192,300 SSA contribution and benefit base SSDI disability definition Unable to work for 12 consecutive months or more (or expected to end in death) SSA disability eligibility"}];
window.RECALL = [{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"An insurer wants to give part of a large risk to another insurer. What is the first company called?","a":"The ceding company.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/ceding-company"},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Who does a public adjuster represent?","a":"The insured, not the insurer.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/public-adjuster"},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Ray's insurer tells him his claim is denied. Ray hires someone to fight the denial and the person is paid by Ray. Who is this person working for?","a":"Scenario B: Ray hired a public adjuster, who works for the insured.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/public-adjuster"},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Why can an insurer predict total losses for 10,000 policies but not for one person?","a":"Law of large numbers: as more independent exposure units are insured, actual losses come closer to expected losses.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/law-of-large-numbers"},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"A homeowner exaggerates a storm loss to get more money. Moral or morale?","a":"Moral hazard (a rise in expected losses caused by the insured's character or circumstances, such as inflating a claim).","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/moral-hazard"},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Which kind of risk can a casino gain from?","a":"Speculative risk (gain or loss).","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/speculative-risk"},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Scenario A: Jim has a history of heart disease and applies for life insurance with a plan to leave out his doctor visits. Which concept is the insurer most worried about? (Hint: think adverse selection and moral hazard.)","a":"Scenario A: Adverse selection (applicants who expect a loss buy insurance at higher rates) and moral hazard (the insured's character, here concealment).","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/adverse-selection"},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"What is the definition of subrogation?","a":"The insurer's right to recover what it paid from the third party who caused the loss.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/subrogation"},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Scenario B: An insurer's agent tells Tom that his pre-existing condition will be covered. Tom relies on this and cancels his other coverage. The insurer then denies the claim. What doctrine helps Tom?","a":"Scenario B: Estoppel. A party cannot contradict its earlier conduct when the other party reasonably relied on it.","src":"https://www.irmi.com/term/insurance-definitions/estoppel"},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start?","a":"10 days. It runs from the date the insured receives the policy (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What is the 7-pay test?","a":"A contract fails the 7-pay test if premiums paid in the first 7 contract years exceed the net level premiums that would have paid it up after 7 level annual premiums. A contract that fails is a modified endowment contract (MEC).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/7702A"},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"How long is the incontestable period, and what are its main exceptions?","a":"Two years in force during the insured's lifetime. Exceptions include nonpayment of premiums and certain military or naval service conditions; the insurer may also exclude disability and accidental-death benefits (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"What happens if the insured dies by suicide in month 18 of a Louisiana individual life policy?","a":"The policy may exclude death by suicide, sane or insane, within two years of issue (R.S. 22:931). Month 18 is inside that window, so the death benefit can be excluded.","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"How long is the conversion period after employment ends?","a":"31 days after employment or class membership ends, and no evidence of insurability is required (R.S. 22:942).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507805"},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Does conversion require proof of insurability?","a":"No. Conversion on termination of eligibility requires no evidence of insurability (R.S. 22:942).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507805"},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What is the largest tax-free amount of group term coverage per employee?","a":"$50,000. Cost of employer-provided coverage above $50,000 is included in income (IRC section 79).","src":"https://www.irs.gov/government-entities/federal-state-local-governments/group-term-life-insurance"},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What must be true about the eligible-employee group for the plan to get favorable tax treatment?","a":"The plan must benefit at least 70% of all employees, and at least 85% of participants must not be key employees (IRC section 79(d)).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/79"},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Dana leaves her job in March. She has group life coverage and wants a permanent policy. The coverage was group term. What is her deadline, and does she need a medical exam?","a":"Scenario A: Dana has 31 days from termination to convert, and no evidence of insurability (medical exam) is required.","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507805"},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan?","a":"Under IRC section 303, a corporation's redemption of stock from an estate to pay death taxes and funeral and administration expenses is treated as a sale of the stock rather than a dividend (stock must exceed 35% of the gross estate).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/303"},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)?","a":"Earnings (income on the contract) first, taxable; then investment in the contract, tax-free (IRC section 72(e)).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72"},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What does the 10% penalty apply to?","a":"The taxable (earnings) portion of amounts received before age 59 1/2, unless an exception applies (IRC section 72(q)).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72"},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What is the exclusion ratio formula?","a":"Investment in the contract divided by expected return (IRC section 72(b)).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/72"},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits?","a":"Age 60 (reduced benefit between 60 and full retirement age).","src":"https://www.ssa.gov/faqs/en/questions/KA-02083.html"},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the lump-sum death benefit?","a":"$255, a one-time payment; apply within 2 years of the death.","src":"https://www.ssa.gov/faqs/en/questions/KA-02083.html"},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the waiting period for SSDI?","a":"5 full calendar months. The first payment comes in the 6th full month after onset.","src":"https://www.ssa.gov/faqs/en/questions/KA-01777.html"},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Scenario C: Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?","a":"Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year.","src":"https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/rtea.html"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Which ERISA form is filed each year?","a":"Form 5500.","src":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/publications/reporting-and-disclosure-guide-for-employee-benefit-plans"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description?","a":"Within 90 days of enrollment.","src":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/publications/reporting-and-disclosure-guide-for-employee-benefit-plans"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½?","a":"10%, plus income tax on the taxable portion.","src":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-tax-on-early-distributions"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free?","a":"The 5-taxable-year period must have been met AND the distribution must be made on or after age 59 1/2, on death, on disability, or as a qualified special purpose (first-home) distribution (IRC section 408A(d)(2)).","src":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/408A"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover?","a":"A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it within 60 days to avoid tax.","src":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions"},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Scenario A: Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done?","a":"Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To keep the full amount tax-deferred, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding.","src":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled?","a":"18 months (29 if disabled).","src":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/publications/workers-guide-health-benefits-cobra"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA?","a":"At least 60 days, counted from the later of the election notice or the date coverage would be lost.","src":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/publications/workers-guide-health-benefits-cobra"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged?","a":"102% of the premium.","src":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Name the ten ACA essential health benefits.","a":"Ambulatory care, emergency, hospitalization, pregnancy and newborn, mental health and substance use, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative, laboratory, preventive and wellness, pediatric services.","src":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/what-marketplace-plans-cover/"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA?","a":"Until age 26.","src":"https://www.healthcare.gov/young-adults/children-under-26/"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution?","a":"55.","src":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost?","a":"Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies.","src":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/publications/workers-guide-health-benefits-cobra"},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is the Medicare waiting period for disabled people who receive SSDI?","a":"24 months of SSDI benefits.","src":"https://www.cms.gov/medicare/enrollment-renewal/original-part-a-b"},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Which condition gives Medicare eligibility without the 24-month SSDI wait?","a":"ALS. Medicare eligibility begins the first month of disability benefits, with no 24-month wait.","src":"https://www.cms.gov/training-education/find-provider-type/employers-unions/top-five-medicare-enrollment"},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"How many employees make a group plan primary for an age-based Medicare claim?","a":"20 or more employees: the group plan pays first for working-aged Medicare beneficiaries and Medicare is secondary. With fewer than 20, Medicare is primary.","src":"https://www.cms.gov/regulations-and-guidance/guidance/manuals/downloads/msp105c02.pdf"},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Scenario B: Greg is 67, works for a company with 15 employees, and is covered by the company's group plan. Which program pays first?","a":"Scenario B: Greg's employer has fewer than 20 employees, so Medicare is PRIMARY and the group plan is secondary.","src":"https://www.cms.gov/regulations-and-guidance/guidance/manuals/downloads/msp105c02.pdf"},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"How much does the insurer pay on a $2,100 expense with a $750 deductible and 70/30 coinsurance?","a":"$945 (70% of $1,350).","src":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/co-insurance/"},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Which account is employer-funded only?","a":"HRA.","src":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Ben has $5,000 of medical bills. His policy has a $1,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance. How much does Ben pay, and how much does the insurer pay?","a":"Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.)","src":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/co-insurance/"},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A worker wants a health account she owns, that rolls over, and that works with a high-deductible plan. Which account fits?","a":"Scenario C: An HSA (individual-owned, needs an HDHP, rollover allowed).","src":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the open enrollment period for Medigap, and when does it begin?","a":"Six months, beginning when the person first enrolls in Part B at age 65 or older.","src":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/ready-to-buy/when"},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"How many Medigap letters are there, and which is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020?","a":"Ten (A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N). Plans C and F are not available to people new to Medicare on or after January 1, 2020.","src":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/ready-to-buy/when"},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the Part D late enrollment penalty?","a":"1% of the national base beneficiary premium for each full month without creditable drug coverage after the Initial Enrollment Period (63 or more days).","src":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d/basics/costs"},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"Scenario A: Ellen turns 65 and enrolls in Medicare Part B. She applies for Medigap within her first 6 months. Can the insurer refuse her because of a heart condition?","a":"Scenario A: No. During the 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment Period the insurer cannot deny coverage because of pre-existing health problems.","src":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/ready-to-buy/when"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What grace period must a Louisiana individual life policy give for premiums after the first?","a":"30 days, or one month at the insurer's option, for any premium after the first (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How many days after a loss must notice of claim be given?","a":"Written notice within 20 days after the accident or start of disability (10 days for industrial policies). Late notice does not defeat the claim if timely notice was not reasonably possible (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Within what time must the insurer provide claim forms?","a":"If the insurer does not furnish claim forms within 15 days after notice, the claimant may submit written proof of loss instead (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long is the proof of loss deadline, and what extends it?","a":"90 days (for disability time-loss claims, 90 days after the period of liability ends). Late proof is excused if timely filing was not reasonably possible, but it is due no later than 1 year after it was otherwise required (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long must a claimant wait before suing after proof of loss?","a":"60 days after proof of loss is filed. Suit is barred after 1 year from when proof was required (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What is the free-look period for a Louisiana individual life policy?","a":"10 days from receipt of the policy, with return of premium (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long does a sickness coverage delay last after reinstatement?","a":"Accepting the overdue premium reinstates the policy. It covers accidental injury afterward and sickness that begins more than 10 days after acceptance (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Which optional provision reduces the benefit when the insured moves to a more hazardous job?","a":"The change of occupation provision: after a move to a more hazardous occupation, the insurer pays only what the premium would have bought at that occupation's rate (R.S. 22:975).","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What is the ACA dependent coverage age?","a":"Age 26.","src":"https://www.healthcare.gov/young-adults/children-under-26/"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Scenario A: Ben's quarterly premium is due March 1. He does not pay. When does the grace period end, and what happens if he dies on March 20?","a":"Scenario A: Louisiana requires a grace period of 30 days or one month at the insurer's option, so it runs to about April 1. Death on March 20 falls within the grace period, so the death benefit is paid less the unpaid premium (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Scenario C: The insurer never responds to Carl's reinstatement application. How does reinstatement work, and when does his sickness coverage start?","a":"Scenario C: Under R.S. 22:975, accepting the overdue premium reinstates the policy. Sickness coverage begins for sickness starting more than 10 days after acceptance; accidents are covered immediately.","src":"https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=507812"},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is the standard free-look period, and what starts it?","a":"10 days, counted from the date the insured receives the policy (R.S. 22:931).","src":"https://legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=507787"}];
